dy', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9614a25c-b38a-5fd5-9dcc-28a991c45ee8', 'word_count': 933, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9614a25c-b38a-5fd5-9dcc-28a991c45ee8'}, {'abstract': 'Television highlights.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/09/01/arts/television/whats-on-tv-monday.html', 'snippet': 'Television highlights.', 'lead_paragraph': '9 P.M. (HBO Signature) TRANCE (2013) Danny Boyle’s thriller begins in an auction house, where the heist of a Goya leaves Simon (James McAvoy), an employee charged with safeguarding the work, with amnesia and unable to reveal where the painting is. Vincent Cassel plays Franck, the leader of the men behind the theft — and, it turns out, Simon’s business partner. And Rosario Dawson, above, is the hypnotherapist who holds the key to Simon’s memory. “Mr. Boyle, as always, works hard to cast a spell in ‘Trance,’ and his energy and commitment to the audience’s entertainment are evident in every scene,” Manohla Dargis wrote in The New York Times about this film from the director of “Trainspotting” and “Slumdog Millionaire.” “But there are times when it feels as if he’s throwing everything at the screen — the throbbing music, bleeding fingers, narrative U-turns and the startling sight of a naked Ms. Dawson striding toward the camera as strategically shorn as a Renaissance nude — less because he wants to distract you from the big reveal than to obscure the material’s thinness.” She added, “That doesn’t lessen the movie’s appeal while you’re watching it, though it drastically shortens the afterglow.”', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What’s On TV Monday', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'What’s On TV Monday', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2014-09-01T04:18:02+0000', 'docume