ization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Erik', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Braund', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Video', '_id': 'nyt://video/b648eb36-ebc2-5f0c-83f5-a245cf276bf7', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://video/b648eb36-ebc2-5f0c-83f5-a245cf276bf7'}, {'abstract': 'False consciousness among the 1 percent?', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/09/01/inflation-septaphobia-and-the-shock-doctrine/', 'snippet': 'False consciousness among the 1 percent?', 'lead_paragraph': 'The bad news from Europe is a reminder that the basic insight some of us have been trying to convey, mostly in vain, ever since 2008 remains valid: the great danger facing advanced economies is that governments and central banks will do too little, not too much. The risk of elevated inflation or fiscal difficulties is dwarfed by the risk of ending up trapped in a deflationary vortex. This view has been overwhelmingly supported by recent experience — if you acted on what they were saying on CNBC or the WSJ editorial page, you would have lost a lot of money. Yet the power of the hard money/fiscal austerity orthodoxy (yes, market monetarists want one without the other, but they have no constituency) remains immense. Why?', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Inflation, Septaphobia, and the Shock Doctrine', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2014-09-01T18:23:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7ea1a333-286d-5c32-aa7b-25a408c9bbaf', 'word_count': 402, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7ea1a333-286d-5c32-aa7b-25a408c9bbaf'}, {'abstract': 'Mr. Carcelle, who led the Louis Vuitton brand and later ran LVMH’s fashion division, was the main architect of an expansion into Asia and other international markets.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/09/02/business/international/yves-carcelle-executive-who-made-louis-vuitton-a-status-symbol-dies-at-66.html', 'snippet': 'Mr. Carcelle, who led the Louis Vuitton brand and later ran LVMH’s fashion division, was the main architect of an expansion into Asia and other international markets.', 'lead_paragraph': 'PARIS — Yves Carcelle, the charismatic executive who transformed Louis Vuitton from a staid French maker of handbags and travel trunks into one of the world’s most recognizable luxury brands, died on Sunday in Paris. He was 66.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '21', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/02/world/CARCELLE-obit/CARCELLE-obit-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/09/02/world/CARCELLE-obit/CARCELLE-obit-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/02/world/CARCELLE-obit/CARCELLE-obit-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/02/world/CARCELLE-obit/CARCELLE-obit-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/02/world/CARCELLE-obit/CARCELLE-obit-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'he