 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2014/09/02/morning-agenda-evans-bank-under-fire/', 'snippet': 'New York set to accuse Evans Bank of redlining. | Dollar General raises bid for Family Dollar. | Public pension funds staying mum on inversions. | Banks pursuing military customers.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Eric T. Schneiderman, New York’s attorney general, is said to be conducting a broad investigation into whether banks are deliberately choking off mortgage lending to predominantly minority communities, known as “redlining,” DealBook’s Jessica Silver-Greenberg writes. The investigation was expected to reach its first target as early as Tuesday, people briefed on the matter said, with Mr. Schneiderman’s office taking aim at Evans Bank, a regional lender whose business in the Buffalo area dates to 1920, accusing it of denying mortgages to African-Americans regardless of their credit.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Morning Agenda: Evans Bank Under Fire', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2014-09-02T11:57:53+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sydney Ember', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sydney', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ember', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/80847d60-9740-5b99-a1b0-dc021b4c00ba', 'word_count': 1933, 'uri': 'nyt://article/80847d60-9740-5b99-a1b0-dc021b4c00ba'}, {'abstract': 'A careful reader alerts us to a cliché in the making: “on the planet.”', 'web_url': 'https://afterdeadline.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/09/02/phrases-we-love-too-much-5/', 'snippet': 'A careful reader alerts us to a cliché in the making: “on the planet.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'A careful reader alerts us to a cliché in the making: “on the planet.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Phrases We Love Too Much', 'kicker': 'After Deadline', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2014-09-02T12:00:33+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Times Topics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Philip B. Corbett', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Philip', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e87f56c0-050e-5480-9f9f-acfd35e29f4a', 'word_count': 1292, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e87f56c0-050e-5480-9f9f-acfd35e29f4a'}, {'abstract': 'The 20-year-old pianist and composer plays the final movement of Prokofiev’s Piano Sonata No. 7.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/09/02/in-performance-conrad-tao/', 'snippet': 'The 20-year-old pianist and composer plays the final movement of Prokofiev’s Piano Sonata No. 7.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Conrad Tao, a pianist and a composer, has been on a roll. Last year he produced a new music festival in New York, released his first major-label album, and wrote a piece for the Dallas Symphony Orchestra for the 50th anniversary of the assassination of John F. Kennedy. This year he will be the artist-in-residence in Dallas, play concerts around the country and the world, and open the New York Youth Symphony’s season Nov. 23 at Carnegie Hall. And he’s only 20.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'In Performance: Conrad Tao', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Classical Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Prokofiev, Sergei', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Tao, Conrad', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-09-02T12:00:46+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael Cooper', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cooper', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/fb0aa080-33fe-5dd2-9c63-0a3ac2715de9', 'word_count': 154, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fb0aa080-33fe-5dd2-9c63-0a3ac2715de9'}, {'abstract': 'Peter Jackson’s Tolkien trilogy will be shown at the Koch Theater with live accompaniment by a 250-member orchestra and chorus.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/09/02/lord-of-the-rings-trilogy-with-live-orchestra-coming-to-lincoln-center/', 'snippet': 'Peter Jackson’s Tolkien trilogy will be shown at the Koch Theater with live accompaniment by a 250-member orchestra and chorus.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Until now, when someone mentioned going to see the “Ring” cycle at Lincoln Center, you knew what that meant: four nights of Wagner at the Metropolitan Opera, where a complicated tale of Norse gods and various mortal beings vying for control of the gold from the Rhine unfolds cataclysmically – lately, on an expensive and rather scary piece of stage machinery.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/30/arts/29artsbeat-lotr/29artsbeat-lotr-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/08/30/arts/29artsbeat-lotr/29artsbeat-lotr-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/30/arts/29artsbeat-lotr/29artsbeat-lotr-