ine': {'original': 'By Edward Wong', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Edward', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Wong', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/412706cf-930c-5080-9714-92cdca29f806', 'word_count': 870, 'uri': 'nyt://article/412706cf-930c-5080-9714-92cdca29f806'}, {'abstract': 'Direct versus indirect effects, and the question of false consciousness.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/09/02/class-interests-and-monetary-policy-take-ii/', 'snippet': 'Direct versus indirect effects, and the question of false consciousness.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Steve Randy Waldman has a long, thoughtful take on my speculations about the hard-money preferences of the wealthy. Basically I confessed myself somewhat confused: I get why creditors should hate inflation, but aggressive monetary responses to the Lesser Depression have been good for asset prices, and hence for the wealthy. Why, then, the vociferous protests?', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Class Interests and Monetary Policy, Take II', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2014-09-02T13:53:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/350d7d1b-963d-5a12-9722-0e64465c3e52', 'word_count': 475, 'uri': 'nyt://article/350d7d1b-963d-5a12-9722-0e64465c3e52'}, {'abstract': 'The British government is trying to grapple with the airport question yet again amid ballooning budget deficits and an economy in a double-dip recession.', 'web_url': 'h