 'Halliburton, the company contracted by BP to cement the ill-fated Macondo oil well in the Gulf of Mexico, has reached a $1.1 billion settlement with thousands of businesses, individuals and local governments that suffered losses from the 2010 Deepwater Horizon oil rig explosion, the company and plaintiffs announced on Tuesday.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/03/business/Halliburton/Halliburton-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/09/03/business/Halliburton/Halliburton-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/03/business/Halliburton/Halliburton-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/03/business/Halliburton/Halliburton-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1367, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/03/business/Halliburton/Halliburton-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/09/03/business/Halliburton/Halliburton-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/03/business/Halliburton/Halliburton-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Halliburton to Pay $1.1 Billion to Settle Damages in Gulf of Mexico Oil Spill', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Halliburton to Pay $1.1 Billion to Settle Damages in Gulf Oil Spill', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Gulf of Mexico Oil Spill (2010)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Krauss, Clifford', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Halliburton Company', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Suits and Litigation (Civil)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fines (Penalties)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-09-03T00:31:58+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Energy & Environment ', 'byline': {'original': 'By Clifford Krauss', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Clifford', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krauss', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6163a237-3e17-5f39-87eb-0e3013c701ef', 'word_count': 973, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6163a237-3e17-5f39-87eb-0e3013c701ef'}, {'abstract': 'Submit your questions about the start of the N.F.L. season, and Times reporters will respond beginning on Thursday.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/09/03/sports/football/nfl-season-is-about-to-begin-any-questions.html', 'snippet': 'Submit your questions about the start of the N.F.L. season, and Times reporters will respond beginning on Thursday.', 'lead_paragraph': "The N.F.L.'s regular season begins Thursday night, when the Seattle Seahawks, the defending Super Bowl champions, host the Green Bay Packers. On Sunday, Rex Ryan’s Jets will play host to the Oakland Raiders, and on Monday night, Eli Manning and the Giants will take on the Lions in Detroit.", 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'N.F.L. Season Is About to Begin. Any Questions?', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Football', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Football League', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-09-03T00:32:33+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Pro Football', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e042251b-bc9d-5deb-934e-6f586e838be0', 'word_count': 112, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e042251b-bc9d-5deb-934e-6f586e838be0'}, {'abstract': 'Just what are we really dealing with in ISIS?', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/09/03/opinion/thomas-friedman-what-are-we-really-dealing-with-in-isis.html', 'snippet': 'Just what are we really dealing with in ISIS?', 'lead_paragraph': 'President Obama has been excoriated for declaring that “we don’t have a strategy yet” for effectively confronting the Islamic State in Iraq and Syria, or ISIS. In criticizing Obama for taking too much time, Representative Mike Rogers, the Republican chairman of the House Intelligence Committee, told “Fox News Sunday” that “this ‘don’t-do-stupid-stuff’ policy isn’t working.” That sounded odd to my ear — like we should just bomb somebody, even if it is stupid. If Obama did that, what would he be ignoring? ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '23', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/11/01/opinion/friedman-circular/friedman-circular-jumbo-v2.png', 'height': 228, 'width': 228, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/11/01/opinion/friedman-circular/friedman-circular-superJum