 traveled with a photographer and a fixer to Amerli, the only town to have successfully defended itself against the Islamic State in Iraq and Syria, to interview villagers.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/03/blogs/amerli/amerli-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/09/03/blogs/amerli/amerli-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/03/blogs/amerli/amerli-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/03/blogs/amerli/amerli-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1367, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/03/blogs/amerli/amerli-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/09/03/blogs/amerli/amerli-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/03/blogs/amerli/amerli-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Road to Amerli', 'kicker': 'Times Insider', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Kurds', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ahmed, Azam', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Iraqi Army', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Islamic State in Iraq and Syria (ISIS)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Pesh Merga', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Amerli (Iraq)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-09-03T17:47:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Blogs', 'byline': {'original': 'By Azam Ahmed', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Azam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ahmed', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/643f439e-3567-5faf-a921-c2e81bd72b14', 'word_count': 731, 'uri': 'nyt://article/643f439e-3567-5faf-a921-c2e81bd72b14'}, {'abstract': 'The publisher of “The Day the Crayons Quit,” a best seller about a group of disgruntled crayons who go on strike, is inviting children to take sides.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/09/03/inviting-young-readers-to-take-sides-in-a-crayon-labor-dispute/', 'snippet': 'The publisher of “The Day the Crayons Quit,” a best seller about a group of disgruntled crayons who go on strike, is inviting children to take sides.', 'lead_paragraph': '“The Day the Crayons Quit,” a cheeky illustrated book about a group of disgruntled crayons who go on strike, is the kind of surprise best seller that no one sees coming. The book, which has more than a million copies in print, recently went into its 10th printing. It’s been on The New York Times best-seller list for more than a year. This past July, it was optioned for film by Universal Pictures.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/03/arts/03artsbeat-crayons/03artsbeat-crayons-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 599, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/201