l', 'section_name': 'Travel', 'byline': {'original': 'By Rachel Lee Harris', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Rachel', 'middlename': 'Lee', 'lastname': 'Harris', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1bc56263-3e8d-5432-b2f1-3a56cf43178f', 'word_count': 317, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1bc56263-3e8d-5432-b2f1-3a56cf43178f'}, {'abstract': 'The men’s wear shows start off the week with little of the hoopla accompanying the women’s wear shows. Some lines are now ganging together on the opening day of #NYFW to stage presentations at Industria Superstudio.', 'web_url': 'https://runway.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/09/03/advocating-for-the-regular-guy-during-fashion-week/', 'snippet': 'The men’s wear shows start off the week with little of the hoopla accompanying the women’s wear shows. Some lines are now ganging together on the opening day of #NYFW to stage presentations at Industria Superstudio.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Everyone knows the men’s wear element of New York Fashion Week exists in a kind of calendric no-man’s land. For lots of reasons — market segment, scale of economic values, cultural disposition — women’s wear is the big tent event at the circus. Though the men’s wear shows start off the week, they do so mostly at out of the way locations with little of the hoopla that accompanies the women’s wear shows at Lincoln Center, and for an audience of news media and buyers that seems like a traveling fraternity.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/03/fashion/davidhart1/davidhart1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 497, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/09/03/fashion/davidhart1/davidhart1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 497}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None