2014-09-03T21:32:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Home', 'section_name': 'Home & Garden', 'byline': {'original': 'By Arlene Hirst', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Arlene', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hirst', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/932227f8-815d-5875-b66a-e10bba4e8b13', 'word_count': 131, 'uri': 'nyt://article/932227f8-815d-5875-b66a-e10bba4e8b13'}, {'abstract': 'A woodworker spent years polishing his latest pieces.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/09/04/garden/is-it-done-yet.html', 'snippet': 'A woodworker spent years polishing his latest pieces.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Michael Robbins, a 31-year-old self-taught woodworker in Columbia County, N.Y., spent three years perfecting his latest introduction, the Taza lounge chair. The piece went through several experimental versions, including one with a seat of canvas webbing, but in the end, a leather sling won out. Mr. Robbins also recently rolled out his Bridle bench, right, which, like the chair, had been “sitting around the shop for a while,” he said. The solid ash top is laminated with bridle leather, and an arc of brass adds a nice architectural detail while supporting the structure. The Taza lounge is available in four woods and four shades of leather, for $2,850; the Bridle bench ranges in length from 5 to 10 feet, in a choice of four woods and four leathers; it starts at $1,675. Information: 315-761-8010 or mchlrbbns.com.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/04/dining/04CURR_FURNITURECOMBO/04CURR_FURNITURECOMBO-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 265, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/09/04/dining/04CURR_FURNITURECOMBO/04CURR_FURNITURECOMBO