ker': 'Sinosphere', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Deaths (Fatalities)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'September 11 (2001)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cai Guo-Qiang', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Power Station of Art', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'China', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Shanghai (China)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'United States', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-09-03T22:00:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Becky Davis', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Becky', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Davis', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1d215da8-3061-5912-a104-113961c967b8', 'word_count': 1676, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1d215da8-3061-5912-a104-113961c967b8'}, {'abstract': 'Celebrating the 20th anniversary of apartheid’s end in South Africa with music at Carnegie Hall and elsewhere.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/09/07/arts/music/fall-arts-preview-ladysmith-black-mambazo-abdullah-ibrahim-and-others-commemorate-the-end-of-south-african-apartheid.html', 'snippet': 'Celebrating the 20th anniversary of apartheid’s end in South Africa with music at Carnegie Hall and elsewhere.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Carnegie Hall is marking 20 years after the end of apartheid with a monthlong festival of music from South Africa, where the long history of tribal, urban, colonial and imported traditions continues to create unexpected hybrids. There are familiar names, like the high-stepping choir Ladysmith Black Mambazo (performing on Oct. 18 with additional guests) and the trumpeter Hugh Masekela, who is sharing a bill with the courageous and nimble-voiced singer and songwriter Vusi Mahlasela on Oct. 10, both at Carnegie Hall. Abdullah Ibrahim, the pianist from Cape Town, brings his rolling, sonorous vamps to Zankel Hall on Oct. 17. ', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '112', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/07/arts/07PARELES1/07PARELES1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 281, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/09/07/arts/07PARELES1/07PARELES1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 281}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/07/arts/07PARELES1/07PARELES1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 480, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/07/arts/07PARELES1/07PARELES1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 959, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/07/arts/07PARELES1/07PARELES1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/09/07/arts/07PARELES1/07PARELES1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/07/arts/07PARELES1/07PARELES1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Music From the Beloved Country', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Music From the Beloved Country', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'South Africa', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Apartheid (Policy)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Carnegie Hall', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Ladysmith Black Mambazo', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ibrahim, Abdullah', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kramer, David', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Masekela, Hugh', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-09-03T22:01:34+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jon Pareles', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jon', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Pareles', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1ea5f27e-274a-5083-ad20-52003865fac7', 'word_count': 241, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1ea5f27e-274a-5083-ad20-52003865fac7'}, {'abstract': '“Nonesuch Records at BAM” celebrates the 50th-anniversary of this prestige record label at the Brooklyn Academy of Music.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/09/07/arts/music/fall-arts-preview-steve-reich-philip-glass-and-others-celebrate-nonesuch-records-at-bam.html', 'snippet': '“Nonesuch Records at BAM” celebrates the 50th-anniversary of this prestige record label at the Brooklyn Academy of Music.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The emblematic concert program in Nonesuch Records at BAM — a 50th-anniversary celebration for a prestige record label, running Tuesday through Sept. 28 at the Brooklyn Academy of Music — will feature two founding fathers of Minimalism, performing landmark works. Or perhaps a magisterial yet mischievous singer-songwriter from Brazil. Or a bril