'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fashion and Apparel', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-09-03T22:36:47+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Styles', 'section_name': 'Fashion & Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nick Bilton', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nick', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bilton', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7d901db4-3f91-55b6-adc3-da7f8e7409ea', 'word_count': 958, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7d901db4-3f91-55b6-adc3-da7f8e7409ea'}, {'abstract': 'What happened to the G.O.P.’s vision of broader opportunity?', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/09/04/opinion/bring-back-the-party-of-lincoln.html', 'snippet': 'What happened to the G.O.P.’s vision of broader opportunity?', 'lead_paragraph': 'CHESTNUT HILL, Mass. — FOR all the differences between establishment Republicans and Tea Party insurgents, their various efforts to rebrand the Grand Old Party tend to start from a common premise: the belief that Ronald Reagan was the quintessential Republican, and that his principle of defending wealth and the wealthy should remain the party’s guiding vision.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '27', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Bring Back the Party of Lincoln', 'kicker': 'Op-Ed Contributor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Bring Back the Party of Lincoln', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Republican Party', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lincoln, Abraham', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Income Inequality', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Roosevelt, Theodore', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Eisenhower, Dwight David', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Reagan, Ronald Wilson', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'United States', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Income Tax', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Economy', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-09-03T22:39:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Heather Cox Richardson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Heather', 'middlename': 'Cox', 'lastname': 'Richardson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/49b7555e-5b5d-5021-8d62-44cfc07a36b2', 'word_count': 860, 'uri': 'nyt://article/49b7555e-5b5d-5021-8d62-44cfc07a36b2'}, {'abstract': 'I choose to pack lunches for my children — but I also choose to make it easier to do.', 'web_url': 'https://parenting.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/09/03/nine-things-to-hate-about-packing-school-lunches-and-how-to-fix-them/', 'snippet': 'I choose to pack lunches for my children — but I also choose to make it easier to do.', 'lead_paragraph': 'There are far worse things than packing school lunches. In fact, I kind of like it — at no other time of day do I feed a captive, hungry audience with no other options who also can’t ask me, ever so politely, if they can have something else. If I didn’t want to do it, I’d have my children do it; they’re in eighth, fifth and third grade and they’re capable. Instead, I choose to load the boxes — and once I identified, and largely eliminated, the things about the process that I hated, our mornings (and my evenings) improved. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/03/us/motherlode-lunch/motherlode-lunch-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 450, 'width': 600, 'subType