 grow up on big and little screens, and sometimes the thinking about girls and girlhood grows, too. Inspired by Richard Linklater’s “Boyhood” — a magnificent film that tells the story of a boy’s life from 6 to 18 — we are taking a look at how girls are growing up in the movies. American mainstream cinema, a timid enterprise dependent on formulas and genres, can be mind-blowingly retrograde when it comes to women and girls. And while an occasional woman or girl rules the box office, too many of their on-screen sisters are sidelined or just left out of the picture.', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '49', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/07/arts/07FILMCOVER/07FILMCOVER-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 677, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/09/07/arts/07FILMCOVER/07FILMCOVER-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 677}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/07/arts/07FILMCOVER/07FILMCOVER-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 908, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/07/arts/07FILMCOVER/07FILMCOVER-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1815, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/07/arts/07FILMCOVER/07FILMCOVER-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/09/07/arts/07FILMCOVER/07FILMCOVER-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/07/arts/07FILMCOVER/07FILMCOVER-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Sugar, Spice and Guts', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Sugar, Spice and Guts', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Women and Girls', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-09-03T23:27:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By A.O. Scott and Manohla Dargis', 'person': [{'firstname': 'A.', 'middlename': 'O.', 'lastname': 'Scott', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Manohla', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Dargis', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/879f6ca5-899a-5729-9b04-8bbaa9fea029', 'word_count': 2640, 'uri': 'nyt://article/879f6ca5-899a-5729-9b04-8bbaa9fea029'}, {'abstract': 'ABC’s “Manhattan Love Story” and NBC’s “A to Z” are taking the same unusual approach to romantic comedies.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/09/07/arts/television/manhattan-love-story-and-a-to-z-new-network-sitcoms.html', 'snippet': 'ABC’s “Manhattan Love Story” and NBC’s “A to Z” are taking the same unusual approach to romantic comedies.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Are you one of the people mourning the loss of the old-fashioned (as in 1990s) rom-com? Do you feel that romantic comedies haven’t been the same — or haven’t even existed — since the last time Meg Ryan and Tom Hanks made a movie together?', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '103', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/07/arts