zation': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e97665a8-f7a6-5626-87a3-fcd978689b3a', 'word_count': 940, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e97665a8-f7a6-5626-87a3-fcd978689b3a'}, {'abstract': 'Protest leaders in Islamabad said Wednesday that their supporters would vacate the Parliament area, where they converged last week, after violent clashes with the police.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/09/04/world/asia/pakistan-protesters-told-to-pull-back.html', 'snippet': 'Protest leaders in Islamabad said Wednesday that their supporters would vacate the Parliament area, where they converged last week, after violent clashes with the police.', 'lead_paragraph': ' Protest leaders in Islamabad said\xa0Wednesday\xa0that their supporters would vacate the Parliament area, where they converged last week, after violent clashes with the police. Muhammad Tahir-ul Qadri, a firebrand cleric, made the announcement but told supporters, “We will not leave Islamabad till the resignation of Nawaz Sharif,” the prime minister. Pakistan Tehreek-e-Insaaf, the political party of the opposition leader Imran Khan, has also ordered its workers to leave the area. Mr. Qadri’s and Mr. Khan’s parties are now negotiating with the leaders of other parties to break a deadlock that has paralyzed the city. Formal talks between representatives of the government and protesters are expected Thursday. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Pakistan: Protesters Told to Pull Back', 'kicker': 'World Briefing', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Pakistan: Protesters Told to Pull Back', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Demonstrations, Protests and Riots', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Islamabad (Pakistan)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Qadri, Muhammad Tahir-ul', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sharif, Nawaz', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Pakistan', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-09-04T01:48:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Salman Masood', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Salman', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Masood', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/dccd761e-ca57-57cb-b4f5-e732fa0f6b1b', 'word_count': 105, 'uri': 'nyt://article/dccd761e-ca57-57cb-b4f5-e732fa0f6b1b'}, {'abstract': 'Samuel “Not The Newscaster” Donaldson goes crazy, but in a good way.', 'web_url': 'https://wordplay.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/09/03/go-crazy/', 'snippet': 'Samuel “Not The Newscaster” Donaldson goes crazy, but in a good way.', 'lead_paragraph': 'THURSDAY’S PUZZLE — The entry IN PEN for the clue “Improper way to take the SAT” made me laugh, not because using pen is such an egregious mistake, but because I have seen how students look at the SAT as a life experience and, from what I saw, using a pen was the least of their worries.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Go Crazy', 'kicker': 'Wordplay', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Crossword Puzzles', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hats and Caps', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'SAT (College Admission Test)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-09-04T02:00:41+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Crosswords & Games', 'byline': {'original': 'By Deb Amlen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Deb', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Amlen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organi