ead-back-to-school.html', 'snippet': 'More than 50,000 children were the first students in the city’s expanded program, and many were delighted to take part.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the classrooms at Pinocchio Children’s Palace in Brooklyn on Thursday, there was a discussion about sharing the toy cash register, there was talk about the importance of saying “I’m sorry,” there was dancing. And there were some tears.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '51,000 Answer de Blasio’s Bell for New Pre-K', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '51,000 Answer De Blasio’s Bell for New Pre-K', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Education (Pre-School)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'de Blasio, Bill', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Education (K-12)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Education Department (NYC)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Farina, Carmen', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-09-04T14:10:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Elizabeth A. Harris and Kate Taylor', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Elizabeth', 'middlename': 'A.', 'lastname': 'Harris', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Kate', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Taylor', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c33ddfbe-1243-59c3-9714-d29c3fa79143', 'word_count': 1179, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c33ddfbe-1243-59c3-9714-d29c3fa79143'}, {'abstract': 'Results of surveys may be helpful in evaluating early results from the new Affordable Care Act plans.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/09/05/upshot/how-people-feel-about-their-employer-sponsored-health-plans.html', 'snippet': 'Results of surveys may be helpful in evaluating early results from the new Affordable Care Act plans.', 'lead_paragraph': 'We’re still waiting to get really good information about how people who have bought health plans on the new state marketplaces are liking them. There’s some early polling evidence that people are happy to have coverage — but also some accounts that people are frustrated by small doctor networks and struggling to afford medical care even after getting insurance.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'How People Feel About Their Employer-Sponsored Health Plans', 'kicker': 'Public Health', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Health Insurance and Managed Care', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Patient Protection and Affordable Care Act (2010)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-09-04T14:15:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Upshot', 'section_name': 'The Upshot', 'byline': {'original': 'By Margot Sanger-Katz', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Margot', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sanger-Katz', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5e925446-9070-5788-a8c9-4c8c0254adc7', 'word_count': 589, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5e925446-9070-5788-a8c9-4c8c0254adc7'}, {'abstract': 'To learn how to study, start by bombing a pretest.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/09/07/magazine/why-flunking-exams-is-actually-a-good-thing.html', 'snippet': 'To learn how to study, start by bombing a pretest.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Imagine that on Day 1 of a difficult course, before you studied a single thing, you got hold of the final exam. The motherlode itself, full text, right there in your email inbox — attached mistakenly by the teacher, perhaps, or poached by a campus hacker. No answer key, no notes or guidelines. Just the questions.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '38', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/07/magazine/07learn5/07learn5-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 733, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/09/07/magazine/07learn5/07learn5-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 733}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/07/magazine/07learn5/07learn5-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 838, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/07/magazine/07learn5/07learn5-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1676, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/07/magazine/07learn5/07learn5-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/09/07/magazine/07learn5/07learn5-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/07/magazine/07learn5/07learn5-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Why Flunking Exams Is Actually a Good Thing', 'kicker': 'The Education Issue', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Test Drive', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tests and Examinations', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Carey, Benedict', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Education (K-12)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Colleges and Universities', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Memory', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Psychology and Psychologists', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'How We Learn (Book)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-09-04T14:16:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Benedict Carey', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Benedict', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Carey', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0d6d3e82-f0e8-5fb8-945f-72de22b9a664', 'word_count': 2838, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0d6d3e82-f0e8-5fb8-945f-72de22b9a664'}, {'abstract': 'Jennifer Aniston, Jennifer Connelly and Reese Witherspoon are turning in low-glitter performances in films playing the festival.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/09/04/toronto-festival-2014-glamorous-actresses-playing-tarnished-characters/', 'snippet': 'Jennifer Aniston, Jennifer Connelly and Reese Witherspoon are turning in low-glitter performances in films playing the festival.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Updated, 5:15 p.m. | TORONTO — It takes a brave actress to rough herself up and appear on-screen without all the tricks and trade-craft that make beautiful people even more so in the movies.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Toronto Festival 2014: Glamorous Actresses Playing Tarnished Characters', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Toronto International Film Festival', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'ANISTON, JENNIFER', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Connelly, Jennifer', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Witherspoon, Reese', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-09-04T14:25:56+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael Cieply', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cieply', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e98290e4-a873-5537-8d98-9c08558c5ebb', 'word_count': 491, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e98290e4-a873-5537-8d98-9c08558c5ebb'}, {'abstract': 'On a journey that starts in Cologne and heads south, finding echoes of a sometimes distant past.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/09/07/travel/tracing-jewish-history-along-the-rhine.html', 'snippet': 'On a journey that starts in Cologne and heads south, finding echoes of a sometimes distant past.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the summer of 1963, when I was 12, my parents, German-speaking Jewish refugees from Vienna, took us children on a boat trip up the Rhine. It was our first time in Germany, the country that had annexed our parents’ native Austria and sent them running for their lives. Family sentiments ran strong: longing spiked with anger, nostalgia with regret. All I really cared about were the castles crowning every slope and the chocolate-covered marzipan. But when we rounded a boulder-strewn bend in the river — “The Lorelei,” the legendary perch of the siren who lured boatmen to their doom — my mother was so moved she leapt up and declaimed, in German: “I don’t know what’s come over me, / That I should be so sad.”', 'print_section': 'TR', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/07/travel/07CULTUREDRHINE5/07CULTUREDRHINE5-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/09/07/travel/07CULTUREDRHINE5/07CULTUREDRHINE5-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/07/travel/07CULTUREDRHINE5/07CULTUREDRHINE5-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/07/travel/07CULTUREDRHINE5/07CULTUREDRHINE5-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/07/travel/07CULTUREDRHINE5/07CULTUREDRHINE5-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/09/07/travel/07CULTUREDRHINE5/07CULTUREDRHINE5-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/07/travel/07CULTUREDRHINE5/07CULTUREDRHINE5-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Tracing Jewish History Along the Rhine', 'kicker': 'Cultured Traveler', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Uncovering a Lost World', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Travel and Vacations', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Cologne (Germany)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Rhine River', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Jews and Judaism', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Synagogues', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Germany', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Mainz (Germany)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-09-04T14:26:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Travel', 'section_name': 'Travel', 'byline': {'original': 'By Peter Wortsman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Peter', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Wortsman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c1365f08-80af-5a6d-9405-75fd48e4d5fe', 'word_count': 1774, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c1365f08-80af-5a6d-9405-75fd48e4d5fe'}, {'abstract': 'Empathy isn’t just taking another perspective. Con men can do that. In order to be empathetic, children need to know how to value, respect and understand another person’s points of view, even when they don’t agree.', 'web_url': 'https://parenting.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/09/04/teaching-children-empathy/', 'snippet': 'Empathy isn’t just taking another perspective. Con men can do that. In order to be empathetic, children need to know how to value, respect and understand another person’s points of view, even when they don’t agree.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When Harvard University’s Making Caring Common Project released their report, “The Children We Mean to Raise: The Real Messages Adults Are Sending About Values,” many parents and educators — myself included — were surprised to learn that despite all our talk about instilling character and empathy, kids may value academic achievement and individual happiness over caring for others. In the report, the authors explained that the children’s values reflected what they believe adults value. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 