st several weeks made up one of the more fulfilling Augusts the Miser can remember. Is it so wrong, then, to want to eke out one last weekend in the warmth of the sun?', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '30', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Jazz to End Summer in Rockaway Beach and Brooklyn', 'kicker': 'Weekend Miser', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Jazz to End Summer in Rockaway Beach and Brooklyn', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Jazz', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Brooklyn (NYC)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-09-04T20:08:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By A. C. Lee', 'person': [{'firstname': 'A.', 'middlename': 'C.', 'lastname': 'Lee', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1af77f2b-c230-5c1a-a142-fe360574aef7', 'word_count': 414, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1af77f2b-c230-5c1a-a142-fe360574aef7'}, {'abstract': 'The London-based designer, who presents for the first time in New York this season, on his influences.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/09/04/gareth-pugh-designer-profile-new-york-fashion-week-spring-2015/', 'snippet': 'The London-based designer, who presents for the first time in New York this season, on his influences.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Today, the London-based designer Gareth Pugh, who has received years of acclaim for his bold collections and sometimes outlandish runway shows, will present at New York Fashion Week for the first time. Except it’s not a fashion show per se, but rather what’s being touted as a “live, immersive fashion experience,” incorporating videos by Pugh’s longtime collaborator Ruth Hogben and choreography by Wayne MacGregor.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/04/t-magazine/04pugh-miller-main/04pugh-miller-main-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 360, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/09/04/t-magazine/04pugh-miller-main/04pugh-miller-main-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 360}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/04/t-magazine/04pugh-miller-main/04pugh-miller-main-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 614, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/04/t-magazine/04pugh-miller-main/04pugh-miller-main-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1229, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/04/t-magazine/04pugh-miller-main/04pugh-miller-main-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/09/04/t-magazine/04pugh-miller-main/04pugh-miller-main-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/04/t-magazine/04pugh-miller-main/04pugh-miller-main-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Gareth Pugh on Nick Knight, Holiday Packages and the Enduring Appeal of ‘The Wicker Man’', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fashion and Apparel', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'New York Fashion Week', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Pugh, Gareth', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-09-04T20:10:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'TStyle', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ken Miller', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ken', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Miller', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c36c83aa-51da-541c-a8e2-eebf19c709c0', 'word_count': 161, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c36c83aa-51da-541c-a8e2-eebf19c709c0'}, {'abstract': 'The three-judge panel ruled the gay marriage bans violated the federal Constitution, thereby increasing the number of states where same-sex marriage will be legal to 21.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/09/05/us/us-appellate-court-overturns-same-sex-marriage-bans-in-wisconsin-and-indiana.html', 'snippet': 'The three-judge panel ruled the gay marriage bans violated the federal Constitution, thereby increasing the number of states where same-sex marriage will be legal to 21.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Only nine days after hearing arguments in the case, a federal appeals court in Chicago declared the bans on same-sex marriage in Indiana and Wisconsin to be unconstitutional on Thursday, adding to the list of marriage cases that could wind up before the Supreme Court in the year ahead. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Gay-Marriage Bans Fall in Wisconsin and Indiana', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Gay-Marriage Bans Fall in Wisconsin and Indiana', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Same-Sex Marriage, Civil Unions and Domestic Partnerships', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Eckholm, Erik', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Indiana', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Wisconsin', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-09-04T20:18:37+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Erik Eckholm', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Erik', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Eckholm', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/eb3ff57e-4456-56b1-a4a2-930748b11de0', 'word_count': 468, 'uri': 'nyt://article/eb3ff57e-4456-56b1-a4a2-930748b11de0'}, {'abstract': 'Joseph O’Neill’s “The Dog” is about a lost and tormented New York lawyer working in Dubai for a family of Lebanese billionaires.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/09/07/books/review/the-dog-by-joseph-oneill.html', 'snippet': 'Joseph O’Neill’s “The Dog” is about a lost and tormented New York lawyer working in Dubai for a family of Lebanese billionaires.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The number of serious literary novels that take for their setting the desert city of Dubai could be numbered on a hand’s digits, with the thumb, ring finger and pinkie unused. When the editors of Time Out Dubai enumerated their “Top 10 Books” about — and set in — Dubai, they came up with, among others, the following: “Carte Blanche,” a James Bond novel by Jeffery Deaver; Ben Mezrich’s “get-rich-quick story” “Rigged”; and Candace Havens’s “Charmed and Dangerous: Bronwyn the Witch No. 1.” Of that last, the venerable travel guide remarks: “This chick-friendly romance by U.S. resident Havens takes an adventurous turn when Bronwyn, a 25-year-old witch from Texas, accepts a job with the British prime minister and finds herself traveling to Dubai, where she meets a sheikh who insists she accept the use of his private jet.” You get the picture. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/25/books/review/0907-BKS-OSBORNE-COVER/0907-BKS-OSBORNE-COVER-articleLarge-v3.jpg', 'height': 783, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/08/25/books/review/0907-BKS-OSBORNE-COVER/0907-BKS-OSBORNE-COVER-articleLarge-v3.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 783}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/25/books/review/0907-BKS-OSBORNE-COVER/0907-BKS-OSBORNE-COVER-jumbo-v3.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 785, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/25/books/review/0907-BKS-OSBORNE-COVER/0907-BKS-OSBORNE-COVER-superJumbo-v3.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1569, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/25/books/review/0907-BKS-OSBORNE-COVER/0907-BKS-OSBORNE-COVER-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/08/25/books/review/0907-BKS-OSBORNE-COVER/0907-BKS-OSBORNE-COVER-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/25/books/review/0907-BKS-OSBORNE-COVER/0907-BKS-OSBORNE-COVER-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Time Out Dubai', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Time Out Dubai', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'The Dog (Book)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': "O'Neill, Joseph", 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-09-04T20:18:58+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Lawrence Osborne', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Lawrence', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Osborne', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/749c6c7d-8996-5c09-869b-73db3e1cc6a8', 'word_count': 1570, 'uri': 'nyt://article/749c6c7d-8996-5c09-869b-73db3e1cc6a8'}, {'abstract': 'Police officers and criminals are hard to tell apart in James Ellroy’s novel of 1940s Los Angeles.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/09/07/books/review/james-ellroys-perfidia.html', 'snippet': 'Police officers and criminals are hard to tell apart in James Ellroy’s novel of 1940s Los Angeles.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Few writers, once established in the public consciousness, have changed their style as drastically as James Ellroy. In his early days, Ellroy wandered through the boneyards of 1980s pulp, channeling Jim Thompson and Dashiell Hammett before he found his own voice with a trilogy of contemporary novels about a troubled, racist genius cop named Lloyd Hopkins. Hopkins was morally despicable in the day-to-day, but compared with the monsters he fought in the neon-drench of underworld Los Angeles, he was quasi-\xadangelic. When Ellroy closed out the Hopkins trilogy with “Suicide Hill” in 1986, he shuttered his interest in topical culture as well and moved into the second incarnation of his career, that of the wildly romantic yet increasingly bilious chronicler of Los Angeles in the years immediately following World War II.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/07/books/review/0907-BKS-LEHANE/0907-BKS-LEHANE-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 511, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/09/07/books/review/0907-BKS-LEHANE/0907-BKS-LEHANE-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 511}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/07/books/review/0907-BKS-LEHANE/0907-BKS-LEHANE-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 872, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/07/books/review/0907-BKS-LEHANE/0907-BKS-LEHANE-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1744, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/07/books/review/0907-BKS-LEHANE/0907-BKS-LEHANE-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/09/07/books/review/0907-BKS-LEHANE/0907-BKS-LEHANE-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/07/books/review/0907-BKS-LEHANE/0907-BKS-LEHANE-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Big Sweep', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Big Sweep', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ellroy, James', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-09-04T20:19:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dennis Lehane', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dennis', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Lehane', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/4e1c0670-1525-5f49-b577-f3cd06d47ce7', 'word_count': 1308, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4e1c0670-1525-5f49-b577-f3cd06d47ce7'}, {'abstract': 'In his years in Argentina, Adolf Eichmann remained a convinced Nazi, proud of his wartime achievements.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/09/07/books/review/eichmann-before-jerusalem-by-bettina-stangneth.html', 'snippet': 'In his years in Argentina, Adolf Eichmann remained a convinced Nazi, proud of his wartime achievements.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Ever since his capture in the early 1960s, Otto Adolf Eichmann, who was in charge of Jewish affairs during the Third Reich, has been the subject of unsettled and passionate controversy — centered, above all, on Hannah Arendt’s portrait of him at his 1961 trial. Her “Eichmann in Jerusalem” in many ways mirrored Eichmann’s own self-presentation. She insisted that, contrary to expectations, the man in the dock was not some kind of demonic Nazi sadist but a thoughtless, relatively anonymous, nonideological bureaucrat dutifully executing orders for the emigration, deportation and murder of European Jewry. Arendt’s insights — that genocide and bureaucratic banality are not necessarily opposed, that fanatical anti-Semitism (or for that matter, any ideological predisposition) is not a sufficient precondition for mass murder — remain pertinent.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/07/books/review/07aschheim/07aschheim-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 655, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xla