 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-09-04T20:19:54+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Steven Aschheim', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Steven', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Aschheim', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/e51bf787-92b9-572b-bb0a-71a0ce88852f', 'word_count': 1239, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e51bf787-92b9-572b-bb0a-71a0ce88852f'}, {'abstract': 'In “Frontera,” directed by Michael Berry, Ed Harris plays a retired sheriff in modern-day Arizona challenged by events at a border crossing.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/09/05/movies/ed-harris-rides-high-in-frontera.html', 'snippet': 'In “Frontera,” directed by Michael Berry, Ed Harris plays a retired sheriff in modern-day Arizona challenged by events at a border crossing.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The image of Ed Harris in a cowboy hat, astride a horse, in “Frontera” is as resonant a symbol of American stoicism as Gary Cooper, glowering and tight-lipped, in “High Noon.” In this first feature directed by Michael Berry, Mr. Harris, the square-jawed 63-year-old actor who portrayed John Glenn in “The Right Stuff,” plays Roy, a retired sheriff in southern Arizona. And when the camera gazes up at him, tall in the saddle, he exudes a righteous, buck-stops-here stubbornness. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/05/arts/FRONTERA1/FRONTERA1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/09/05/arts/FRONTERA1/FRONTERA1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'ima