 remembered for her jokes. For that reason, The Times asked readers on Twitter to share their favorite zingers and one-liners delivered by Ms. Rivers over the arc of her comedic career. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/05/arts/rivers-comedy/rivers-comedy-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 398, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/09/05/arts/rivers-comedy/rivers-comedy-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 398}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/05/arts/rivers-comedy/rivers-comedy-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 680, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/05/arts/rivers-comedy/rivers-comedy-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1360, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/05/arts/rivers-comedy/rivers-comedy-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/09/05/arts/rivers-comedy/rivers-comedy-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/05/arts/rivers-comedy/rivers-comedy-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'What Was Your Favorite Joan Rivers Joke?', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Comedy and Humor', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rivers, Joan', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-09-04T20:27:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael Roston', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Roston', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4d2f1312-8187-57cf-97f0-e34e7a7ce2cb', 'word_count': 126, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4d2f1312-8187-57cf-97f0-e34e7a7ce2cb'}, {'abstract': 'Tim Sutton’s “Memphis” follows a blocked songwriter (Willis Earl Beal) as he struggles to rediscover his muse.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/09/05/movies/memphis-from-tim-sutton-mixes-music-and-mysticism.html', 'snippet': 'Tim Sutton’s “Memphis” follows a blocked songwriter (Willis Earl Beal) as he struggles to rediscover his muse.', 'lead_paragraph': '“Everything is artifice,” declares Willis (Willis Earl Beal), the melancholy, creatively blocked singer, songwriter and restless dreamer at the heart of “Memphis,” a soulful cinematic tone poem written and directed by Tim Sutton. This luminescent second feature from Mr. Sutton was inspired partly by the legend of O. V. Wright, an African-American blues and gospel singer who had several R&B hits in the 1960s and early ’70s and died in 1980 at 41. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/05/arts/05MEMPHIS/MEMPHIS-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 337, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/09/05/arts/05MEMPHIS/MEMPHIS-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 337}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/05/