esk': 'Fashion & Style', 'section_name': 'Fashion & Style', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Slideshow', '_id': 'nyt://slideshow/33619290-4de0-57a7-8c04-7403648f057c', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://slideshow/33619290-4de0-57a7-8c04-7403648f057c'}, {'abstract': '“Nam June Paik: Becoming Robot” a large exhibition at Asia Society Museum, explores the artist’s use of technology.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/09/05/arts/design/nam-june-paiks-work-at-asia-society.html', 'snippet': '“Nam June Paik: Becoming Robot” a large exhibition at Asia Society Museum, explores the artist’s use of technology.', 'lead_paragraph': 'If you want someone to praise or blame for the relentlessly wired, chatty, information-soaked 21st-century world we inhabit, the artist Nam June Paik is an apt candidate. Credited as the founder of video art in the 1960s, he turned television into an interactive vehicle for radical theater. He invented the phrase “electronic superhighway” and imagined beta versions of smartphones, Google Glass, distant learning, YouTube, Instagram and the Internet itself. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '23', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Hitchhiker on an Electronic Road', 'kicker': 'Art Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Hitchhiker on an Electronic Road', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cotter, Holland', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Asia Society', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Robots and Robotics', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sculpture', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Paik, Nam June', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-09-04T21:34:37+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts',