+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Dining', 'section_name': 'Food', 'byline': {'original': 'By Eric Asimov', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Eric', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Asimov', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/bf4a03da-1a7f-574e-89bb-2c4fc3eeddf7', 'word_count': 1788, 'uri': 'nyt://article/bf4a03da-1a7f-574e-89bb-2c4fc3eeddf7'}, {'abstract': 'The three-judge panel declined to reinstate a lawsuit filed by 34 victims who said they were abused in high school by rabbis from the 1970s to the early 1990s.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/09/05/nyregion/appeals-court-agrees-sex-abuse-victims-waited-too-long-to-sue-yeshiva-university.html', 'snippet': 'The three-judge panel declined to reinstate a lawsuit filed by 34 victims who said they were abused in high school by rabbis from the 1970s to the early 1990s.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A federal appeals court panel ruled on Thursday that dozens of men who say Yeshiva University covered up their sexual abuse at the hands of rabbis cannot sue for damages because too many years had elapsed since the abuse took place.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Appeals Court Says Men Alleging Sex Abuse Waited Too Long to Sue Yeshiva University', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sex Crimes', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Yeshiva University', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Washington Heights (Manhattan, NY)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Suits and Litigation (Civil)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-09-04T22:28:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Benjamin Mueller', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Benjamin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Mueller', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a5c4a57b-b002-5893-a27d-66ce133bace2', 'word_count': 501, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a5c4a57b-b002-5893-a27d-66ce133bace2'}, {'abstract': 'As an alternative to bronzer, a new golden beige lipstick mimicked the natural flush of the model’s cheeks.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/09/04/new-york-fashion-week-spring-summer-2015-creatures-of-the-wind-nars-backstage-beauty-report/', 'snippet': 'As an alternative to bronzer, a new golden beige lipstick mimicked the natural flush of the model’s cheeks.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The start of New York Fashion Week might mark the unofficial beginning of fall, but backstage at Creatures of the Wind this morning, the designers Shane Gabier and Christopher Peters were still in summer mode. Dressed in t-shirts and looking laid-back as usual, the pair talked about exploring distant horizons with their prints and patterns, and the casual appeal of their debut shoe collection (“they’re really meant to be an extension of a summer shoe,” Gabier said, glancing at the sporty slip-ons at his feet). The models’ hair and makeup evoked the same breezy vibe. “Everything is relaxed and easy, with a certain softness to it,” Peters said. During casting earlier this week, on a day marked with humidity and thunderclouds, the makeup artist James Boehmer noted the natural flush evident on everyone’s skin. So instead of using bronzer on the cheeks, he dabbed on the new Nars Lipstick in Ligueria (out this spring) to recreate that moment. “This gives you that overheated effect,” he explained, tapping in the golden beige pigments. Outside the show, where the temperature was in the low 80s, the same tawny glow was easy to spot.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlar