news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Stephanie Clifford', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Stephanie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Clifford', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d7625983-cdbb-565d-878a-f126010d4947', 'word_count': 727, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d7625983-cdbb-565d-878a-f126010d4947'}, {'abstract': 'A methodology that aims to ease partisan divide through personal connections could have advantages both in politics and personal life.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/09/04/realizing-we-the-people-one-person-at-a-time/', 'snippet': 'A methodology that aims to ease partisan divide through personal connections could have advantages both in politics and personal life.', 'lead_paragraph': 'About two years ago, I had the opportunity to participate in a retreat led by the renowned author and activist Parker J. Palmer. The retreat was based on a methodology called a Circle of Trust, drawn from Palmer’s writings, intended to help people step back from the noise of modern life, reflect, and return more centered and effective in their vocations. I was amazed at how, in just two days, this process brought 40 individuals together into respectful and strikingly honest discourse. It helped participants connect to their core values and sparked many new relationships.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/04/25/opinion/opinionator-pog-fixes/opinionator-pog-fixes-articleLarge.png', 'height': 600, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/04/25/opinion/opinionator-pog-fixes/opinionator-pog-fixes-articleLarge.png', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 600}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/04/25/opinion/opinionator-pog-fixes/opinionator-pog-fixes-jumbo.png', 'height': 1000, 'width': 1000, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/04/25/opinion/opinionator-pog-fixes/opinionator-pog-fixes-superJumbo.png', 'height': 1000, 'width': 1000, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/04/25/opinion/opinionator-pog-fixes/opinionator-pog-fixes-thumbStandard-v2.png', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/04/25/opinion/opinionator-pog-fixes/opinionator-pog-fixes-thumbStandard-v2.png', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/04/25/opinion/opinionator-pog-fixes/opinionator-pog-fixes-thumbLarge-v2.png', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Reclaiming  ‘We the People,’ One Person at a Time', 'kicker': 'Fixes', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Education (K-12)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Palmer, Parker J', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-09-05T00:30:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Bornstein', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bornstein', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4978b7ca-0870-5650-9ae6-fa02326d0186', 'word_count': 1859, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4978b7ca-0870-5650-9ae6-fa02326d0186'}, {'abstract': 'A guide to cultural events taking place in the New York City area, including literary talks and readings, street fairs and culinary festivals.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/09/05/arts/spare-times-for-sept-5-11.html', 'snippet': 'A guide to cultural events taking place in the New York City area, including literary talks and readings, street fairs and culinary festivals.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Readings Around Town (through next Friday) A wide range of authors will be in the spotlight next week, starting on Monday at the Strand Book Store, where crime and suspense are the focus of a reading with Megan Abbott, whose latest novel is “The Fever,” and Chelsea Cain, who recently released “One Kick.” At 7 p.m., 828 Broadway, at 12th Street, East Village; purchase of either book is required for admission. Also on Monday, at 7 p.m., Donald Antrim, a MacArthur fellow, will read from his new collection of stories, “The Emerald Light in the Air,” at McNally Jackson, 52 Prince Street, between Lafayette and Mulberry Streets, SoHo; free.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '20', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Spare Times for Sept. 5-11', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Spare Times', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Culture (Arts)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-09-05T00:31:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew Boryga and Anne Mancuso', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Boryga', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Anne', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Mancuso', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Schedule', '_id': 'nyt://article/60171f3f-5b34-5a6f-921c-0f09f9c870e8', 'word_count': 3813, 'uri': 'nyt://article/60171f3f-5b34-5a6f-921c-0f09f9c870e8'}, {'abstract': 'The company hopes the construction of a factory in Nevada will catalyze the entry of the electric car into the American market and provide as many as 6,500 manufacturing jobs.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/09/05/business/energy-environment/nevada-a-winner-in-teslas-battery-contest.html', 'snippet': 'The company hopes the construction of a factory in Nevada will catalyze the entry of the electric car into the American market and provide as many as 6,500 manufacturing jobs.', 'lead_paragraph': 'After pitting five potential host states against one another in a quest for hundreds of millions of dollars in incentives, Tesla Motors said Thursday that it had struck a deal with Nevada for construction of its sprawling factory to build batteries for electric cars and the power grid.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/05/business/Battery/Battery-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 659, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/09/05/business/Battery/Battery-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 659}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/05/business/Battery/Battery-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 933, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/05/business/Battery/Battery-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1865, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbna