/article/cda67fad-84ac-500a-b1ab-ff6ae95964e4', 'word_count': 592, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cda67fad-84ac-500a-b1ab-ff6ae95964e4'}, {'abstract': 'A flavor chemist lands a job as an animal tester and is disturbed by the effects a new sweetener appears to have on his rats.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/09/07/books/review/sweetness-9-by-stephan-eirik-clark.html', 'snippet': 'A flavor chemist lands a job as an animal tester and is disturbed by the effects a new sweetener appears to have on his rats.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It’s a very “now” premise: the confessional narrative of a food scientist complicit in the development of a chemical additive that has played a sneaky role in America’s obesity epidemic. The nonfiction shelves already abound with gastro-exposés (hello, Michael Pollan, Marion Nestle and Michael Moss), so why not fiction?', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '17', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/07/books/review/0907-BKS-KAMP/0907-BKS-KAMP-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 858, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/09/07/books/review/0907-BKS-KAMP/0907-BKS-KAMP-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 858}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/07/books/review/0907-BKS-KAMP/0907-BKS-KAMP-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 716, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/07/books/review/0907-BKS-KAMP/0907-BKS-KAMP-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1432, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/07/books/review/0907-BKS-KAMP/0907-BKS-KAMP-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/09/07/books/review/0907-BKS-KAMP/0907-BKS-KAMP-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/07/books/review/0907-BKS-KAMP/0907-BKS-KAMP-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Weird Science', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Weird Science', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Sweetness #9 (Book)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Clark, Stephan Eirik', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-09-05T14:56:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Kamp', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kamp', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/33e2c68c-c244-585d-a26a-921aa2f784ea', 'word_count': 1105, 'uri': 'nyt://article/33e2c68c-c244-585d-a26a-921aa2f784ea'}, {'abstract': 'Grief and cold dominate this novel set in remote Alaska.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/09/07/books/review/hold-the-dark-by-william-giraldi.html', 'snippet': 'Grief and cold dominate this novel set in remote Alaska.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Early in William Giraldi’s fierce, extraordinary new novel, “Hold the Dark,” the nature writer Russell Core tracks a pack of wolves deep into the Alaskan tundra. The wolves, it seems, have been taking children from a nearby village, and Core, a wolf expert, has been summoned by the latest victim’s mother to “get her boy’s bones and maybe slaughter the wolf that took him.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/07/books/review/07WILWOL/07WILWOL-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 906, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/09/07/books/review/07WILWOL/07WILWOL-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 906}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/07/books/review/07WILWOL/07WILWOL-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 679, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/07/books/review/07WILWOL/07WILWOL-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1357, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/07/books/review/07WILWOL/07WILWOL-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/09/07/books/review/07WILWOL/07WILWOL-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/07/books/review/07WILWOL/07WILWOL-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Hour of the Wolf', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Hour of the Wolf', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Giraldi, William (1974- )', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-09-05T14:56:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By John Wilwol', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Wilwol', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/52b2e171-3342-53c9-abab-f0100de5adb2', 'word_count': 608, 'uri': 'nyt://article/52b2e171-3342-53c9-abab-f0100de5adb2'}, {'abstract': 'A journalist examines Asian-American manhood and the cultural stereotypes that shaped his coming-of-age.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/09/07/books/review/big-little-man-by-alex-tizon.html', 'snippet': 'A journalist examines Asian-American manhood and the cultural stereotypes that shaped his coming-of-age.', 'lead_paragraph': 'There’s nothing subtle or coy or particularly evocative about the title of “Big Little Man: In Search of My Asian Self,” a memoir by the Pulitzer Prize-winning journalist Alex Tizon. In fact, the title is so straightforward that when I was asked to write this review, I felt vaguely offended by the idea that my editor would push such an obvious identity-politics project on me, perhaps believing that I, by dint of being “Asian-American,” must therefore have some special insight into Tizon and this search for his Asian self. I am 20 years younger than Tizon and grew up in snooty college towns and attended schools where I was taught that there was a hierarchy of race writing. The immigrant experience was not enough in itself, and when I read the self-evident title of Tizon’s book, I flashed back to a fiction workshop in \xadColumbia’s M.F.A. program and a \xadprofessor who told me that the book I was working on — a book much like Tizon’s — could “definitely be published,” but wondered if there was anything I could do to elevate it into something more “interesting.” I recognized the code immediately — anything labeled “the immigrant experience” would be considered earnest and déclassé, and would therefore need to be gussied up in fashionable literary frills. For the next year, I wrote bad evocations of Lydia Davis, Lorrie Moore, Barthelme and Borges. My protagonists, God help them, became whiter and whiter. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/07/books/review/07KANG/07KANG-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 749, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/09/07/books/review/07KANG/07KANG-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 749}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/07/books/review/07KANG/07KANG-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 821, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/07/books/review/07KANG/07KANG-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1204, 'width': 965, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/07/books/review/07KANG/07KANG-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/09/07/books/review/07KANG/07KANG-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/07/books/review/07KANG/07KANG-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Minority Report', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Minority Report', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kang, Jay Caspian', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Tizon, Alex', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Asian-Americans', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-09-05T14:57:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jay Caspian Kang', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jay', 'middlename': 'Caspian', 'lastname': 'Kang', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/2f48da4e-721f-5f40-8abd-8c3f559c160d', 'word_count': 1266, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2f48da4e-721f-5f40-8abd-8c3f559c160d'}, {'abstract': 'A series of challenges unsettles an extended family’s comfortable existence.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/09/07/books/review/time-present-and-time-past-by-deirdre-madden.html', 'snippet': 'A series of challenges unsettles an extended family’s comfortable existence.', 'lead_paragraph': 'We spend our lives tumbling through time, carried along as it eddies and pools, buckles and loops, stutters and flies. As T. S. Eliot put it in the opening lines of “Burnt Norton,” “Time present and time past / Are both perhaps present in time future, / And time future contained in time past.” We like to think that time is ours to waste, but it is we who pass through time, wasting only ourselves.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '21', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/07/books/review/0907-BKS-FERGUSON/0907-BKS-FERGUSON-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 627, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/09/07/books/review/0907-BKS-FERGUSON/0907-BKS-FERGUSON-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 627}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/07/books/review/0907-BKS-FERGUSON/0907-BKS-FERGUSON-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 689, 'width': 659, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/07/books/review/0907-BKS-FERGUSON/0907-BKS-FERGUSON-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 689, 'width': 659, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/07/books/review/0907-BKS-FERGUSON/0907-BKS-FERGUSON-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/09/07/books/review/0907-BKS-FERGUSON/0907-BKS-FERGUSON-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/07/books/review/0907-BKS-FERGUSON/0907-BKS-FERGUSON-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Dubliners', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Dubliners', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Madden, Deirdre', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-09-05T14:58:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sarah Ferguson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sarah', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ferguson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/f1fd0c6b-d81f-5aa3-9f57-65522edf3895', 'word_count': 570, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f1fd0c6b-d81f-5aa3-9f57-65522edf3895'}, {'abstract': 'This collection of reviews and lectures, posthumously published, mourns the end of European high culture.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/09/07/books/review/fractured-times-by-eric-hobsbawm.html', 'snippet': 'This collection of reviews and lectures, posthumously published, mourns the end of European high culture.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A 1980 television film written by the British director Stephen Poliakoff depicted an interminable train journey through Europe, in which a young Englishman is trapped in a cramped compartment with a pompous Viennese matron, played by Peggy Ashcroft. The woman is rude and vain, but the film, “Caught on a Train,” refuses to despise her: Instead it recognizes that she is an elderly ambassador for a world that is about to pass away, the lost civilization of prewar Europe.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/07/books/review/07freedland/07freedland-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 861, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/09/07/books/review/07freedland/07freedland-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 861}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/07/books/review/07freedland/07freedland-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 714, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/07/books/review/07freedland/07freedland-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1427, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/07/books/review/07freedland/07freedland-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/09/07/books/review/07freedland/07freedland-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/07/books/review/07freedland/07freedland-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Age of Hobsbawm', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Age of Hobsbawm', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Freedland, Jonathan', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hobsbawm, Eric', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-09-05T14:58:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jonathan Freedland', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jonathan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Freedland', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/0351283f-8194-537e-9f3a-5ef95a765e47', 'word_count': 1184, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0351283f-8194-537e-9f3a-5ef95a765e47'}, {'abstract': 'In Louise Penny’s “The Long Way Home,” Chief Inspector Armand Gamache follows a lost artist’s perilous journey to find himself.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/09/07/books/review/louise-pennys-the-long-way-home-and-more.html', 'snippet': 'In Louise Penny’s “The Long Way Home,” Chief Inspector Armand Gamache follows a lost artist’s perilous journey to find himself.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Sooner or later, every writer of small-town mysteries must confront the occupational hazard of running out of murderers and victims. Louise Penny, who sets her enchanting stories in a sheltered Quebec village she calls Three Pines, keeps inventing new ways to avoid the inevitable reckoning. In THE LONG WAY HOME (Minotaur, $27.99), she makes the task more challenging by putting her detective out to pasture. Chief Inspector Armand Gamache has retired from the Sûreté du Québec to live a contemplative life, enjoying the company of his eccentric neighbors and hoping to be anointed with the “balm in Gilead” that will “make the wounded whole” and “cure a sin-sick soul.” Gamache is moved by the plight of one neighbor, Clara Morrow, a painter whose husband, Peter, left home when her artistic reputation eclipsed his own — promising to return in exactly one year, presumably after intense soul-searching. But that anniversary has come and gone without any word, and Clara is afraid Peter’s in terrible trouble, might even be dead. Clutching at a few meager clues, the most intriguing being some atrocious paintings Peter sent to his sister’s child, Gamache and Clara follow the lost artist’s perilous journey to find himself.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '23', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/07/books/review/0907-BKS-CRIME/0907-BKS-CRIME-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 855, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/09/07/books/review/0907-BKS-CRIME/0907-BKS-CRIME-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 855}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/07/books/review/0907-BKS-CRIME/0907-BKS-CRIME-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 719, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/07/books/review/0907-BKS-CRIME/0907-BKS-CRIME-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1438, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/07/books/review/0907-BKS-CRIME/0907-BKS-CRIME-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/09/07/books/review/0907-BKS-CRIME/0907-BKS-CRIME-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/07/books/review/0907-BKS-CRIME/0907-BKS-CRIME-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Art of Murder', 'kicker': 'Crime', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Art of Murder', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'The Long Way Home (Book)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Penny, Louise', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Child, Lee', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'French, Tana', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Krueger, William Kent', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-09-05T14:59:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Marilyn Stasio', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Marilyn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Stasio', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/5fd4ce13-8a4a-555d-a59b-4f2bf7676774', 'word_count': 920, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5fd4ce13-8a4a-555d-a59b-4f2bf7676774'}, {'abstract': 'The Yankees and the Cardinals met for Game 7 of the World Series as a series of momentous events shook the world.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/09/06/upshot/the-earth-stood-still-but-the-game-played-on.html', 'snippet': 'The Yankees and the Cardinals met for Game 7 of the World Series as a series of momentous events shook the world.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The “split screen” convergence of important news this summer — from Russia, Ukraine, Israel, Gaza, Syria, Iraq and Ferguson, Mo. — recalls an astonishing day 50 years ago next month. On Thursday, Oct. 15, 1964, the Yankees played the Cardinals at Busch Stadium in St. Louis. It was the deciding game of that year’s World Series, and, off the field, it seemed that all hell was breaking loose.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/05/upshot/06UP-Series/06UP-Series-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 512, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/09/05/upshot/06UP-Series/06UP-Series-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 512}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/05/upshot/06UP-Series/06UP-Series-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 874, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/05/upshot/06UP-Series/06UP-Series-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1748, 'width': 2048, 's