 Vincent A. Cianci Jr. has exerted outsize influence as candidates scrambled to avoid indirectly helping him by splitting the vote.', 'lead_paragraph': 'PROVIDENCE, R.I. — It seemed outlandish, unbelievable at first. Vincent A. Cianci Jr., the infamous former mayor of Providence whose two administrations were both cut short by felony convictions, announced earlier this summer that he wanted his old job back. With every ounce of the bombast and zeal that has, over the past several decades, made him this city’s premier practitioner of chutzpah, the man who served more than four years in federal prison is running for another four years as mayor. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/06/us/PROVIDENCE1/PROVIDENCE1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 385, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/09/06/us/PROVIDENCE1/PROVIDENCE1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 385}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/06/us/PROVIDENCE1/PROVIDENCE1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 657, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/06/us/PROVIDENCE1/PROVIDENCE1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1313, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/06/us/PROVIDENCE1/PROVIDENCE1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/09/06/us/PROVIDENCE1/PROVIDENCE1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thum