, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Roman Catholic Church', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-09-06T04:15:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Samuel G. Freedman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Samuel', 'middlename': 'G.', 'lastname': 'Freedman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8de95b6f-15f9-5bc1-b815-40c432e70440', 'word_count': 1075, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8de95b6f-15f9-5bc1-b815-40c432e70440'}, {'abstract': 'Television highlights.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/09/06/arts/television/whats-on-tv-saturday.html', 'snippet': 'Television highlights.', 'lead_paragraph': '8 P.M. (HBO) 12 YEARS A SLAVE (2013) In this Oscar winner for best picture, written by John Ridley and directed by Steve McQueen, Chiwetel Ejiofor, above, portrays Solomon Northup, a free African-American man who, in 1841, was kidnapped in Washington and sold into slavery. Paul Giamatti plays the brutish Louisiana trader who sells Solomon to William Ford (Benedict Cumberbatch), a seemingly decent plantation owner, who in turn sells him to Edwin Epps (Michael Fassbender) a drunken madman, who epitomizes the villainy of slave ownership. Lupita Nyong’o won a best supporting actress Oscar for her turn as Patsey, Epp’s favorite, whom he brutalizes. “Mr. McQueen’s sympathies are as unqualified as his control,” Manohla Dargis wrote in The New York Times. “There is much to admire about ‘12 Years a Slave,’ including the cleareyed, unsentimental quality of its images — this is a place where trees hang with beautiful moss and black bodies — and how Mr. Ejiofor’s restrained, open, translucent performance works as a ballast, something to cling onto, especially during the frenzies of violence.” And while these scenes are hard to watch, “the genius of ‘12 Years a Slave’ is its insistence on banal evil, and on terror, that seeped into souls, bound bodies and reaped an enduring, terrible price.” ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What’s on TV Saturday', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'What’s On TV Saturday', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2014-09-06T05:00:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kathryn Shattuck', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kathryn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Shattuck', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Schedule', '_id': 'nyt://article/5d88e183-ffc5-5587-ad1a-4bfd6a627cb1', 'word_count': 700, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5d88e183-ffc5-5587-ad1a-4bfd6a627cb1'}, {'abstract': 'Corrections appearing in print on September 6, 2014.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/09/06/pageoneplus/corrections-september-6-2014.html', 'snippet': 'Corrections appearing in print on September 6, 2014.', 'lead_paragraph': 'FRONT PAGE', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Corrections: September 6, 2014', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Corrections', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2014-09-06T06:27:24+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Summary', 'section_name': 'Corrections', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8be3c61c-697f-5ea2-b84c-d1e9dff85155', 'word_count': 356, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8be3c61c-697f-5ea2-b84c-d1e9dff85155'}, {'abstract': 'It may be Saturday, but while the world sleeps in, the fashion pack troops bravely on.', 'web_url': 'https://runway.blogs.nytimes.com/2