ousands of attendees, are meant to serve as escapes. But what about when you need to escape from festivals? This weekend, just north of New York City, two separate events attempt to reclaim the idea of festivals for people seeking something more intimate. ', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Breaking Loose From Big Festivals', 'kicker': 'The Week Ahead', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Pop: Breaking Loose From Big Festivals', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2014-09-06T16:00:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jon Caramanica', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jon', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Caramanica', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4fe60f9e-8a39-58e7-b306-c40add172b4a', 'word_count': 136, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4fe60f9e-8a39-58e7-b306-c40add172b4a'}, {'abstract': 'Artists frequently dislike the labels assigned to their work: Philip Glass prefers “music with repetitive structures” to “minimalism,” for example.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/09/07/arts/music/glass-and-reich-share-a-stage.html', 'snippet': 'Artists frequently dislike the labels assigned to their work: Philip Glass prefers “music with repetitive structures” to “minimalism,” for example.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Artists frequently dislike the labels assigned to their work: Philip Glass prefers “music with repetitive structures” to “minimalism,” for example. But the minimalist label has stuck. As part of the Brooklyn Academy of Music’s Next Wave Festival, Mr. Glass will be joined by Steve Reich, another pioneer of the genre, for three programs that illustrate their aesthetic with some of their best-known works. The two ensembles they founded will perform, and for the first time in more than three decades, the two composers will collaborate onstage during several selections. ', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/07/arts/07GUIDE5/07GUIDE5-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 472, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/09/07/arts/07GUIDE5/07GUIDE5-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 472}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/07/arts/07GUIDE5/07GUIDE5-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 806, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/07/arts/07GUIDE5/07GUIDE5-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1611, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/07/arts/07GUIDE5/07GUIDE5-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/09/07/arts/07GUIDE5/07GUIDE5-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/07/arts/07GUIDE5/07GUIDE5-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Glass and Reich Share a Stage', 'kicker': 'The Week Ahead', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Classical: Glass and Reich Share a Stage', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Glass, Philip', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Reich, Steve', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Classical Music', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-09-06T16:00:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Vivien Schweitzer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Vivien', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schweitzer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c01f3a8e-6c11-5109-ae47-ccd754d50070', 'word_count': 180, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c01f3a8e-6c11-5109-ae47-ccd754d50070'}, {'abstract': 'HBO’s “Boardwalk Empire” begins its fifth and final season, debuting Sunday.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/09/07/arts/television/nucky-thompson-back-in-the-day.html', 'snippet': 'HBO’s “Boardwalk Empire” begins its fifth and final season, debuting Sunday.', 'lead_paragraph': '“Be honest and true, boys/Whatever you do, boys/Let this be your motto through life,” a woman recites as a battalion of lads flit like fish through a watery underworld, grasping at gold coins raining down. Cut to the pier above, where the wealthy are toasting their sun-kissed summer by tossing away their money in Atlantic City, circa 1884. Thus begins the origin story of Nucky Thompson, a very good boy (played by Nolan Lyons) until Commodore Louis Kaestner, the power broker who becomes his mentor, gets hold of him in HBO’s “Boardwalk Empire.” Nearly 50 years later, the adult Nucky (Steve Buscemi) is fighting to go legit by courting the Bacardi Rum family in Cuba in the series’s fifth and final season, debuting Sunday. But as he gazes down at boys diving into the Havana harbor, his mind returns to his stormy New Jersey childhood, now a world apart in every conceivable sense.', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/07/arts/07GUIDE6/07GUIDE6-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 399, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/09/07/arts/07GUIDE6/07GUIDE6-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 399}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/07/arts/07GUIDE6/07GUIDE6-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 681, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/07/arts/07GUIDE6/07GUIDE6-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1363, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/07/arts/07GUIDE6/07GUIDE6-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/09/07/arts/07GUIDE6/07GUIDE6-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/07/arts/07GUIDE6/07GUIDE6-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Nucky Thompson, Back in the Day', 'kicker': 'The Week Ahead', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Television: Nucky Thompson, Back in the Day', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Boardwalk Empire (TV Program)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-09-06T16:00:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kathryn Shattuck', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kathryn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Shattuck', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3197e21f-e227-5e1c-92a6-9a41c8c839fa', 'word_count': 188, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3197e21f-e227-5e1c-92a6-9a41c8c839fa'}, {'abstract': 'It’s time to fall into dance: City Center’s 2014 Fall for Dance Festival unveils an early kickoff with two free evenings at the Delacorte Theater in Central Park.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/09/07/arts/dance/olden-goldies-and-wild-cards.html', 'snippet': 'It’s time to fall into dance: City Center’s 2014 Fall for Dance Festival unveils an early kickoff with two free evenings at the Delacorte Theater in Central Park.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It’s time to fall into dance: City Center’s 2014 Fall for Dance Festival unveils an early kickoff with two free evenings at the Delacorte Theater in Central Park. Presented in association with the Public Theater, the program covers a wealth of dance styles and just as many moods. The Bill T. Jones/Arnie Zane Company reprises Mr. Jones’s “D-Man in the Waters (Part 1),” created in 1989 in the thick of the AIDS crisis — it’s a piece about loss, resiliency and hope — while New York City Ballet offers William Forsythe’s “Herman Schmerman (Pas de Deux),” a sinuous duet set to an electronic score by Thom Willems. Hubbard Street Dance Chicago explores the heat of North Africa and Spain in “Gnawa,” Nacho Duato’s lively 2005 group work. ', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/07/arts/07GUIDE3/07GUIDE3-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 474, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/09/07/arts/07GUIDE3/07GUIDE3-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 474}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'capti