nis coach who was recently inducted into the International Tennis Hall of Fame discusses the benefits and drawbacks of a disappearing stroke.', 'web_url': 'https://6thfloor.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/09/06/nick-bollettieri-on-the-dinosaur-that-is-the-one-handed-backhand/', 'snippet': 'The legendary tennis coach who was recently inducted into the International Tennis Hall of Fame discusses the benefits and drawbacks of a disappearing stroke.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Wimbledon was where I did most of the reporting for my recent article on the fate of the one-handed backhand. Late in the afternoon on my last day there, I ran into the legendary tennis coach Nick Bollettieri. He was en route to an interview and I was heading for the airport, so our conversation about the one-hander was brief. A few days after my article was published, however, he emailed to say he wanted to talk some more about the one-hander. So last Saturday we met at the U.S. Open for that conversation. The 83-year-old Bollettieri was recently inducted into the International Tennis Hall of Fame, an honor granted in recognition of his role in nurturing some of the sport’s greatest talents, including Andre Agassi, Maria Sharapova, Monica Seles and Jim Courier. (Another Bollettieri disciple, the Japanese player Kei Nishikori, reached the semifinals of this year’s Open with a five-set victory Wednesday over Stan Wawrinka, who has what many consider today’s best one-handed backhand.)', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/05/magazine/05backhand/05backhand-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/09/05/magazine/05backhand/05backhand-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/05/magazine/05b