inal': 'By Russell Shorto', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Russell', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Shorto', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/832a65e0-a931-5ebe-b06f-5f9fccf61347', 'word_count': 883, 'uri': 'nyt://article/832a65e0-a931-5ebe-b06f-5f9fccf61347'}, {'abstract': 'Four dams on the Susquehanna are causing its death.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/09/08/opinion/let-the-susquehanna-river-run-wild.html', 'snippet': 'Four dams on the Susquehanna are causing its death.', 'lead_paragraph': 'IF the Chesapeake Bay is America’s Estuary, then its largest tributary, the Susquehanna River, could arguably be called America’s River. But we certainly don’t treat it as a national treasure: This once magnificent watercourse, which runs through New York, Pennsylvania and Maryland toward the coast, is today an ecological disaster — largely thanks to four hydroelectric dams, built along its lower reaches between 1904 and 1931. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '19', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Let the River Run Wild', 'kicker': 'Op-Ed Contributors', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Let the River Run Wild', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Waldman, John', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Susquehanna River', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Limburg, Karin E', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hydroelectric Power', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Roe, Amy', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Levees and Dams', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Water', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Chesapeake Bay', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fish and Other Marine