ther members of the Atlanta Hawks’ ownership, Bruce Levenson pointed out the team’s continued struggles to attract fans and corporate sponsors from Atlanta’s white community.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Bruce Levenson’s Email to Hawks Executives', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Basketball', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Atlanta Hawks', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-09-07T22:42:18+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Pro Basketball', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Interactive Feature', '_id': 'nyt://interactive/87b5ea25-38a0-594a-bb68-f157b5472dec', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://interactive/87b5ea25-38a0-594a-bb68-f157b5472dec'}, {'abstract': 'SWAT teams, created to quell unrest in Los Angeles in the 1960s, are the principal beneficiaries of heavy-duty military equipment from the federal government.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/09/08/us/the-rise-of-the-swat-team-in-american-policing.html', 'snippet': 'SWAT teams, created to quell unrest in Los Angeles in the 1960s, are the principal beneficiaries of heavy-duty military equipment from the federal government.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Posse comitatus is not a phrase that trips lightly off every tongue. It is typically translated from Latin as “force of the county.” Anyone who has ever watched an old Western movie will instantly recognize the first word as referring to men deputized by the sheriff to chase down some varmints who went thataway. (Rappers and their tag-alongs later gave “posse” a different context.) The full phrase is more obscure, but the concept that it embraces is enshrined in American law. The Posse Comitatus Act, passed in 1878 at the end of Reconstruction and amended but slightly over the decades, prohibits the nation’s armed forces from being used as a police force within the United States. Soldiers, the reasoning goes, exist to fight wars. Chasing local wrongdoers is a job for cops.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Rise of the SWAT Team in American Policing', 'kicker': 'Retro Report', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Police', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Posse Comitatus Act (1878)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Demonstrations, Protests and Riots', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Police Brutality, Misconduct and Shootings', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-09-07T22:45:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Clyde Haberman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Clyde', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Haberman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/067a5f3f-29fd-5510-a460-11ced490b3c7', 'word_count': 1129, 'uri': 'nyt://article/067a5f3f-29fd-5510-a460-11ced490b3c7'}, {'abstract': 'The following tax-exempt fixed-income issues are scheduled for pricing this week.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/09/08/business/treasury-auctions-for-the-week-of-sept-8.html', 'snippet': 'The following tax-exempt fixed-income issues are scheduled for pricing this week.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Treasury’s schedule of financing this week includes Monday’s regular weekly auction of new three- and six-month bills and an auction of four-week bills on Tuesday.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Treasury Auctions for the Week of Sept. 8', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Treasury Auctions Set for This Week', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Stocks and Bonds', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tax Credits, Deductions and Exemptions', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Auctions', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-09-07T22:45:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/bb662a0e-7053-5fb8-bdcc-f7cb3ec2d561', 'word_count': 303, 'uri': 'nyt://article/bb662a0e-7053-5fb8-bdcc-f7cb3ec2d561'}, {'abstract': 'The well-known New Atheist makes a case for the value of “spirituality,” which he bases on his experiences in meditation.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/09/07/sam-harriss-vanishing-self/', 'snippet': 'The well-known New Atheist makes a case for the value of “spirituality,” which he bases on his experiences in meditation.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Sam Harris is a neuroscientist and prominent “new atheist,” who along with others like Richard Dawkins, Daniel Dennett and Christopher Hitchens helped put criticism of religion at the forefront of public debate in recent years. In two previous books, “The End of Faith” and “Letter to a Christian Nation,” Harris argued that theistic religion has no place in a world of science. In his latest book, “Waking Up,” his thought takes a new direction. While still rejecting theism, Harris nonetheless makes a case for the value of “spirituality,” which he bases on his experiences in meditation. I interviewed him recently about the book and some of the arguments he makes in it. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/05/opinion/stone-optical-illusion/stone-optical-illus