e or other information about its origins. Join the conversation by posting about what you see, and why. A live discussion is offered from 9 a.m. to 2 p.m. Eastern time.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Updated: Sept. 9, 2014', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/04/learning/VTS09-08-14LN/VTS09-08-14LN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/09/04/learning/VTS09-08-14LN/VTS09-08-14LN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/04/learning/VTS09-08-14LN/VTS09-08-14LN-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/04/learning/VTS09-08-14LN/VTS09-08-14LN-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/04/learning/VTS09-08-14LN/VTS09-08-14LN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/09/04/learning/VTS09-08-14LN/VTS09-08-14LN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/04/learning/VTS09-08-14LN/VTS09-08-14LN-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'What’s Going On in This Picture? | Sept. 8, 2014', 'kicker': 'The Learning Network', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2014-09-08T11:00:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael Gonchar', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gonchar', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/52e43b03-46b5-5ae8-9b36-a39c5e760eb2', 'word_count': 370, 'uri': 'nyt://article/52e43b03-46b5-5ae8-9b36-a39c5e760eb2'}, {'abstract': 'One client was hesitant to sign on with 10x Management, mostly because of the 15 percent fee. “That could be $30,000 or $40,000 a year,” he said. “That’s a lot of money.”', 'web_url': 'https://boss.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/09/08/representing-programmers-as-if-they-were-celebrities/', 'snippet': 'One client was hesitant to sign on with 10x Management, mostly because of the 15 percent fee. “That could be $30,000 or $40,000 a year,” he said. “That’s a lot of money.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'The pitch: Software developers may not be rock stars but they are in high demand and competition for them, especially among top tech companies, is fierce. Many choose to work as freelancers, taking on short and long-term assignments for a variety of companies. These developers are often better at writing code than they are at writing contracts, negotiating fees, or communicating with clients. 10x Management represents those freelance developers much the way talent agents represent entertainers.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Representing Programmers as if They Were Celebrities', 'kicker': 'You&#039;re the Boss', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Freelancing, Self-Employment and Independent Contracting', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Labor and Jobs', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Small Business', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-09-08T11:00:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Entrepreneurship', 'byline': {'original': 'By Eilene Zimmerman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Eilene', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Zimmerman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0c1d7d43-e8de-5906-83e6-b2bb1d091356', 'word_count': 736, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0c1d7d43-e8de-5906-83e6-b2bb1d091356'}, {'abstract': 'An interview about Sorkin’s article in the magazine’s special Education Issue.', 'web_url': 'https://6thfloor.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/09/08/behind-the-cover-story-andrew-ross-sorkin-on-the-real-bill-gates/', 'snippet': 'An interview about Sorkin’s article in the magazine’s special Education Issue.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Andrew Ross Sorkin, financial columnist for The Times, co-anchor of CNBC’s “Squawk Box” and the author of “Too Big to Fail,” wrote this week’s cover story about the plan by Bill Gates to introduce a new kind of history classes into high schools across the country.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/12/magazine/Sorkin1/Sorkin1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 901, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/05/12/magazine/Sorkin1/Sorkin1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 901}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/12/magazine/Sorkin1/Sorkin1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 682, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': 