placed as supports at the opening of the tomb, Agence France-Presse reported on Monday. The figures were holding up a lintel at the entrance, and were apparently meant to guard the treasures inside the tomb, which dates from the fourth century B.C. and which archaeologists described as the largest ancient tomb discovered in Greece.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Ancient Greek Tomb Yields Two Large Statues', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2014-09-08T20:16:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Allan Kozinn', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Allan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kozinn', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/668ecfbc-45df-5b96-b2cb-9158f9cf1e83', 'word_count': 212, 'uri': 'nyt://article/668ecfbc-45df-5b96-b2cb-9158f9cf1e83'}, {'abstract': 'After two stunning upsets in the semifinals, what had been an uneventful men’s tournament at the United States Open will culminate in one of the most unexpected finals in recent memory: 10th-seeded Kei Nishikori vs. 14th-seeded Marin Cilic.', 'web_url': 'https://sports.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/09/08/kei-nishikori-vs-marin-cilic-u-s-open-2014-live-blog/', 'snippet': 'After two stunning upsets in the semifinals, what had been an uneventful men’s tournament at the United States Open will culminate in one of the most unexpected finals in recent memory: 10th-seeded Kei Nishikori vs. 14th-seeded Marin Cilic.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Marin Cilic, the 14th seed, became the unlikeliest winner of a men’s Grand Slam title in a decade, beating 10th-seeded Kei Nishikori in a lopsided 6-3, 6-3, 6-3 rout in the United States Open men’s singles final.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'