-09-08T20:16:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ben Rothenberg', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ben', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rothenberg', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f85e08c9-d9d8-59e4-82e6-5cd41596f97a', 'word_count': 1814, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f85e08c9-d9d8-59e4-82e6-5cd41596f97a'}, {'abstract': 'The Cassatt String Quartet’s performance at Bargemusic on Sunday was a program of works by Dvorak, Beethoven and Franck.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/09/09/arts/music/the-cassatt-string-quartet-at-bargemusic.html', 'snippet': 'The Cassatt String Quartet’s performance at Bargemusic on Sunday was a program of works by Dvorak, Beethoven and Franck.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the final movement of the Cassatt String Quartet’s performance of Dvorak’s “American” Quartet at Bargemusic on Sunday afternoon, a passing water taxi created a bow wake so strong that it sent a jolt through the barge, briefly pitching the stage forward. The violist Sarah Adams smiled as she shot a glance at her colleagues, who kept their eyes fixed on the music. In their playing, the wave did not register as so much as a hiccup.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Striving for a Delicate Balance, Even in Times of Change', 'kicker': 'Music Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Striving for a Delicate Balance, Even in Times of Change', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'da Fonseca-Wollheim, Corinna', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Bargemusic', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Classical Music', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Cassatt String Quartet', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-09-08T20:18:43+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Corinna da Fonseca-Wollheim', 'person': [{'firstname': '', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/f9e038c6-2663-5ecb-bf9f-3557adea99e0', 'word_count': 473, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f9e038c6-2663-5ecb-bf9f-3557adea99e0'}, {'abstract': 'A longtime environmental blogger and Twitter addict logs on after a year of self-imposed exile to the analog world.', 'web_url': 'https://dotearth.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/09/08/a-twitterholic-and-green-blogger-assesses-a-year-of-living-offline/', 'snippet': 'A longtime environmental blogger and Twitter addict logs on after a year of self-imposed exile to the analog world.', 'lead_paragraph': 'You may be among those, like me, who confronted a great digital emptiness over the last 12 months as Grist’s prolific environmental and political blogger and Twitter presence David Roberts went into a self-imposed offline exile. He’s back, having learned a thing or two, and he charts his 12 months of analog living in the October issue of Outside Magazine. It’s this week’s essential reading for students in my Blogging a Better Planet course at Pace University.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/08/blogs/dotroberts/dotroberts-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 443, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/09/08/blogs/dotroberts/dotroberts-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 443}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'ur