cle', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Julia Preston', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Julia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Preston', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/37864c4d-1aa6-565b-87bf-e531fb879e7c', 'word_count': 1046, 'uri': 'nyt://article/37864c4d-1aa6-565b-87bf-e531fb879e7c'}, {'abstract': 'Many employers in states that have legalized recreational or medical marijuana are penalizing workers for off-premise activity that is legal.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/09/09/opinion/obsolete-zero-tolerance-on-pot.html', 'snippet': 'Many employers in states that have legalized recreational or medical marijuana are penalizing workers for off-premise activity that is legal.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Even marijuana companies don’t want their employees stoned on the job. But in states that have legalized marijuana for recreational or medical use, many employers have retained or imposed “no tolerance” policies, penalizing workers for off-premise activity that is perfectly legal.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '28', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Obsolete Zero Tolerance on Pot', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Obsolete Zero Tolerance on Pot', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Marijuana', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Labor and Jobs', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Workplace Environment', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tests (Drug Use)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Medical Marijuana', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'States (US)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Football League', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Colorado', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-09-09T01:25:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Editorial Board', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Editorial Board'}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/dfd33951-61ce-51f0-af97-e667f21ba057', 'word_count': 452, 'uri': 'nyt://article/dfd33951-61ce-51f0-af97-e667f21ba057'}, {'abstract': 'President Obama will have to explain how the United States can avoid entanglement in another military morass if it moves more aggressively into Syria.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/09/09/opinion/confronting-the-isis-threat.html', 'snippet': 'President Obama will have to explain how the United States can avoid entanglement in another military morass if it moves more aggressively into Syria.', 'lead_paragraph': 'President Obama said on Sunday that now is the time “to start going on some offense” against the Islamic State in Iraq and Syria, or ISIS, the Sunni extremist group that is overrunning northern Iraq and has a stronghold in Syria. That would be a serious escalation of the American role there.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '28', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Confronting the ISIS Threat', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Confronting the ISIS Threat', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Islamic State in Iraq and Syria (ISIS)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Syria', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Iraq', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Defense and M