rporation', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-09-10T11:00:39+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'byline': {'original': 'By Quentin Hardy', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Quentin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hardy', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/cf511e0a-4c45-504e-afd8-e24375acb114', 'word_count': 733, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cf511e0a-4c45-504e-afd8-e24375acb114'}, {'abstract': 'Janska’s owner said she would hire at least 10 more qualified sewers right now if she could find them.', 'web_url': 'https://boss.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/09/10/a-small-clothing-maker-tries-to-keep-sewing-in-the-u-s/', 'snippet': 'Janska’s owner said she would hire at least 10 more qualified sewers right now if she could find them.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Earlier this year, Jan Erickson stood on the floor of her small sewing factory in Colorado Springs and watched as a Spanish-speaking management consultant persuaded her sewers to move their work stations\xa0— normally spread out in straight rows, like desks in a classroom\xa0— into what’s known as a cellular layout.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Small Clothing Maker Tries to Keep Sewing in the U.S.', 'kicker': 'You&#039;re the Boss', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Factories and Manufacturing', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fashion and Apparel', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sewing', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Small Business', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-09-10T11:00:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Entrepreneurship', 'byline': {'original': 'By Colleen DeBaise', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Colleen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'DeBaise', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9ce69be2-fc85-5240-8f1e-5a289f8ae53b', 'word_count': 930, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9ce69be2-fc85-5240-8f1e-5a289f8ae53b'}, {'abstract': 'The persistence of the “death panels” myth shows that the issue is vulnerable to exploitation by a demagogue.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/09/11/upshot/can-we-have-a-fact-based-conversation-about-end-of-life-planning.html', 'snippet': 'The persistence of the “death panels” myth shows that the issue is vulnerable to exploitation by a demagogue.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Dealing with health care needs at the end of life is a difficult but unavoidable issue in an aging society with rising health care costs like ours. After a failed attempt to deal with the issue as part of the Affordable Care Act, it may again be returning to the policy agenda. Can we avoid another catastrophic bout of misinformation?', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/10/upshot/10UP-PANELS/10UP-PANELS-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/09/10/upshot/10UP-PANELS/10UP-PANELS-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/10/upshot/10UP-PANELS/10UP-PANELS-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/10/upshot/10UP-PANELS/10UP-PANELS-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name