 '_id': 'nyt://article/32c8d201-5cdf-54d4-b4b5-6851ea2f8116', 'word_count': 510, 'uri': 'nyt://article/32c8d201-5cdf-54d4-b4b5-6851ea2f8116'}, {'abstract': 'Highlights from the International Herald Tribune archives: A grisly discovery has London speculating about Jack the Ripper in 1889.', 'web_url': 'https://iht-retrospective.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/09/10/1889-another-whitechapel-mystery/', 'snippet': 'Highlights from the International Herald Tribune archives: A grisly discovery has London speculating about Jack the Ripper in 1889.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LONDON — London in general and Whitechapel in particular were thrown into a feverish state of excitement this morning [Sept. 10] by the news that Jack the Ripper had murdered and mutilated his ninth victim. Both murder and mutilation were reported to be, and indeed proved to be, more horrible than in any one of the eight cases preceding. The quick and close review of facts by the police department led to the conclusion late this afternoon that the remains found did not represent Jack the Ripper’s handiwork, and this may or may not be true. — The New York Herald, European Edition, Sept. 11, 1889', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '1889: Another Whitechapel Mystery', 'kicker': 'IHT Retrospective', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Murders and Attempted Murders', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Serial Murders', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'London (England)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-09-10T14:01:51+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'byline': {'original': 'By International Herald Tribune', 'person': [{'firstname': 'International', 'middlename': 'Herald', 'lastname': 'Tribune', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5bad72e2-5d37-56b6-84a0-d7dcc8292da4', 'word_count': 106, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5bad72e2-5d37-56b6-84a0-d7dcc8292da4'}, {'abstract': 'Strains of enterovirus circulate every year. This year, the number of children affected is high, but the symptoms themselves (fever and coughing) are consistent with the way this virus has behaved in the past.', 'web_url': 'https://parenting.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/09/10/those-mysterious-deadly-virus-headlines-dont-panic-do-watch-that-cold-and-wash-your-hands/', 'snippet': 'Strains of enterovirus circulate every year. This year, the number of children affected is high, but the symptoms themselves (fever and coughing) are consistent with the way this virus has behaved in the past.', 'lead_paragraph': 'With reports of a respiratory illness that can require hospitalization and even intensive care affecting children across the Midwest and West, and breathless headlines like “Potentially Deadly Mystery Virus” and “Rare, potentially deadly ‘Enterovirus’ hits thousands of kids,” parents might be inclined to circle the wagons, or at the very least whip out the hand sanitizer. (One exaggerated report, which I won’t link to, read: “Enterovirus starts with wheezing and coughing, but within hours, kids stop breathing altogether.”)', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/10/us/motherlode-hand-washing/motherlode-hand-washing-articleLarge.png', 'height': 416, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/09/10/us/motherlode-hand-washing/motherlode-hand-washing-articleLarge.png', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 416}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/10/us/motherlode-hand-washing/motherlode-hand-washing-jumbo.png', 'height': 446, 'width': 644, 'sub