': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'France Won’t Meet Budget Deficit Target Until 2017, Government Warns', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'France Won’t Meet Budget Deficit Target Until 2017, It Warns', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'European Sovereign Debt Crisis (2010- )', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sapin, Michel', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Moscovici, Pierre', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'France', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Budgets and Budgeting', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-09-10T14:52:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'International Business', 'byline': {'original': 'By Liz Alderman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Liz', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Alderman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e5dd14d4-3db0-5344-a443-e558aaf9908f', 'word_count': 652, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e5dd14d4-3db0-5344-a443-e558aaf9908f'}, {'abstract': '“To Singapore, With Love,” by the filmmaker Tan Pin Pin, will not be shown in Singapore.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/09/10/film-about-exiles-is-banned-in-singapore/', 'snippet': '“To Singapore, With Love,” by the filmmaker Tan Pin Pin, will not be shown in Singapore.', 'lead_paragraph': 'KUALA LUMPUR, Malaysia – A documentary about the lives of Singaporean exiles has been banned in Singapore on the grounds that it undermines national security.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Film About Exiles Is Banned in Singapore', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Singapore', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-09-10T14:52:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Chen May Yee', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Chen', 'middlename': 'May', 'lastname': 'Yee', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9707507a-67a4-5626-a1ba-fa4de3db626b', 'word_count': 527, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9707507a-67a4-5626-a1ba-fa4de3db626b'}, {'abstract': 'We asked readers to send us their best photos of the September 8-9 supermoon. Here is a gallery of our favorites.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/09/10/science/space/readers-best-shots-of-the-supermoon.html', 'snippet': 'We asked readers to send us their best photos of the September 8-9 supermoon. Here is a gallery of our favorites.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Maybe you happened to have looked up into the night sky this week. And maybe you noticed that the moon looks a bit bigger and a little brighter than normal. Your eyes aren’t playing tricks on you. The third supermoon since July is visible this week. A supermoon occurs when the moon is full at its closest approach to Earth. It appears about 30 percent brighter than average.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Readers’ Best Shots of the Supermoon', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Moon', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Space and Astronomy', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Photography', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-09-10T14:53:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Science', 'section_name': 'Science', 'subsection_name': 'Space & Cosmos', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'List', '_id': 'nyt://article/1b847dc4-705a-5b72-abc6-5a9a1b2d937f', 'word_count': 101, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1b847dc4-705a-5b72-abc6-5a9a1b2d937f'}, {'abstract': 'Two new picture books have the feel of folk tales and a sweet, rustic appeal.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/09/10/books/give-and-take-and-findus-disappears.html', 'snippet': 'Two new picture books have the feel of folk tales and a sweet, rustic appeal.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The most emotionally satisfying folk tales, for me, are the ones made up from scratch by a living author. Sometimes it’s fun to play cultural anthropologist, but sometimes, a tendril of my attention will drift away upon hearing the name Baba Yaga, say, or Rumpelstiltskin. Those stories contain some timeless, universal themes —growing up and finding one’s place in the world, figuring out when someone is trying to rip you off — but they’ve also been concocted to address concerns born of a specific time and place, and not this one.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/10/books/10childrens-1/10childrens-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 300, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/09/10/books/10childrens-1/10childrens-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 300}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/10/books/10childrens-1/10childrens-1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 512, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/10/books/10childrens-1/10childrens-1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 2048, 'subType': '