ocks fit for flower children were also seen at Zimmermann, where different sheer prints were paneled together with romantic ruffles. Finally, at Vera Wang, the mostly black collection switched its tune when the model Daphne Groeneveld glided by in a pleated, floor-length chiffon gown with a micro-floral pattern.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/10/t-magazine/10trend-hippie-gilchrist/10trend-hippie-gilchrist-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/09/10/t-magazine/10trend-hippie-gilchrist/10trend-hippie-gilchrist-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/10/t-magazine/10trend-hippie-gilchrist/10trend-hippie-gilchrist-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/10/t-magazine/10trend-hippie-gilchrist/10trend-hippie-gilchrist-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/10/t-magazine/10trend-hippie-gilchrist/10trend-hippie-gilchrist-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/09/10/t-magazine/10trend-hippie-gilchrist/10trend-hippie-gilchrist-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/10/t-magazine/10trend-hippie-gilchrist/10trend-hippie-gilchrist-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'For Spring/Summer 2015, a Return to the Summer of Love', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fashion and Apparel', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hilfiger, Tommy', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wang, Vera', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-09-10T22:03:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'TStyle', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Malina Joseph Gilchrist', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Malina', 'middlename': 'Joseph', 'lastname': 'Gilchrist', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d1a4b098-48d1-58d0-b108-7a537c052f7e', 'word_count': 116, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d1a4b098-48d1-58d0-b108-7a537c052f7e'}, {'abstract': 'Mayor Bill de Blasio endorsed Kathy Hochul for lieutenant governor, despite her unusually conservative record.', 'web_url': 'https://takingnote.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/09/10/ignore-endorsements-from-politicians/', 'snippet': 'Mayor Bill de Blasio endorsed Kathy Hochul for lieutenant governor, despite her unusually conservative record.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Tim Wu, who had hoped to be lieutenant governor New York, did surprisingly well in Tuesday’s primary for a candidate with no money and little name recognition among ordinary voters. He won 40.1 percent of the vote, and 27 of the state’s 62 counties.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Ignore Endorsements From Politicians', 'kicker': 'Taking Note', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Endorsements', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'de Blasio, Bill', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wu, Timothy', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York State', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-09-10T22:05:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Firestone', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Firestone', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b3e55598-d567-5094-8724-33ed6910338b', 'word_count': 435, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b3e55598-d567-5094-8724-33ed6910338b'}, {'abstract': 'Art is a form of pollution, in Alexander Melamid’s view, but plumbing ‘is invisible and an amazing human invention.’', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/09/11/garden/who-needs-art-theres-plumbing.html', 'snippet': 'Art is a form of pollution, in Alexander Melamid’s view, but plumbing ‘is invisible and an amazing human invention.’', 'lead_paragraph': 'It tickles Alexander Melamid, a 69-year-old conceptual artist and provocateur, that his Chelsea landlord has printed his name on the building’s tenant listing board as “Melamid’s Healing Shrine.” But so much amuses Mr. Melamid, a Russian-born painter with a ready cackle and a fondness for bear hugs, whose past provocations have included deep-frying photographs of artists like Andy Warhol, Picasso and Marcel Duchamp. It has long been Mr. Melamid’s mission to poke fun at the art world. His Art Healing Ministry, an ongoing project, proposes that certain extractions and gadgets involving the emanations of Great Works — Raphael, say, or Botticelli — could be cures for a grab-bag of ailments: bulimia, acne, anxiety.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/