, Michael', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-09-10T22:44:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Home', 'section_name': 'Home & Garden', 'byline': {'original': 'By Rima Suqi', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Rima', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Suqi', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5cee1eac-7cd1-5f52-9740-ba0794c3af6a', 'word_count': 118, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5cee1eac-7cd1-5f52-9740-ba0794c3af6a'}, {'abstract': 'Roughly 10 miles from the brewery, the Dogfish Inn welcomes dogs.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/09/11/garden/beer-with-its-own-accommodations.html', 'snippet': 'Roughly 10 miles from the brewery, the Dogfish Inn welcomes dogs.', 'lead_paragraph': 'This summer, Dogfish Head, the craft brewer, opened the Dogfish Inn in the historic beach town of Lewes, Del., roughly 10 miles from the brewery. Designed by Studio Tack of Brooklyn, the rugged-good-looks hotel offers 16 rooms with wide balconies, custom-designed Woolrich blankets (handy in the off season), Tivoli radios, barware dedicated to beer drinking and artwork that pays tribute to one of the few remaining floating lighthouses in the United States (it’s docked a few hundred feet from the building). Almost needless to say, dogs are welcome, but call if yours weighs more than 80 pounds. “We get about 1,000 people a week who visit our brewery,” said Sam Calagione, an owner. “But now we get to hang out with them for 24 hours or more, rather than just an hour. It’s a natural extension of the Dogfish community.” Information: 302-644-8292 or dogfishinn.com.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/11/garden/11CURR6/11CURR6-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/09/11/garden/11CURR6/11CURR6-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/11/garden/11CURR6/11CURR6-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/11/garden/11CURR6/11CURR6-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/11/garden/11CURR6/11CURR6-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/09/11/garden/11CURR6/11CURR6-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/11/garden/11CURR6/11CURR6-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Beer With Its Own Accommodations', 'kicker': 'Currents: Rooms', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Beer With Its Own Accommodations', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Dogfish Inn (Lewes, Del)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Dogfish Head Craft Brewery', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Lewes (Del)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Studio Tack', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Interior Design and Furnishings', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hotels and Travel Lodgings', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Delaware', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-09-10T22:47:12+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Home', 'section_name': 'Home & Garden', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew Wagner', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Wagner', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/624d1dc5-297e-5dbd-a4dc-23b543373212', 'word_count': 144, 'uri': 'nyt://article/624d1dc5-297e-5dbd-a4dc-23b543373212'}, {'abstract': 'After three failed attempts to build an outdoor fireplace, the writer emerged from his ordeal warmer, wiser and ready for s’mores.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/09/11/garden/baptism-by-fire-pit.html', 'snippet': 'After three failed attempts to build an outdoor fireplace, the writer emerged from his ordeal warmer, wiser and ready for s’mores.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the 13 years we’ve owned our home, my wife and I have built three fire pits. The first was a rock ring in the woods, where we sat on boulders and invited armies of mosquitoes to mainline our blood while we pretended not to notice.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/11/garden/11PRAGMATIST/11PRAGMATIST-articleLarge-v3.jpg', 'height': 384, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/09/11/garden/11PRAGMATIST/11PRAGMATIST-articleLarge-v3.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 384}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/11/garden/11PRAGMATIST/11PRAGMATIST-jumbo-v3.jpg', 'height': 655, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/11/garden/11PRAGMATIST/11PRAGMATIST-superJumbo-v3.jpg', 'height': 1310, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/11/garden/11PRAGMATIST/11PRAGMATIST-thumbStandard-v5.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/09/11/garden/11PRAGMATIST/11PRAGMATIST-thumbStandard-v5.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/11/garden/11PRAGMATIST/11PRAGMATIST-thumbLarge-v5.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Baptism by Fire Pit', 'kicker': 'The Pragmatist', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Baptism by Fire Pit', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Tedeschi, Bob', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fireplaces', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Porches, Patios and Decks', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Landscaping', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Gardens and Gardening', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Connecticut', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Brick and Tile', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-09-10T22:47:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Home', 'section_name': 'Home & Garden', 'byline': {'original': 'By Bob Tedeschi', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Bob', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Tedeschi', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9c2d63d6-0598-588c-ad20-1b8a6f74bef0', 'word_count': 1349, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9c2d63d6-0598-588c-ad20-1b8a6f74bef0'}, {'abstract': 'Gabriel Scott’s new showroom on Broome Street offers furniture and lighting with the firm’s signature combination of metal, mold-blown glass and Italian marble.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/09/11/garden/metal-makes-a-connection-in-lighting-and-furniture.html', 'snippet': 'Gabriel Scott’s new showroom on Broome Street offers furniture and lighting with the firm’s signature combination of metal, mold-blown glass and Italian marble.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Gabriel Kakon and Scott Richler have collaborated for a decade designing furniture and lighting; in 2012 they introduced their signature collection under the name Gabriel Scott. Now the Montreal natives (and brothers-in-law) are opening a New York showroom in the Brewster Carriage House, where coaches were once made. There they will show their new Harlow lighting collection of mold-blown, gem-shaped glass elements connected by metal prongs (a pendant starts at $2,950), and Prong tables with Italian marble tops on spiky metal legs (from $2,150). Other pieces on display include the Welles, a modular light system made of suspended clusters of blackened polyhedrons with metal interiors ($750 per polyhedron). Open Monday to Friday, 10 a.m. to 6 p.m.; weekends by appointment, 372 Broome Street (Mott Street). Information: 212-837-2923 or gabriel-scott.com.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/11/garden/11CURR9/11CURR9-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 637, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/09/11/garden/11CURR9/11CURR9-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 637}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/11/garden/11CURR9/11CURR9-jumbo.jpg', 'hei