ry performance.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/09/11/new-york-today-the-city-remembers/', 'snippet': 'Thursday: a Sept. 11 guide, moody weather, and the governor reviews his primary performance.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Good morning on this cloudy Thursday.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/12/nyregion/WTC/WTC-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/09/12/nyregion/WTC/WTC-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/12/nyregion/WTC/WTC-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/12/nyregion/WTC/WTC-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/12/nyregion/WTC/WTC-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/09/12/nyregion/WTC/WTC-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/12/nyregion/WTC/WTC-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'New York Today: The City Remembers', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2014-09-11T09:51:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Annie Correal and Andy Newman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Annie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Correal', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Andy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Newman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9ddd2551-74b3-54bf-89c6-49ab139d86e3', 'word_count': 905, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9ddd2551-74b3-54bf-89c6-49ab139d86e3'}, {'abstract': 'Although polls are certainly important, they don’t tell the whole story. Here’s why not.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/09/12/upshot/polls-vs-other-stuff-in-predicting-elections.html', 'snippet': 'Although polls are certainly important, they don’t tell the whole story. Here’s why not.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It’s after Labor Day, and a batch of new polls on this year’s midterm elections is released every day. Despite this torrent of polling data, our forecasting model — which currently gives Republicans a 60 perccent chance of winning control in the Senate — stubbornly refuses to believe that polls tell the whole story.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'When Election Polls Don’t Tell the Whole Story', 'kicker': 'Pulse of the People', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Polls and Public Opinion', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Midterm Elections (2014)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-09-11T10:28:39+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Upshot', 'section_name': 'The Upshot', 'byline': {'original': 'By Josh Katz', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Josh', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Katz', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2717b34a-305d-5047-a6f8-64dc66174c26', 'word_count': 673, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2717b34a-305d-5047-a6f8-64dc66174c26'}, {'abstract': 'If you’ve made it this far, congratulations: We’ve reached the final day of fashion week.', 'web_url': 'https://runway.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/09/11/new-york-fashion-week-morning-report-day-8/', 'snippet': 'If you’ve made it this far, congratulations: We’ve reached the final day of fashion week.', 'lead_paragraph': 'If you’ve made it this far, congratulations: We’ve reached the final day of Fashion Week. Unless, of course, you’re an editor, stylist or retailer embarking on the full international circuit. In that case, gird yourself: Your flight to London leaves imminently, and the circus travels on.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'New York Fashion Week Morning Report, Day 8', 'kicker': 'On the Runway', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fashion and Apparel', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'New York Fashion Week', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Calvin Klein Inc', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Marc Jacobs International LLC', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Opening Ceremony LLC', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-09-11T10:30:32+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Styles', 'section_name': 'Fashion & Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Matthew Schneier', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Matthew', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schneier', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8bb22ce9-1931-5d25-84ac-536795adb9d6', 'word_count': 244, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8bb22ce9-1931-5d25-84ac-536795adb9d6'}, {'abstract': 'Programs to keep employees healthy are popular with their employers, but the evidence of their effectiveness is very thin.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/09/12/upshot/do-workplace-wellness-programs-work-usually-not.html', 'snippet': 'Programs to keep employees healthy are popular with their employers, but the evidence of their effectiveness is very thin.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Most news coverage of the new Kaiser Family Foundation annual survey on employer-sponsored health plans has focused on the fact that growth in premiums in 2013 was as low as it has ever been in the 16 years of the survey. But buried in the details of the report are some interesting insights into how employers think about controlling health care costs. One example is that they’re very fond of workplace wellness programs. This is surprising, because while such programs sound great, research shows they rarely work as advertised.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/12/upshot/12up-health/12up-health-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/09/12/upshot/12up-health/12up-health-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/12/upshot/12up-health/12up-health-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/12/upshot/12up-health/12up-health-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/12/upshot/12up-health/12up-health-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/09/12/upshot/12up-health/12up-health-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/12/upshot/12up-health/12up-health-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Do Workplace Wellness Programs Work? Usually Not', 'kicker': 'The New Health Care', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Health Insurance and Managed Care', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Workplace Wellness', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Kaiser Family Foundation', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Research', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-09-11T10:31:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Upshot', 'section_name': 'The Upshot', 'byline': {'original': 'By Austin Frakt and Aaron E. Carroll', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Austin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Frakt', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Aaron', 'middlename': 'E.', 'lastname': 'Carroll', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e43b1ed9-16dc-56e2-8716-766d11482267', 'word_count': 1037, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e43b1ed9-16dc-56e2-8716-766d11482267'}, {'abstract': 'A joint communiqué issued by the United States and 10 Arab states endorsed a broad strategy to fight ISIS, but none of the Arab participants said precisely how they would help.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/09/12/world/middleeast/john-kerry-saudi-arabia-isis-strategy.html', 'snippet': 'A joint communiqué issued by the United States and 10 Arab states endorsed a broad strategy to fight ISIS, but none of the Arab participants said precisely how they would help.', 'lead_paragraph': 'JIDDA, Saudi Arabia — Arab nations vowed on Thursday to “do their share” to confront and ultimately destroy the Sunni extremist group known as the Islamic State in Iraq and Syria. The promise came after the nations’ foreign ministers met here behind closed doors with Secretary of State John Kerry.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Kerry Seeks Arab Consensus in Campaign Against ISIS', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Arab Nations Vow Help ‘as Appropriate’ to Fight ISIS', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Islamic State in Iraq and Syria (ISIS)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kerry, John', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Faisal, Saud al-', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Assad, Bashar al-', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Saudi Arabia', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States International Relations', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-09-11T10:33:53+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Middle East', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael R. Gordon', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': 'R.', 'lastname': 'Gordon', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7dc6492a-b388-5873-ac1a-17908ed366f6', 'word_count': 833, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7dc6492a-b388-5873-ac1a-17908ed366f6'}, {'abstract': 'Microsoft’s acquisition of Minecraft would be an acknowledgment that games are central to many people’s lives, as players can spend months building estates and entire countries.', 'web_url': 'https://bits.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/09/11/daily-report-in-popular-games-tech-titans-see-potential-beyond-fun/', 'snippet': 'Microsoft’s acquisition of Minecraft would be an acknowledgment that games are central to many people’s lives, as players can spend months building estates and entire countries.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Parents like Minecraft because it can be a creative outlet for their children, Nick Wingfield reports. Teachers like the game because it can bring lessons to life. And Microsoft likes the game so much that it wants to buy Minecraft’s parent company for more than $2 billion.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/11/30/technology/bits-daily-report/bits-daily-report-articleLarge-v4.png', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/11/30/technology/bits-daily-report/bits-daily-report-articleLarge-v4.png', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/11/30/technology/bits-daily-report/bits-daily-report-jumbo-v6.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/11/30/technology/bits-daily-report/bits-daily-report-superJumbo-v3.png', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/11/30/technology/bits-daily-report/bits-daily-report-thumbStandard-v5.png', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/11/30/te