ers Find the Killer App', 'kicker': 'Bits', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mobile Applications', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Wearable Computing', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Apple Inc', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Google Inc', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Starwood Hotels & Resorts Worldwide Inc', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-09-11T11:00:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'byline': {'original': 'By Molly Wood', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Molly', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Wood', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/de6bdd64-6262-5bf5-9857-cb5ae9f79d97', 'word_count': 889, 'uri': 'nyt://article/de6bdd64-6262-5bf5-9857-cb5ae9f79d97'}, {'abstract': 'It hurts when a company invests time, money and resources to train new hires, and then sees them walk out the door for a few dollars. It can be a huge and demoralizing loss for the culture and the profits of a company.', 'web_url': 'https://boss.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/09/11/heres-how-to-keep-competitors-from-poaching-your-employees/', 'snippet': 'It hurts when a company invests time, money and resources to train new hires, and then sees them walk out the door for a few dollars. It can be a huge and demoralizing loss for the culture and the profits of a company.', 'lead_paragraph': 'If there were any doubt about what is the hottest and most important issue to fast-growth entrepreneurs, we now know what it is. Hands down, it is the poaching of each other’s employees.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Here’s How to Keep Competitors From Poaching Your Employees', 'kicker': 'You&#039;re the Boss', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Antitrust Laws and Competition Issues', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Entrepreneurship', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Labor and Jobs', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Small Business', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Wages and Salaries', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Atlanta (Ga)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-09-11T11:00:37+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Entrepreneurship', 'byline': {'original': 'By Cliff Oxford', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Cliff', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Oxford', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/564e28e3-328c-5fbf-b6cb-4fc84f74fafe', 'word_count': 941, 'uri': 'nyt://article/564e28e3-328c-5fbf-b6cb-4fc84f74fafe'}, {'abstract': 'From the International Herald Tribune archives: A French fugitive returned to his country to enlist in the army in 1939.', 'web_url': 'https://iht-retrospective.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/09/11/1939-convict-who-fled-returns/', 'snippet': 'From the International Herald Tribune archives: A French fugitive returned to his country to enlist in the army in 1939.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LILLE — An escaped French convict, who fled the Guiana penal colony in South America 13 years ago, returned to France today [Sept. 11] to take his place in the ranks of the French Army. An acquaintance of the fugitive named Jean Pouille recognized him as he entered barracks here to sign up. During a violent argument in 1919, the convict killed a friend with a knife for voicing unpatriotic sentiments. Condemned to Guiana for 20 years, he escaped in 1926 to Brazil. In 1930, homesick, he went to Belgium, married, and settled in Antwerp and later in Ransart. This morning he arrived in Lille from Ransart and applied for enlistment. — New York Herald Tribune, European Edition, Sept. 12, 1939', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '1939: Convict Who Fled Returns', 'kicker': 'IHT Retrospective', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Prisons and Prisoners', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'World War II (1939-45)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Lille (France)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-09-11T11:17:59+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'byline': {'original': 'By International Herald Tribune', 'person': [{'firstname': 'International', 'middlename': 'Herald', 'lastname': 'Tribune', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f97619ab-6dc7-5a28-9598-4eab2999b6c8', 'word_count': 121, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f97619ab-6dc7-5a28-9598-4eab2999b6c8'}, {'abstract': 'Cees Vermaas will join CME Europe Limited on Jan. 2 after serving as chief executive of NYSE Euronext Amsterdam since June 2010.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2014/09/11/cme-group-hires-new-head-of-london-derivatives-business/', 'snippet': 'Cees Vermaas will join CME Europe Limited on Jan. 2 after serving as chief executive of NYSE Euronext Amsterdam since June 2010.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LONDON – The CME Group said on Thursday that it had hired the former head of NYSE Euronext’s operations in Amsterdam as the chief executive of its London derivatives business, which had its debut this year.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'CME Group Names Head of London Derivatives Business', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'New Chief', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Appointments and Executive Changes', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Derivatives (Financial Instruments)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Stocks and Bonds', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'BGC Partners Inc', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Chicago Board of Trade', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Chicago Mercantile Exchange', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'CME Group Inc', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'GFI Group Inc', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'IntercontinentalExchange Inc', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Mercantile Exchange', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Stock Exchange', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'NYSE Euronext', 'rank': 12, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Europe', 'rank': 13, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'London (England)', 'rank': 14, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-09-11T11:23:39+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Chad Bray', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Chad', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bray', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0ae0fb63-6cc6-5d2c-9666-136631019a47', 'word_count': 362, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0ae0fb63-6cc6-5d2c-9666-136631019a47'}, {'abstract': 'Alibaba brings luxury to China’s middle class. | Dollar store tug of war gets contentious. | Detroit’s bankruptcy deal hinges on two banks. | Fed slow to pop bubbles.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2014/09/11/morning-agenda-alibabas-key-to-success/', 'snippet': 'Alibaba brings luxury to China’s middle class. | Dollar store tug of war gets contentious. | Detroit’s bankruptcy deal hinges on two banks. | Fed slow to pop bubbles.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As the Alibaba Group prepares to go public, the Chinese company remains committed to making global e-commerce available to China’s expanding middle class, DealBook’s David Gelles writes. “The degree to which Alibaba can deliver on this promise will help determine how much the company is ultimately worth and to what extent it can open up the enormous Chinese market to both global retailers and small businesses in search of growth,” he writes.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Morning Agenda: Alibaba’s Key to Success', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2014-09-11T11:48:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sydney Ember', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sydney', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ember', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c3e281be-89a6-51a9-a1ed-fd46539f9414', 'word_count': 2148, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c3e281be-89a6-51a9-a1ed-fd46539f9414'}, {'abstract': 'Pyongyang said the 52-year-old man had entered the North through an unidentified third country to escape his economic difficulties in the capitalist South.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/09/12/world/asia/north-korea-returns-a-defector-to-the-south.html', 'snippet': 'Pyongyang said the 52-year-old man had entered the North through an unidentified third country to escape his economic difficulties in the capitalist South.', 'lead_paragraph': 'SEOUL, South Korea — North Korea returned a South Korean man on Thursday who had defected to the North, in the first such repatriation since October.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'North Korea Returns a Defector to the South', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'North Korea: Defector Is Sent Home', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'North Korea', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'South Korea', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Defectors (Political)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kim Sang-geun', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-09-11T11:50:44+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Choe Sang-Hun', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Choe', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sang-Hun', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7c08416c-e21a-54fa-87d6-acc76e212eec', 'word_count': 441, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7c08416c-e21a-54fa-87d6-acc76e212eec'}, {'abstract': 'In “The Drop,” based on a short story by Dennis Lehane, a decade-old unsolved murder casts a shadow on a Brooklyn bar.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/09/12/movies/the-drop-unfolds-in-a-brooklyn-underworld.html', 'snippet': 'In “The Drop,” based on a short story by Dennis Lehane, a decade-old unsolved murder casts a shadow on a Brooklyn bar.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Bob Saginowski, the hero and sometime voice-over narrator of “The Drop,” is a Brooklyn bartender. Not the currently fashionable kind — a liberal arts graduate with an armful of tattoos and a carefully cultivated beard who plies artisanal bitters and obscure craft beers — but a more traditional variety. He pours shots and brews to working stiffs in Giants jackets at a roughed-up establishment in a hipster-free, ungentrified and somewhat mythical stretch of the borough. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/11/arts/drop/drop-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 248, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/09/11/arts/drop/drop-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 248}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/11/arts/drop/drop-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 422, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/11/arts/drop/drop-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 845, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/11/arts/drop/drop-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/09/11/arts/drop/drop-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/11/arts/drop/drop-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Quiet Bartender’s Secret', 'kicker': 'Movie Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Quiet Bartender’s Secret', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'ke