/article/1dbb8bd7-7270-5f89-923d-822a828fdd5b', 'word_count': 1150, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1dbb8bd7-7270-5f89-923d-822a828fdd5b'}, {'abstract': 'The Abstract Expressionists Lee Krasner and Norman Lewis, not quite in the “in”crowd, have a dialogue of sorts in a show pairing them at the Jewish Museum.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/09/12/arts/design/lee-krasner-and-norman-lewis-at-the-jewish-museum.html', 'snippet': 'The Abstract Expressionists Lee Krasner and Norman Lewis, not quite in the “in”crowd, have a dialogue of sorts in a show pairing them at the Jewish Museum.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Critics think they have the last word, but sometimes art keeps talking. In 2008, while organizing the Jewish Museum’s boisterous survey of Abstract Expressionism, “Action/Abstraction: Pollock, de Kooning and American Art, 1940-1976,” the curator, Norman L. Kleeblatt, noticed that two paintings — Lee Krasner’s “Untitled” (1948) and Norman Lewis’s “Twilight Sounds” (1947) — seemed to be speaking to each other. He had the good sense to listen and, later, to orchestrate a deeper conversation. The result is “From the Margins: Lee Krasner and Norman Lewis, 1945-1952,” a nuanced, sensitive and profound exhibition.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '30', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/12/arts/KRASNER1/KRASNER1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 291, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/09/12/arts/KRASNER1/KRASNER1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 291}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/12/arts/KRASNER1/KRASNER1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 497, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/