, 'middlename': 'O.', 'lastname': 'Scott', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/5591328b-97c7-57f7-bb9a-2f80e4bcfb01', 'word_count': 738, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5591328b-97c7-57f7-bb9a-2f80e4bcfb01'}, {'abstract': 'Salad Pizza: If you’ve got a ball of whole-wheat pizza dough, you can get dinner on the table faster than you could get it delivered.', 'web_url': 'https://parenting.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/09/11/thursday-dinner-faster-than-delivery/', 'snippet': 'Salad Pizza: If you’ve got a ball of whole-wheat pizza dough, you can get dinner on the table faster than you could get it delivered.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Busy parents. Active kids. Families that arrive home every evening, starved. This week, in the series Dinner: The Boot Camp, Jenny Rosenstrach takes a recipe for chaos and coaches one family, and all of us, in the planning and preparation needed to turn it into a pleasant nightly meal. Find all the Boot Camp recipes here.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/11/us/motherlode-salad-pizza/motherlode-salad-pizza-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/09/11/us/motherlode-salad-pizza/motherlode-salad-pizza-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/11/us/motherlode-salad-pizza/motherlode-salad-pizza-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/11/us/motherlode-salad-pizza/motherlode-salad-pizza-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/11/us/motherlode-salad-pizza/motherlode-salad-pizza-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/09/11/us/motherlode-salad-pizza/motherlode-salad-pizza-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/11/us/motherlode-salad-pizza/motherlode-salad-pizza-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Thursday Dinner, Faster Than Delivery', 'kicker': 'Motherlode', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cooking and Cookbooks', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Parenting', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-09-11T15:25:19+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Styles', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jenny Rosenstrach', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jenny', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rosenstrach', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/672b7edd-6955-53da-a3a1-e5bc4eaf1f63', 'word_count': 729, 'uri': 'nyt://article/672b7edd-6955-53da-a3a1-e5bc4eaf1f63'}, {'abstract': 'From the International Herald Tribune archives: South Rhodesia’s independence was put off in 1964.', 'web_url': 'https://iht-retrospective.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/09/11/1964-independence-threat-dropped/', 'snippet': 'From the International Herald Tribune archives: South Rhodesia’s independence was put off in 1964.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LONDON — Southern Rhodesia’s threat to defy the British government and declare itself independent “has been chucked out the window for the time being,” Rhodesian Prime Minister Ian Smith said today [Sept. 11]. But he is hopeful, he said, that Britain will grant independence before the end of the year. After a week of talks here, Mr. Smith and British Prime Minister Sir Alec Douglas-Home agreed in a communiqué that independence should come only when a majority of Rhodesians — black and white — show their approval of the present constitution. — New York Herald Tribune, European Edition, Sept. 12, 1964', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/12/blogs/12iht-oldsept12/12iht-oldsept12-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 326, 'width': 250, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/12/blogs/12iht-oldsept12/12iht-oldsept12-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 326, 'width': 250, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/12/blogs/12iht-oldsept12/12iht-oldsept12-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/09/12/blogs/12iht-oldsept12/12iht-oldsept12-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/12/blogs/12iht-oldsept12/12iht-oldsept12-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '1964: Independence Threat Dropped', 'kicker': 'IHT Retrospective', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Smith, Ian', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Great Britain', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Rhodesia', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-09-11T15:27:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'byline': {'original': 'By International Herald Tribune', 'person': [{'firstname': 'International', 'middlename': 'Herald', 'lastname': 'Tribune', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/89e0e26a-29f4-59ea-8da4-32f665091942', 'word_count': 101, 'uri': 'nyt://article/89e0e26a-29f4-59ea-8da4-32f665091942'}, {'abstract': 'Scotland will soon vote to remain within Britain or become a country of its own. Why did the independence campaign stay away from the fundamental question of Scottish national identity?', 'web_url': 'https://op-talk.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/09/11/what-does-it-mean-to-be-scottish/', 'snippet': 'Scotland will soon vote to remain within Britain or become a country of its own. Why did the independence campaign stay away from the fundamental question of Scottish national identity?', 'lead_paragraph': 'Next week, the people of Scotland will decide whether they want to remain with\xa0Britain\xa0or become an independent state. Until recently, polls steadily showed that the “unionist” option was winning over the independence one, but a poll released Sunday showed 51 percent favoring independence and 49 percent keeping the status quo —\xa0results that came as a surprise in the British Isles.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What Does It Mean to Be Scottish?', 'kicker': 'Op-Talk', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Polls and Public Opinion', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Writing and Writers', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gibson, Mel', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wallace, William', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Scottish National Party', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'England', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Great Britain', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Scotland', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-09-11T15:32:57+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Hanna Kozlowska', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Hanna', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kozlowska', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/de7162af-1ce3-581a-b401-a3e521533576', 'word_count': 1013, 'uri': 'nyt://article/de7162af-1ce3-581a-b401-a3e521533576'}, {'abstract': 'Anne Gole had a plan, and saved for it: to live in an apartment on the East Side.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/09/14/realestate/how-to-get-to-manhattan-save-save-save.html', 'snippet': 'Anne Gole had a plan, and saved for it: to live in an apartment on the East Side.', 'lead_paragraph': 'After her 2011 graduation from Binghamton University in upstate New York, Anne Gole returned home to Tenafly, N.J.', 'print_section': 'RE', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/14/realestate/14HUNT-MAIN/14HUNT-MAIN-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/09/14/realestate/14HUNT-MAIN/14HUNT-MAIN-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images