39:56+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dorothy J. Samuels', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dorothy', 'middlename': 'J.', 'lastname': 'Samuels', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2058e7d4-6553-5c95-8add-083739cd9a4f', 'word_count': 219, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2058e7d4-6553-5c95-8add-083739cd9a4f'}, {'abstract': 'Ernest Cole’s photographs capture what it was like to be black in apartheid South Africa.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/09/12/arts/design/what-ernest-coles-hidden-camera-revealed.html', 'snippet': 'Ernest Cole’s photographs capture what it was like to be black in apartheid South Africa.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Artists will stop making art about race when racism ends. I won’t be here when that happens, and you won’t either. But in the early 1960s, a brash, South African photographer in his 20s named Ernest Cole thought he just might be, once his pictures of his racially sundered homeland got out into the world. By the time he died in New York, homeless and street-stunned, some two decades later, he probably knew better.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '23', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Capturing Apartheid’s Daily Indignity', 'kicker': 'Art Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Capturing Apartheid’s Daily Indignity', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cotter, Holland', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Photography', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cole, Ernest', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Apartheid (Policy)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Race and Ethnicity', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'South Africa', 'rank': 6, 'major