ion Australian chardonnays to a group of American wine fanatics and one result might be a resounding silence punctuated by feigned snoring. I know what they’re thinking and I understand completely. But it’s time to take another look at Australian chardonnays, and at Aussie wines in general.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/17/dining/WINE/WINE-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 656, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/09/17/dining/WINE/WINE-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 656}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/17/dining/WINE/WINE-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 937, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/17/dining/WINE/WINE-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1873, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/17/dining/WINE/WINE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/09/17/dining/WINE/WINE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/17/dining/WINE/WINE-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Slow Flow From Down Under', 'kicker': 'Wines of The Times', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Slow Flow From Down Under', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Wines', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Asimov, Eric', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Australia', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Chardonnay (Wine)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-09-11T21:26:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Dining', 'section_name': 'Food', 'byline': {'original': 'By Eric Asimov', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Eric', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Asimov', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/786040aa-2e79-5ab6-b19b-c11a22193f75', 'word_count': 977, 'uri': 'nyt://article/786040aa-2e79-5ab6-b19b-c11a22193f75'}, {'abstract': 'The main attraction in this exhibition is a spectacular life-size diorama representing an airport security gateway.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/09/12/arts/design/roxy-paine-denuded-lens.html', 'snippet': 'The main attraction in this exhibition is a spectacular life-size diorama representing an airport security gateway.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Roxy Paine is known for technically ambitious sculptures, like trees made of stainless-steel tubes, and computer-controlled machines that produce globby plastic sculptures. Lately he has taken to woodworking, creating realistic objects that look as if they had been transformed by a Midas with a wooden touch.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '27', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Roxy Paine: ‘Denuded Lens’', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Art in Review', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Paine, Roxy', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-09-11T21:27:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Art & Design', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ken Johnson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ken', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Johnson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/2ad4bcf4-6186-5f3f-be28-8fedeeb0ae24', 'word_count': 251, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2ad4bcf4-6186-5f3f-be28-8fedeeb0ae24'}, {'abstract': 'Ms. Shettar has always found formal sources for her abstract work in the craft traditions of Karnataka, India.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/09/12/arts/design/ranjani-shettar-night-skies-and-daydreams.html', 'snippet': 'Ms. Shettar has always found formal sources for her abstract work in the craft traditions of Karnataka, India.', 'lead_paragraph': 'One of Ranjani Shettar’s diaphanous, constellation-like sculptures of hand-molded wax beads and cotton thread, installed at the entrance to “On Line: Drawing Through the Twentieth Century,” at the Museum of Modern Art, made a stellar introduction to that 2010 show. A new, similar piece, called “Tuntoroo,” fills a room at Talwar Gallery. And here, in a way that wasn’t obvious at MoMA, light plays a big role in the work, as patterns of shadows cast on the wall compound its intricacy and emphasize its apparent fragility.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '27', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/12/arts/12SHETTAR-GALLERIE3/12SHETTAR-GALLERIE3-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 430, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/09/12/arts/12SHETTAR-GALLERIE3/12SHETTAR-GALLERIE3-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 430}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/12/arts/12SHETTAR-GALLERIE3/12SHETTAR-GALLERIE3-jumbo.jpg', 'he