of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9b9b77a0-cf51-54ff-9d3b-908d0ad9f521', 'word_count': 271, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9b9b77a0-cf51-54ff-9d3b-908d0ad9f521'}, {'abstract': 'From the International Herald Tribune archives: American broadcasters debated ways to present news of the war in Europe in 1939.', 'web_url': 'https://iht-retrospective.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/09/12/1939-radio-station-charged/', 'snippet': 'From the International Herald Tribune archives: American broadcasters debated ways to present news of the war in Europe in 1939.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WASHINGTON — Charging that New York radio station WMCA has been intercepting secret government military messages from London and Berlin and rebroadcasting them as news, the Federal Communications Commission demanded today [Sept. 12] that the station show cause why its license should not be suspended. The investigation is the first case in which the commission has used its authority to deal with an offender under the terms of the neutrality act. Meanwhile, after an all-day session, representatives of the three major American broadcasting chains announced that they had agreed to present war news broadcasts so as to avoid “undue horror and suspense and will try to be temperate, responsible and mature in selecting material and the manner in which the facts are made public.” As far as possible, the chains said they would attempt to have the European broadcast conducted by Americans to insure the greatest degree of impartiality. — New York Herald Tribune, European Edition, Sept. 13, 1939 ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '1939: Radio Station Charged', 'kicker': 'IHT Retrospective', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Radio', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'World War II (1939-45)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Federal Communications Commission', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'WMCA-AM', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-09-12T11:19:32+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'byline': {'original': 'By International Herald Tribune', 'person': [{'firstname': 'International', 'middlename': 'Herald', 'lastname': 'Tribune', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2b25fb99-ac6a-5416-a123-bcaa7b4d83dd', 'word_count': 160, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2b25fb99-ac6a-5416-a123-bcaa7b4d83dd'}, {'abstract': 'Banks eager to work with Apple on new mobile payment system. | Signs rattle subprime lending industry. | Bankers fear Britain’s exit from E.U. | Hertz and Icahn make peace.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2014/09/12/morning-agenda-banks-bow-to-apple-pay/', 'snippet': 'Banks eager to work with Apple on new mobile payment system. | Signs rattle subprime lending industry. | Bankers fear Britain’s exit from E.U. | Hertz and Icahn make peace.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BANKS BOW TO APPLE PAY | As Apple looks to assume a more central role in the financial universe with its new payment system, Apple Pay, the eagerness of banks and credit card companies to work with the technology giant underscores both its influence and the recognition among financial institutions that they face bigger challenges from upstart technology ventures, DealBook’s Nathaniel Popper writes. Apple Pay could threaten some revenue streams, but the banking industry appears willing to invest in programs like Apple’s that still respect the traditional financial system.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Morning Agenda: Banks Bow to Apple Pay', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2014-09-12T11:20:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sydney Ember', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sydney', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ember', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c3c706c0-e79c-5556-9ec7-13e73468266c', 'word_count': 2234, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c3c706c0-e79c-5556-9ec7-13e73468266c'}, {'abstract': 'A slideshow of arts events taking place across the world this coming week.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/09/12/international-arts-events-happening-in-the-week-ahead-8/', 'snippet': 'A slideshow of arts events taking place across the world this coming week.', 'lead_paragraph': '', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'International Arts Events Happening in the Week Ahead', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2014-09-12T11:36:58+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Christopher D. Shea', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Christopher', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3eae01f7-4288-51a7-ba58-6fa970b8b13d', 'word_count': 1, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3eae01f7-4288-51a7-ba58-6fa970b8b13d'}, {'abstract': 'Fosun International, a giant Chinese financial and industrial conglomerate, and its French partner, Ardian, increased their bid to about $1.1 billion to counter an Italian group’s offer.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2014/09/12/battle-for-club-med-heats-up-as-chinese-and-french-investors-increase-bid/', 'snippet': 'Fosun International, a giant Chinese financial and industrial conglomerate, and its French partner, Ardian, increased their bid to about $1.1 billion to counter an Italian group’s offer.', 'lead_paragraph': 'PARIS — The battle for Club Méditerranée took a sharp last-minute turn on Friday when the Chinese and French investors who had originally sought to buy the vacation resort operator came back with an improved counteroffer just before the deadline for action.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/12/business/dealbook/dbpix-clubmed/dbpix-clubmed-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 450, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/09/12/business/dealbook/dbpix-clubmed/dbpix-clubmed-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 450}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/12/business/dealbook/dbpix-clubmed/dbpix-clubmed-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 768, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/12/business/dealbook/dbpix-clubmed/dbpix-clubmed-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1536, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/12/business/dealbook/dbpix-clubmed/dbpix-clubmed-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/09/12/business/dealbook/dbpix-clubmed/dbpix-clubmed-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/12/business/dealbook/dbpix-clubmed/dbpix-clubmed-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'wid