'headline': {'main': 'Reply All: The 8.31.14 Issue', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Reply All the 8.31.14 Issue', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2014-09-12T14:20:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/fd09655e-5fc7-5385-9a5c-726592c694b0', 'word_count': 654, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fd09655e-5fc7-5385-9a5c-726592c694b0'}, {'abstract': 'The 26-year-old musician made a record. Then she threw it out and started over.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/09/14/magazine/why-everyone-should-be-waiting-for-grimess-next-album.html', 'snippet': 'The 26-year-old musician made a record. Then she threw it out and started over.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Earlier this year, Claire Boucher, the 26-year-old Canadian musician known as Grimes, briefly dabbled in the world of Billboard-chart pop. She wrote a song for Rihanna. After that, she went to a group songwriting boot camp, as she entertained the idea of receding from the intensifying spotlight she’d earned as an electro-pop phenom. “I thought, Oh, I don’t want to be a front person,” she says. “I want to write.” ', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '48', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/14/magazine/14grimes/mag-14Grimes-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/09/14/magazine/14grimes/mag-14Grimes-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/14/magazine/14grimes/mag-14Grimes-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/14/magazine/14grimes/mag-14Grimes-t_CA0-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/14/magazine/14grimes/mag-14Grimes-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/09/14/magazine/14grimes/mag-14Grimes-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/14/magazine/14grimes/mag-14Grimes-t_CA0-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Why Everyone Should Be Waiting for Grimes’s Next Album', 'kicker': 'The Culture Issue', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Grimes', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Battan, Carrie', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Boucher, Claire', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-09-12T14:20:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Carrie Battan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Carrie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Battan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3e885c39-7802-5a5d-9428-6cfff769a731', 'word_count': 433, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3e885c39-7802-5a5d-9428-6cfff769a731'}, {'abstract': 'After three decades of making kindly supporting characters memorable, she finally gets her star turn.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/09/14/magazine/viola-davis.html', 'snippet': 'After three decades of making kindly supporting characters memorable, she finally gets her star turn.', 'lead_paragraph': '“Even when I get the fried-chicken special of the day, I have to dig into it like it’s filet mignon,” Viola Davis said. She was speaking not of meals, but of roles. During her 30-year career as an actress, Davis has played a crack-addicted mother (“Antwone Fisher”), the mother of an abducted child (“Prisoners”) and the mother of James Brown (“Get On Up”). Her characters often serve to “hold up the wall” of the narrative, she said, like the empathetic best friend in “Eat, Pray, Love” or the kindly stranger in “Extremely Loud and Incredibly Close.” Or the kindly mental-institution psychiatrist in “It’s Kind of a Funny Story,” the kindly rape-treatment counselor in “Trust” or the kindly medium in “Beautiful Creatures.”', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '44', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/14/magazine/14davis1/14davis1-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 597, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/09/14/magazine/14davis1/14davis1-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 597}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/14/magazine/14davis1/14davis1-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1019, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/14/magazine/14davis1/14davis1-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 2037, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/14/magazine/14davis1/14davis1-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/09/14/magazine/14davis1/14davis1-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/14/magazine/14davis1/14davis1-thumbLarge-v3.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Viola Davis as You’ve Never Seen Her Before: Leading Lady!', 'kicker': 'The Culture Issue', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'At Last', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Davis, Viola', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'How to Get Away With Murder (TV Program)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ACTORS AND ACTRESSES', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Race and Ethnicity', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Blacks', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Black People', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-09-12T14:20:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Amy Wallace', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Amy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Wallace', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/60f930b1-6799-5425-968c-2e705c22bd90', 'word_count': 3003, 'uri': 'nyt://article/60f930b1-6799-5425-968c-2e705c22bd90'}, {'abstract': 'Shayne Oliver’s flights of fancy.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/09/14/magazine/hood-by-airs-collective-fashions.html', 'snippet': 'Shayne Oliver’s flights of fancy.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Shayne Oliver began the clothing line Hood by Air in late 2006 with almost no formal training. He and his collaborators — assembled largely from the downtown night life circuit — communicated their visions to fabricators via visual aid. “We’d go around with huge bags of clothes that had details we liked: ‘This stitch needs to look like this stitch,’ ” Oliver says, “because we didn’t know the technical terms.” Some ideas were hard to convey: “At one point, we wanted to make men look like schoolgirls, and we were explaining a piece to a seamstress. ‘It’s a schoolgirl basketball short.’ She was like, ‘That makes no sense.’ So we got a schoolgirl skirt and made a new pattern out of it, to show her it was feasible.” ', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '42', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/14/magazine/14hood/mag-14Hood-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/09/14/magazine/14hood/mag-14Hood-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/14/magazine/14hood/mag-14Hood-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/14/magazine/14hood/mag-14Hood-t_CA0-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/14/magazine/14hood/mag-14Hood-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/09/14/magazine/14hood/mag-14Hood-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/14/magazine/14hood/mag-14Hood-t_CA0-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Hood by Air’s Collective Fashions', 'kicker': 'The Culture Issue', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Hood by Air', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fashion and Apparel', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Weiner, Jonah', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Hood by Air', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Oliver, Shayne', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-09-12T14:20:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jonah Weiner', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jonah', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Weiner', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c324991c-c95d-5169-8a3d-004452d21ae9', 'word_count': 414, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c324991c-c95d-5169-8a3d-004452d21ae9'}, {'abstract': 'Does asking a driver to “hurry” implicate the passenger in a speeding ticket?', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/09/14/magazine/paying-for-a-need-for-speed.html', 'snippet': 'Does asking a driver to “hurry” implicate the passenger in a speeding ticket?', 'lead_paragraph': 'A friend and I spent the weekend camping. On Monday morning, we were late getting on the road. I was going to be late for a summer course and asked my friend to hurry. At some point, I noticed that we were traveling at 130 m.p.h. I did not say anything because I wanted to make it back to class. Shortly after, we were stopped for speeding. My friend thinks I should help pay for the $500 ticket. I did not tell him to drive 130 m.p.h. Do I have an obligation to help with this ticket? NAME WITHHELD, HOUSTON', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '21', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/14/magazine/14ethicist/mag-14Ethicist-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 329, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/09/14/magazine/14ethicist/mag-14Ethicist-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 329}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/14/magazine/14ethicist/mag-14Ethicist-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 561, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/14/magazine/14ethicist/mag-14Ethicist-t_CA0-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1122, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/14/magazine/14ethicist/mag-14Ethicist-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/09/14/magazine/14ethicist/mag-14Ethicist-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/14/magazine/14ethicist/mag-14Ethicist-t_CA0-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Paying for a Need for Speed', 'kicker': 'The Ethicist', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Ticket Taker', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Klosterman, Chuck', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Ethicist, The (Times Column)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'AIRLINES AND AIRPLANES', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Speed Limits and Speeding', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Drunken and Reckless Driving', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Ethics (Personal)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Roads and Traffic', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-09-12T14:20:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Chuck Klosterman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Chuck', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Klosterman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Question', '_id': 'nyt://article/1cffa8a1-3d92-516a-ab57-4d35fe5a24bd', 'word_count': 498, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1cffa8a1-3d92-516a-ab57-4d35fe5a24bd'}, {'abstract': 'The child actor on learning to have fun on set and weeping (and burping) on demand.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/09/14/magazine/quvenzhane-walliss-secrets-work-hard-play-hard-think-about-polar-bears.html', 'snippet': 'The child actor on learning to have fun on set and weeping (and burping) on demand.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Quvenzhané Wallis’s career started with a little fib: that she was 6 years old. It was the minimum age to audition for the lead role in “Beasts of the Southern Wild,” and she was just shy. It worked. She landed the part and shot the movie in a soup-swampy Louisiana bayou town called Montegut, a short drive from her family’s home. A lesser child actor might have evaporated into the film’s momentous plot, but Wallis — with her watchful gaze, physical grace and talent for banshee-level shrieking — suffered no such fate, becoming the youngest person ever nominated for an Academy Award for best actress. Now 11 and in the sixth grade, she has built up a tiny but luminous list of credits that includes “12 Years a Slave” and a remake of “Annie” due out this December, in which she plays the title character. ', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '56', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/14/magazine/14wallis/mag-14Wallis-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 401, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/09/14/magazine/14wallis/mag-14Wallis-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 401}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/14/magazine/14wallis/mag-14Wallis-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 684, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/14/magazine/14wallis/mag-14Wallis-t_CA0-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1369, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/14/magazine/14wallis/mag-14Wallis-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/09/14/magazine/14wallis/mag-14Wallis-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/14/magazine/14wallis/mag-14Wallis-t_CA0-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Quvenzhané Wallis’s Secrets: Work Hard, Play Hard, Think About Polar Bears', 'kicker': 'The Culture Issue', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Quvenzhané Wallis', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Young, Molly', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wallis, Quvenzhane', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Annie (Movie)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ACTORS AND ACTRESSES', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-09-12T14:20:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Molly Young', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Molly', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Young', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7eaaccc0-7103-5020-830f-aed450da901f', 'word_count': 424, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7eaaccc0-7103-5020-830f-aed450da901f'}, {'abstract': 'Squeegees, suction and patent wars.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/09/14/magazine/who-made-that-windshield-wiper.html', 'snippet': 'Squeegees, suction and patent wars.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the winter of 1902, an Alabama woman named Mary Anderson visited New York and was appalled by how the weather slowed down streetcars. Snow and sleet obscured the trolleys’ two-paneled windshields, forcing drivers to open both panes and peer through the gap between them. In her notebook, Anderson sketched out a solution: a squeegee wiper on the outside of the windshield, connected to a lever on the inside.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/14/magazine/14wmt/mag-14WMT-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 716, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/09/14/magazine/14wmt/mag-14WMT-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 716}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/14/magazine/14wmt/mag-14WMT-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 859, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/14/magazine/14wmt/mag-14WMT-t_CA0-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1717, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/14/magazine/14wmt/mag-14WMT-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/09/14/magazine/14wmt/mag-14WMT-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/14/magazine/14wmt/mag-14WMT-t_CA0-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Who Made That Windshield Wiper?', 'kicker': 'Innovation', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Who Made That? (Windshield Wiper)', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Slater, Dashka', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Inventions and Patents', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobiles', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Anderson, Mary (1866-1953)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Oishei, John', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-09-12T14:20:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dashka Slater', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dashka', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Slater', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/133be445-be94-5cff-96ed-f7ba72260cdd', 'word_count': 463, 'uri': 'nyt://article/133be445-be94-5cff-96ed-f7ba72260cdd'}, {'abstract': 'The creative process of a fearless choreographer.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/09/14/magazine/how-trajal-harrell-mixes-his-moves.html', 'snippet': 'The creative process of a fearless choreographer.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When the dancer and choreographer Trajal Harrell is working on new ideas, he likes to arrange his performers across “islands” — foot-and-a-half-high platforms padded with foam rubber. “I spread the islands out in different configurations, and the dancers improvise on them,” Harrell says. “All of a sudden the body is on a small white space. I can see very clearly what each dancer is doing. It’s a way to sketch. It’s an information generator.” Last month, Harrell, who divides his year between the United States and Europe, drifted amid islands in a Berlin rehearsal space. He gave the dancers directions that ranged from the blunt — “stop doing that” — to the fanciful — “move with the smell of fashion.” This session was practice for “Practice,” a not-quite-performance that Harrell was preparing to give in New York at the Museum of Modern Art, in which he would invite spectators to spy on his creative methods. “It’s a nightmare in many ways,” he says, “because I’m very private about my process.”', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '37', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/14/magazine/14harrell/mag-14Harrell-t_C