 None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2014-09-12T15:38:32+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Editors', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Editors'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c7225d66-f241-54bf-b749-9a47c32ee4c1', 'word_count': 52, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c7225d66-f241-54bf-b749-9a47c32ee4c1'}, {'abstract': 'This week, Henry Kissinger discusses “World Order”; Alexandra Alter has news from the literary world; Lawrence Wright talks about “Thirteen Days in September”; and Gregory Cowles has best-seller news. Pamela Paul is the host.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/audio/2014/09/12/books/review/14books_pod.html', 'snippet': 'This week, Henry Kissinger discusses “World Order”; Alexandra Alter has news from the literary world; Lawrence Wright talks about “Thirteen Days in September”; and Gregory Cowles has best-seller news. Pamela Paul is the host.', 'lead_paragraph': 'This week, Henry Kissinger discusses “World Order”; Alexandra Alter has news from the literary world; Lawrence Wright talks about “Thirteen Days in September”; and Gregory Cowles has best-seller news. Pamela Paul is the host.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Inside The New York Times Book Review: Henry Kissinger’s ‘World Order’', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-09-12T15:40:39+0000', 'document_type': 'audio', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://audio/019e3ea2-ce3c-52e9-8967-ac670d139be3', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://audio/019e3ea2-ce3c-52e9-8967-ac670d139be3'}, {'abstract': 'This week, Henry Kissinger and Lawrence Wright.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/audio/2014/09/12/books/review/14books_pod-blog.html', 'snippet': 'This week, Henry Kissinger and Lawrence Wright.', 'lead_paragraph': 'This week, Henry Kissinger and Lawrence Wright.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Inside The New York Times Book Review: Henry Kissinger’s ‘World Order’', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-09-12T15:41:59+0000', 'document_type': 'audio', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://audio/148864ed-1317-5997-9a62-95ddcd5d4181', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://audio/148864ed-1317-5997-9a62-95ddcd5d4181'}, {'abstract': 'The legendary founder of Nars Cosmetics explains the show’s no-makeup directive and reflects on 20 years in the business.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/09/12/francois-nars-at-marc-jacobs-interview-talking-bare-faces-70s-supermodels-and-macarons/', 'snippet': 'The legendary founder of Nars Cosmetics explains the show’s no-makeup directive and reflects on 20 years in the business.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Last night, the Marc Jacobs spring/summer 2015 show officially closed the curtain on New York Fashion Week. Backstage beforehand, the mood was decidedly mellow, as if everyone was relieved at having reached the final mile of a marathon. Classical music played, photographers casually milled about and the model Edie Campbell even chilled in a chair doing a crossword puzzle.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/12/t-magazine/12nars-molvar/12nars-molvar-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 401, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/09/12/t-magazine/12nars-molvar/12nars-molvar-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 401}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/12/t-magazine/12nars-molvar/12nars-molvar-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 684, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/12/t-magazine/12nars-molvar/12nars-molvar-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 868, 'width': 1300, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/12/t-magazine/12nars-molvar/12nars-molvar-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/09/12/t-magazine/12nars-molvar/12nars-molvar-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/12/t-magazine/12nars-molvar/12nars-molvar-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Talking Bare Faces, ’70s Supermodels and Macarons with Francois Nars at Marc Jacobs', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cosmetics and Toiletries', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'New York Fashion Week', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Nars, Francois', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Marc Jacobs International LLC', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Nars Cosmetics', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-09-12T15:44:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'TStyle', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kari Molvar', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kari', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Molvar', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2028df35-e627-55f2-b3fa-991c8bea24da', 'word_count': 567, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2028df35-e627-55f2-b3fa-991c8bea24da'}, {'abstract': 'Paperback books of particular interest.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/09/14/books/review/paperback-row.html', 'snippet': 'Paperback books of particular interest.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BLEEDING EDGE,  by Thomas Pynchon. (Penguin, $17.) The horror of Sept. 11 looms over Pynchon’s novel of New York in the vortex of the dot-com boom. When Maxine Tarnow, a fraud investigator pinballing between work and family, starts looking into a computer-security firm and its corrupt chief executive, she finds herself mixed up with an array of hackers, drug runners and entrepreneurs, some of whom begin turning up dead. Our reviewer, Jonathan Lethem, called this shaggy-dog conspiracy tale “dazzling and ludicrous.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '28', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Paperback Row', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Paperback Row', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-09-12T15:48:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ihsan Taylor', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ihsan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Taylor', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'List', '_id': 'nyt://article/9107dcb0-3ea8-5523-bb54-a4e5bdc383bf', 'word_count': 479, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9107dcb0-3ea8-5523-bb54-a4e5bdc383bf'}, {'abstract': 'Recently reviewed books of particular interest.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/09/14/books/review/editors-choice.html', 'snippet': 'Recently reviewed books of particular interest.', 'lead_paragraph': 'THE DOG,  by Joseph O’Neill. (Pantheon, $25.95.) In O’Neill’s disturbing, elegant novel, his first since “Netherland,” a lost and tormented New York lawyer recognizes more darkness within himself than in the iniquitous place he works, Dubai.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Editors’ Choice', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Editors’ Choice | Recent Books of Particular Interest', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-09-12T15:49:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'List', '_id': 'nyt://article/cb96a198-fd82-5e98-8dac-f4cd88e26cbf', 'word_count': 250, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cb96a198-fd82-5e98-8dac-f4cd88e26cbf'}, {'abstract': '“The Secret Place,” by Tana French, enters the hardcover fiction list at No. 4, just after the critic Laura Miller recently argued that women write the best crime novels.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/09/21/books/review/inside-the-list.html', 'snippet': '“The Secret Place,” by Tana French, enters the hardcover fiction list at No. 4, just after the critic Laura Miller recently argued that women write the best crime novels.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Unsolved Mysteries: Do women write the best crime novels? That’s the thesis of a recent essay in Salon by the critic Laura Miller, who argues that mysteries by men have grown sclerotic and predictable — locked into a hard-boiled formula little changed since the days of Raymond Chandler and Dashiell Hammett. Meanwhile (Miller continues), women are mixing their crime stories with other genres, mining the emotional terrains of home and family to advance a field the editor Sarah Weinman calls “domestic suspense.” One novel that features prominently in Miller’s analysis is “The Secret Place,” by Tana French, which enters the hardcover fiction list at No. 4. “A thin vein of the uncanny runs through most of French’s novels,” Miller notes: “mysteries that go unsolved, premises that flirt with impossibility, paranormal occurrences that may or may not be imagined.” I don’t know if Miller is right about men and women; who is? But she’s not wrong about French, whose books really do cultivate an appealing lack of resolution — at least in part, no doubt, because she jumps in without a plan. “When I start writing, I have the narrator, the basic premise and a load of coffee,” French told The Sunday Times of London in 2008. “I don’t even know \xad‘whodunit.’ ” Not that she would let uncertainty keep her from a genre she loves. “Crime fiction is one of the best barometers of what’s going on at any different moment,” she added. “It’s about society’s highest priorities and deepest fears.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/14/books/review/14list-img/14list-img-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 450, 'width': 453, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/14/books/review/14list-img/14list-img-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 450, 'width': 453, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/14/books/review/14list-img/14list-img-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/09/14/books/review/14list-img/14list-img-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/14/books/review/14list-img/14list-img-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Inside the List', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Inside the List', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-09-12T15:49:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Gregory Cowles', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Gregory', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cowles', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2a514e8e-0273-57e9-9014-40b530dca8dc', 'word_count': 467, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2a514e8e-0273-57e9-9014-40b530dca8dc'}, {'abstract': 'Mr. Kissinger discusses his new book, and Lawrence Wright talks about “Thirteen Days in September: Carter, Begin, and Sadat at Camp David.”', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/09/12/book-review-podcast-henry-kissingers-world-order/', 'snippet': 'Mr. Kissinger discusses his new book, and Lawrence Wright talks about “Thirteen Days in September: Carter, Begin, and Sadat at Camp David.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'In The New York Times Book Review, John Micklethwait reviews Henry Kissinger’s “World Order.” Mr. Micklethwait writes:', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'ra