, 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/207b53ec-f708-54cc-bb43-3f6ee924bb02', 'word_count': 991, 'uri': 'nyt://article/207b53ec-f708-54cc-bb43-3f6ee924bb02'}, {'abstract': 'Hercule Poirot reappears, courtesy of Sophie Hannah, years after solving his “last case.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/09/14/books/review/the-monogram-murders-sophie-hannahs-poirot-mystery.html', 'snippet': 'Hercule Poirot reappears, courtesy of Sophie Hannah, years after solving his “last case.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'Nobody would dispute the fact that Hercule Poirot, the elegant Belgian detective, he of the patent-leather shoes and the waxed mustache, is dead. Agatha Christie brought him to an end in her appropriately named novel, “Curtain: Poirot’s Last Case,” and The New York Times itself marked his death with a fictional obituary. But the demise of the hero, and of the author, no longer needs to be the end of the story. The literary executors of James Bond’s creator, Ian Fleming, have held this view for some years, and there seems to be no end to the public’s enthusiasm for rewritten versions of a whole host of literary favorites.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/14/books/review/14BKSSMITH/14BKSSMITH-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 478, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/09/14/books/review/14BKSSMITH/14BKSSMITH-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 478}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/14/books/review/14BKSSMITH/14BKSSMITH-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 816, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/14/books/review/14BKSSMITH/14BKSSMITH-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1121, 'width': 1407, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/14/books/review/14BKSSMITH/14BKSSMITH-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/09/14/books/review/14BKSSMITH/14BKSSMITH-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/14/books/review/14BKSSMITH/14BKSSMITH-thumbLarge-v3.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'True Detective', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'True Detective', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'The Monogram Murders (Book)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Christie, Agatha', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hannah, Sophie (1971- )', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-09-12T15:51:47+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alexander McCall Smith', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alexander', 'middlename': 'Mccall', 'lastname': 'Smith', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/d207ae86-fca7-5057-a381-552c911f8648', 'word_count': 907, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d207ae86-fca7-5057-a381-552c911f8648'}, {'abstract': 'In this Ian McEwan novel, a judge must decide the case of a teenage Jehovah’s Witness who refuses treatment for leukemia.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/09/14/books/review/the-children-act-by-ian-mcewan.html', 'snippet': 'In this Ian McEwan novel, a judge must decide the case of a teenage Jehovah’s Witness who refuses treatment for leukemia.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When Anne Darwin died just after her 10th birthday in 1851, her father “lost the joy of the Household.” No longer could he see “as plainly as others do, and as I should wish to do, evidence of design and beneficence on all sides of us. There seems to me too much misery in the world.” Charles Darwin’s faith was gone; he began writing “On the Origin of Species.” As Ian McEwan has told interviewers, “rather like Darwin when his favorite daughter died,” on Sept. 11 he “felt, more than ever,” that there was no God, and that “religion was distinctly unhelpful in making compassionate, reasonable judgments about people’s lives. On the whole, the secular mind seems far superior in making reasonable judgments.” In McEwan’s early novels, intelligent characters had often “slithered” along the “axis of belief and unbelief,” just as he had. Not anymore. Atheism and the secular state are under attack, and he must defend them.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/14/books/review/14friedell-SUB/14friedell-SUB-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 384, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/09/14/books/review/14friedell-SUB/14friedell-SUB-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 384}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/14/books/review/14friedell-SUB/14friedell-SUB-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 655, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/14/books/review/14friedell-SUB/14friedell-SUB-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1310, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/14/books/review/14friedell-SUB/14friedell-SUB-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/09/14/books/review/14friedell-SUB/14friedell-SUB-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/14/books/review/14friedell-SUB/14friedell-SUB-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Body’s Temple', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Body’s Temple', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'McEwan, Ian', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-09-12T15:51:58+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Deborah Friedell', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Deborah', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Friedell', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/1c780296-fdf6-5132-bbae-4d122bf18062', 'word_count': 1074, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1c780296-fdf6-5132-bbae-4d122bf18062'}, {'abstract': 'An antiquities collector disappears off the coast of Greece in 1905, and his wife discovers that his life was not what it seemed.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/09/14/books/review/de-potters-grand-tour-by-joanna-scott.html', 'snippet': 'An antiquities collector disappears off the coast of Greece in 1905, and his wife discovers that his life was not what it seemed.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The 19th century was the Golden Age of Collectors. Or, depending on your perspective, the Gilded Age of Plunderers. From Lord Elgin’s marbles to Alfred Russel Wallace’s tropical fauna (over 80,000 beetles!) to Edward Drinker Cope’s dinosaur bones to Charles Frederick Newcombe’s Northwest Coast totem poles, it was the unquestioned privilege of the enlightened world to amass the specimens and relics of the dark one, and to study, paint or display them in cabinets and museums — or sell them. The nadir of this frenzy may have occurred when Robert Peary brought six Inuits from Greenland to New York to be housed at the American Museum of Natural History, an event that most likely inspired Andrea Barrett’s novel “The Voyage of the Narwhal.” ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/14/books/review/0914-bks-VERNON/0914-bks-VERNON-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 700, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/09/14/books/review/0914-bks-VERNON/0914-bks-VERNON-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 700}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/14/books/review/0914-bks-VERNON/0914-bks-VERNON-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 879, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/14/books/review/0914-bks-VERNON/0914-bks-VERNON-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1958, 'width': 1679, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thum