'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'AL QAEDA', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Central Intelligence Agency', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lister, Tim', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-09-12T15:54:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Scott Shane', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Scott', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Shane', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/290b8edb-a169-5794-a259-edf0afcf5d41', 'word_count': 680, 'uri': 'nyt://article/290b8edb-a169-5794-a259-edf0afcf5d41'}, {'abstract': 'After a pandemic kills most of the human race, a traveling theater company roams around, searching for an audience.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/09/14/books/review/station-eleven-by-emily-st-john-mandel.html', 'snippet': 'After a pandemic kills most of the human race, a traveling theater company roams around, searching for an audience.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Emily St. John Mandel’s fourth novel, “Station Eleven,” begins with a spectacular end. One night, in a Toronto theater, onstage performing the role of King Lear, 51-year-old Arthur Leander has a fatal heart attack. There is barely time for people to absorb this shock when tragedy on a considerably vaster scale arrives in the form of a flu pandemic so lethal that, within weeks, most of the world’s population has been killed.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/14/books/review/14BKSNUNEZ/0914-bks-NUNEZ-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 675, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'cr