], 'pub_date': '2014-09-12T19:32:34+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Automobiles', 'section_name': 'Automobiles', 'byline': {'original': 'By John Pearley Huffman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': 'Pearley', 'lastname': 'Huffman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7278e9de-5a8e-5702-9589-09444006f27d', 'word_count': 1509, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7278e9de-5a8e-5702-9589-09444006f27d'}, {'abstract': 'The Wu-Tang Clan will take the stage at Barclays Center in what organizers are calling an “official reunion.”', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/09/12/wu-tang-clan-joins-a-tribute-to-the-source-in-brooklyn/', 'snippet': 'The Wu-Tang Clan will take the stage at Barclays Center in what organizers are calling an “official reunion.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Source, a music and culture magazine that has been an important voice in the development and chronicling of hip-hop, is celebrating its 25th anniversary with Source360, a concert at the Barclays Center in Brooklyn, which the magazine says will be an annual event.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Wu-Tang Clan Joins a Tribute to The Source in Brooklyn', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Barclays Center (Brooklyn, NY)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Wu-Tang Clan', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-09-12T19:33:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Allan Kozinn', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Allan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kozinn', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/86c9f216-e3d8-5e4c-8c01-1f42929e1374', 'word_count': 197, 'uri': 'nyt://article/86c9f216-e3d8-5e4c-8c01-1f42929e1374'}, {'abstract': 'The musical adaptation of Bret Easton Ellis’s novel had been scheduled to run at Second Stage Theater.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/09/12/off-broadway-run-of-american-psycho-is-dead/', 'snippet': 'The musical adaptation of Bret Easton Ellis’s novel had been scheduled to run at Second Stage Theater.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The highly anticipated Off Broadway production of the musical “American Psycho” has been canceled after the show’s commercial backers pulled the rights from Second Stage Theater, its artistic director, Carole Rothman, said on Friday. No explanation was given; the commercial backers at Act 4 Entertainment, who were teaming up with Second Stage to develop the show in hopes of an eventual transfer to Broadway, did not immediately return phone calls.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/21/arts/21BRANTLEY/21BRANTLEY-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 429, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/01/21/arts/21BRANTLEY/21BRANTLEY-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 429}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/21/arts/21BRANTLEY/21BRANTLEY-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 733, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/21/arts/21BRANTLEY/21BRANTLEY-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1465, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/21/arts/21BRANTLEY/21BRANTLEY-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/01/21/arts/21BRANTLEY/21BRANTLEY-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/21/arts/21BRANTLEY/21BRANTLEY-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Off Broadway Run of ‘American Psycho’ Is Dead', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'u2018American Psychou2019 Run Canceled', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'American Psycho (Play)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ellis, Bret Easton', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Goold, Rupert', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rothman, Carole', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sheik, Duncan', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Walker, Benjamin (1982- )', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'ALMEIDA THEATER', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Second Stage Theater', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Manhattan (NYC)', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-09-12T19:38:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Patrick Healy', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Patrick', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Healy', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ce67a24a-d0ae-517f-a409-801c8cceb0ec', 'word_count': 460, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ce67a24a-d0ae-517f-a409-801c8cceb0ec'}, {'abstract': 'The winner of the LVMH Young Fashion Designer Prize will present his spring/summer 2015 collection against a backdrop transformed by the French visual artist and photographer Georges Rousse.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/09/12/london-fashion-week-thomas-tait-spring-2015-presentation-sneak-peek/', 'snippet': 'The winner of the LVMH Young Fashion Designer Prize will present his spring/summer 2015 collection against a backdrop transformed by the French visual artist and photographer Georges Rousse.', 'lead_paragraph': '“I love a fashion show,” says the Canadian-born, London-based fashion designer Thomas Tait. “Some people question the need for them, but there is something that happens at a fashion show that can never be replicated in any other way.” With Monday’s spring/summer 2015 runway, his first since winning the LVMH Young Fashion Designer Prize in May, Tait will prove that point. Attendees will file into a derelict warehouse in central London to watch Tait’s presentation against a backdrop transformed by the French visual artist and photographer Georges Rousse. Known for his large-scale installations that seem to float in space like an optical illusion, Rousse created various graphic treatments on the walls and pillars, seen for the first time here, which spell out “déchiré” (ripped) in one room. Spoiler alert: his color palette, electric reds and midnight blues, comes from Tait’s collection.', 'print_section': 'ST', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/12/t-magazine/12tait-beddie-1/12tait-beddie-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/09/12/t-magazine/12tait-beddie-1/12tait-beddie-1-articleLarge