dening epidemic of Ebola in West Africa are falling short — too little help arriving too late. The World Health Organization estimates that as many as 20,000 people could be infected over the course of the outbreak. President Obama has called the epidemic a “national security priority” and pledged to use American military assets to help the beleaguered countries, primarily Guinea, Liberia and Sierra Leone, to bring the epidemic under control. The Agency for International Development and the Defense Department are already rushing personnel and supplies to the scene.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '20', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Sluggish Fight Against Ebola', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Sluggish Fight Against Ebola', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Ebola Virus', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'AFRICA', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Epidemics', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Humanitarian Aid', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Liberia', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Doctors Without Borders', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Sierra Leone', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Guinea', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Centers for Disease Control and Prevention', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Defense Department', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-09-13T01:27:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Editorial Board', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Editorial Board'}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/f