, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-09-13T14:30:37+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'SundayBusiness', 'section_name': 'The Upshot', 'byline': {'original': 'By Tyler Cowen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Tyler', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cowen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f911d319-1f37-50af-9197-2f1c689e2207', 'word_count': 985, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f911d319-1f37-50af-9197-2f1c689e2207'}, {'abstract': 'Polls show that the referendum on Scottish independence will be close, and people in England’s northernmost town fear that life will never be the same again — even if Scots vote to stay in the union.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/09/14/world/europe/scottish-independence-referendum-berwick-upon-tweed.html', 'snippet': 'Polls show that the referendum on Scottish independence will be close, and people in England’s northernmost town fear that life will never be the same again — even if Scots vote to stay in the union.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BERWICK-UPON-TWEED, England — This is England’s northernmost town, and it has changed hands between the Scots and the English more than a dozen times in the last 1,400 years. It hasn’t been a true border outpost since the unification of the two countries more than 300 years ago, but it may soon become one again.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Bracing for Change on Scotland’s Border, Whatever the Referendum Result', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Bracing for Change on Scotland’s Border, Whatever the Referendum Result', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Erlanger, Steven', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Scotland', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Great Britain', 'rank