5:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Science', 'section_name': 'Health', 'byline': {'original': 'By Gretchen Reynolds', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Gretchen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Reynolds', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9b00281a-a1d3-5f91-b5ac-2ff06dac6aa8', 'word_count': 362, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9b00281a-a1d3-5f91-b5ac-2ff06dac6aa8'}, {'abstract': 'Bills keep mounting for animal welfare advocates who have stepped forward to take responsibility for hundreds of dogs recently seized by the authorities in China.', 'web_url': 'https://sinosphere.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/09/15/after-dog-rescues-activists-left-with-a-daunting-task/', 'snippet': 'Bills keep mounting for animal welfare advocates who have stepped forward to take responsibility for hundreds of dogs recently seized by the authorities in China.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Every morning since late August, Li Wei has started her day by putting on rubber boots and wading through a barrel of disinfectant before entering a shed filled with dozens of dogs that would have wound up on restaurant tables were it not for rescuers like herself. She and other volunteers in Lucun, a village on the southwestern outskirts of Beijing, then get to the day’s chores: feeding the dogs, filling their water bowls, washing the floor and helping veterinarians administer rabies shots. When the sickest animals die, the volunteers\xa0carry the bodies out for burial. They can’t afford cremation, Ms. Li said.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/15/world/15sino-dogs01/15sino-dogs01-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 395, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/09/15/world/15sino-dogs01/15sino-dogs01-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlarge