https://www.nytimes.com/2014/09/16/sports/baseball/yankees-wild-card-hopes-all-but-disappear-with-loss-to-rays.html', 'snippet': 'The Yankees trail by six games in the race for the second American League wild-card spot. They now have only 13 games to make up the deficit.', 'lead_paragraph': 'ST. PETERSBURG, Fla. — It was difficult to tell where the more important Yankee game was being held Monday. There was one at night at Tropicana Field in front of an announced crowd of 16,058, and there was the one earlier in the day in Tampa at Steinbrenner Field in front of about two dozen reporters, a handful of prospects in street clothes and Jennifer Steinbrenner Swindal, the Yankees’ general partner and vice chairwoman, who watched from the stands.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/16/sports/CITY-YANKEES-2/CITY-YANKEES-2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 401, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/09/16/sports/CITY-YANKEES-2/CITY-YANKEES-2-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 401}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/16/sports/CITY-YANKEES-2/CITY-YANKEES-2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 684, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/16/sports/CITY-YANKEES-2/CITY-YANKEES-2-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1369, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/16/sports/CITY-YANKEES-2/CITY-YANKEES-2-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/09/16/sports/CITY-YANKEES-2/CITY-YANKEES-2-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/16/sports/CITY-YANKEES-2/CITY-YANKEES-2-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Masahiro Tanaka Near Returning, but Yankees’ Wild-Card Hopes Are All but Gone', 'kicker': 'Rays 1, Yankees 0', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Tanaka Near Returning, but Yankees’ Wild-Card Hopes Are All but Gone', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Baseball', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Waldstein, David', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Tanaka, Masahiro', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Capuano, Chris', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Yankees', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Tampa Bay Rays', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-09-16T03:39:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Baseball', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Waldstein', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Waldstein', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6c184d74-b467-559a-9d29-1a05806cd26c', 'word_count': 802, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6c184d74-b467-559a-9d29-1a05806cd26c'}, {'abstract': 'Originally hired by the team in 1989, Mark Newman, now the Yankees’ senior vice president for baseball operations, plans to spend more time with his family.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/09/16/sports/baseball/longtime-yankees-executive-is-retiring.html', 'snippet': 'Originally hired by the team in 1989, Mark Newman, now the Yankees’ senior vice president for baseball operations, plans to spend more time with his family.', 'lead_paragraph': 'ST. PETERSBURG, Fla. — The Yankees’ senior vice president for baseball operations, Mark Newman, who was part of player development during the glory years of the early 1990s and in later years when the fruits of the system were less obvious, is retiring after 26 years with the team.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Longtime Yankees Executive Is Retiring', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Longtime Yankees Executive Newman Is Retiring', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Baseball', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Yankees', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-09-16T03:52:47+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Baseball', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Waldstein', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Waldstein', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9ec3eab2-e74e-5056-a290-89174a9ed5fc', 'word_count': 474, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9ec3eab2-e74e-5056-a290-89174a9ed5fc'}, {'abstract': 'The quirky French product designers are the first honorees of the London Design Festival from outside the U.K.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/09/16/t-magazine/london-design-week-medalists-bouroullec-brothers-profile.html', 'snippet': 'The quirky French product designers are the first honorees of the London Design Festival from outside the U.K.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Since 2007, beginning with Zaha Hadid, the organizers of the London Design Festival have bestowed the London Design Medal on a leading local talent. This year, the prize leaps outside the U.K. for the first time — and breaks with tradition by recognizing a team instead of an individual — with the renowned product designers Ronan and Erwan Bouroullec.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/16/t-magazine/16bouroullecs-rubinstein/16bouroullecs-rubinstein-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/09/16/t-magazine/16bouroullecs-rubinstein/16bouroullecs-rubinstein-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/16/t-magazine/16bouroullecs-rubinstein/16bouroullecs-rubinstein-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/16/t-magazine/16bouroullecs-rubinstein/16bouroullecs-rubinstein-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/16/t-magazine/16bouroullecs-rubinstein/16bouroullecs-rubinstein-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/09/16/t-magazine/16bouroullecs-rubinstein/16bouroullecs-rubinstein-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/16/t-magazine/16bouroullecs-rubinstein/16bouroullecs-rubinstein-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Brave Work of the Bouroullec Brothers, This Year’s London Design Medalists', 'kicker': 'By Design', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2014-09-16T04:00:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'TDesign', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dan Rubinstein', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rubinstein', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/01567cef-c11a-54a8-8369-b5bca55a7f70', 'word_count': 420, 'uri': 'nyt://article/01567cef-c11a-54a8-8369-b5bca55a7f70'}, {'abstract': 'The first-ever design exhibition at the SoHo space spotlights an eccentric selection of functional and not-so-functional seating curated in homage to Ionesco’s play “The Chairs.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/09/16/t-magazine/swiss-institute-chairs-exhibition.html', 'snippet': 'The first-ever design exhibition at the SoHo space spotlights an eccentric selection of functional and not-so-functional seating curated in homage to Ionesco’s play “The Chairs.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'In Eugène Ionesco’s 1952 absurdist tragedy, “The Chairs,” the last two survivors of an apocalypse speak to a group of chairs that purportedly have invisible occupants. That the vacant seats are not the only witnesses to the couple’s suicide at the end of the play — the chairs in the actual audience being occupied by viewers — heightens the sense of lost promise. In those chairs lies the slightness of the distinction between useful objects and relics. “We are after the end of the world, in our culture and in our perception of stuff,” says the Greek-Norwegian curator and architect Andreas Angelidakis, who conceived of the inaugural design exhibition at the Swiss Institute as a sequel of sorts to Ionesco’s play: just chairs, no people. He credits his therapist with the reference. “These are not the key pieces of modernist design, but an eccentric selection,” Angelidakis says of the survey, “Fin de Siècle,” opening tonight. “I didn’t curate it as museum overview, but as a casting director. Each chair was picked based on character, like casting actors.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'At the Swiss Institute, a Survey of Eccentric Chair Designs', 'kicker': 'By Design', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2014-09-16T04:00:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'TDesign', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Su Wu', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Su', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Wu', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/fdd4a6c1-b44e-5da8-b582-030e45d02c02', 'word_count': 385, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fdd4a6c1-b44e-5da8-b582-030e45d02c02'}, {'abstract': 'Federal researchers found that the number of uninsured declined substantially in the first quarter of this year.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/09/16/us/number-of-americans-without-health-insurance-falls-survey-shows.html', 'snippet': 'Federal researchers found that the number of uninsured declined substantially in the first quarter of this year.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Federal researchers reported on Tuesday that the number of Americans without health insurance had declined substantially in the first quarter of this year, the first federal measure of the number of uninsured Americans since the Affordable Care Act extended coverage to millions of people in January.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Number of Americans Without Health Insurance Falls, Survey Shows', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Number of Ame