Inmates', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Eckholm, Erik', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Prisons and Prisoners', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Population', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Justice Department', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'United States', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-09-16T14:02:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Erik Eckholm', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Erik', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Eckholm', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/cb8ab230-2cee-5db0-a1b6-64dc0ccb509b', 'word_count': 644, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cb8ab230-2cee-5db0-a1b6-64dc0ccb509b'}, {'abstract': 'Highlights from the International Herald Tribune archives: The war invades women’s fashion in 1939.', 'web_url': 'https://iht-retrospective.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/09/16/1939-women-adopt-military-style/', 'snippet': 'Highlights from the International Herald Tribune archives: The war invades women’s fashion in 1939.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LONDON — Fashionable London women who have joined auxiliary war-time services today [Sept. 16] were reported to be abandoning the expensively-cultivated coiffure for a boyish shingle in order to wear regulation service caps. At the same time West End manicurists reported that fingernails are being cut shorter with rounded tips. Service regulations require that no curls show and ban the use of nail polish. Many shops are featuring a ‘‘hair harness’’ to keep softly waved coiffures under control during work hours, but allowing a fully feminine appearance when the wearers are off duty. — New York Herald Tribune, European Edition, Sept. 17, 1939', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multim