icle/fa4102e4-21ab-52cc-a3d5-af8c683bd4b5'}, {'abstract': 'Military and police planes began airlifting tourists from the resort town of Los Cabos on Tuesday as residents began to tally the wreckage left by Hurricane Odile.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/09/17/world/americas/mexico-tourists-being-airlifted-after-hurricane-odile.html', 'snippet': 'Military and police planes began airlifting tourists from the resort town of Los Cabos on Tuesday as residents began to tally the wreckage left by Hurricane Odile.', 'lead_paragraph': ' Military and police planes began airlifting tourists from the resort town of Los Cabos on Tuesday as residents began to tally the wreckage left by Hurricane Odile, the strongest storm to hit the Baja California peninsula in almost 50 years. The Category 3 storm smashed luxury hotels and swept away poor neighborhoods, but officials said there were no deaths. Electricity and phone service were cut off across the state of Baja California Sur, and the Los Cabos airport was closed to commercial flights. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Mexico: Tourists Being Airlifted After Hurricane Odile', 'kicker': 'World Briefing', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Mexico: Tourists Being Airlifted After Hurricane Odile', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hurricanes and Tropical Storms', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Los Cabos (Mexico)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-09-17T04:17:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Americas', 'byline': {'original': 'By Elisabeth Malkin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Elisabeth', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Malkin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/da081cec-81fa-5bf8-96e5-4cd5ba97f57f', 'word_count': 83, 'uri': 'nyt://article/da081cec-81fa-5bf8-96e5-4cd5ba97f57f'}, {'abstract': 'An American was arrested Tuesday night on a river that borders North Korea, the South Korean news media reported on Wednesday.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/09/17/world/asia/south-korea-american-fails-in-effort-to-swim-to-north.html', 'snippet': 'An American was arrested Tuesday night on a river that borders North Korea, the South Korean news media reported on Wednesday.', 'lead_paragraph': 'An American was arrested\xa0Tuesday\xa0night on a river that borders North Korea, the South Korean news media reported\xa0on Wednesday. The South Korean news agency\xa0Yonhap, quoting an anonymous government source, said that the American man had attempted to swim to North Korea. “All we can say at this point is that our border guards arrested an American man last night on the riverbank near the border,” said a Defense Ministry spokesman, who spoke on condition of anonymity because the inquiry was continuing. Yonhap\xa0said that the American, in his early 30s, had been found on the riverbank shortly before\xa0midnight. After he was apprehended, he told South Korean officials that he had intended to go to North Korea to meet its leader, Kim\xa0Jong-un, Yonhap reported. The United States Embassy said it was aware of the reports and had been “in touch with the appropriate South Korean authorities.”', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'South Korea: American Fails in Effort to Swim to North', 'kicker': 'World Briefing', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'South Korea: American Fails in Effort to Swim to North', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'North Korea', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'South Korea', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Swimming', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-09-17T04:18:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Choe Sang-Hun', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Choe', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sang-Hun', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/744eaad6-e387-5063-b160-51cd2d6c1f38', 'word_count': 150, 'uri': 'nyt://article/744eaad6-e387-5063-b160-51cd2d6c1f38'}, {'abstract': 'Television highlights.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/09/17/arts/television/whats-on-tv-wednesday.html', 'snippet': 'Television highlights.', 'lead_paragraph': '6:30 P.M. (Showtime) FRUITVALE STATION (2013) On Jan. 1, 2009, Oscar Grant III, a 22-year-old African-American father, was shot in the back by a white Bay Area Rapid Transit police officer on a subway platform in Oakland, Calif., his killing captured on cellphone. Ryan Coogler’s feature directorial debut reconstructs the final 24 hours in the life of Mr. Grant (played by Michael B. Jordan, above, with Ariana Neal) who, after two stints in a state prison, had returned to selling drugs after losing his job at a supermarket because of chronic lateness. Octavia Spencer plays his mother, still able to boss him around, and Melonie Diaz the mother of his daughter, with whom he’s trying to stay. Mr. Coogler’s “main intention — and his great achievement, as well as Mr. Jordan’s — is to make Oscar a fully human presence, to pay him the respect of acknowledging his complexities and contradictions,” A. O. Scott wrote in The New York Times. “The radicalism of ‘Fruitvale Station’ lies precisely here, in its refusal to turn a man into a symbol. Nearly every black man, whether or not he is president, tends to be flattened out by popular culture and the psychopathology of everyday American life, rendered as an innocent victim, a noble warrior or a menace to society. There is a dehumanizing violence in this habit, a willed, toxic blindness that ‘Fruitvale Station’ at once exposes and resists.” ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What’s On TV Wednesday', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'What’s On TV Wednesday', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2014-09-17T04:19:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kathryn Shattuck', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kathryn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Shattuck', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Schedule', '_id': 'nyt://article/86e7d15f-f9b1-5d94-b4ea-5d7daaf3aad9', 'word_count': 733, 'uri': 'nyt://article/86e7d15f-f9b1-5d94-b4ea-5d7daaf3aad9'}, {'abstract': 'Corrections appearing in print on September 17, 2014.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/09/17/pageoneplus/corrections-september-17-2014.html', 'snippet': 'Corrections appearing in print on September 17, 2014.', 'lead_paragraph': 'INTERNATIONAL', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Corrections: September 17, 2014', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Corrections', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2014-09-17T04:38:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Summary', 'section_name': 'Corrections', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e0013a4f-03dd-570f-b8b5-8ddc2dcf20c7', 'word_count': 737, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e0013a4f-03dd-570f-b8b5-8ddc2dcf20c7'}, {'abstract': 'The Islamic State has released a Hollywood-style video to respond to President Obama’s declaration that he would “degrade and ultimately destroy” it.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/09/17/world/middleeast/isis-issues-video-riposte-to-obama.html', 'snippet': 'The Islamic State has released a Hollywood-style video to respond to President Obama’s declaration that he would “degrade and ultimately destroy” it.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WASHINGTON — In one of the Islamic State’s first responses to President Obama’s declaration that he would “degrade and ultimately destroy” it, the group released a short video late Tuesday in which it appeared to say that its militants would kill American ground forces should President Obama deploy them.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Islamic State Issues Video Challenge to Obama', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Militants Issue Riposte on Video to Obama', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Islamic State in Iraq and Syria (ISIS)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Schmidt, Michael S', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Video Recordings, Downloads and Streaming', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Iraq', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-09-17T05:03:12+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Middle East', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael S. Schmidt', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': 'S.', 'lastname': 'Schmidt', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a59f546c-eafd-535f-ab72-79008166d2fe', 'word_count': 599, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a59f546c-eafd-535f-ab72-79008166d2fe'}, {'abstract': 'With luck and 