s a window onto the weird world of untrue news.', 'lead_paragraph': 'For fans of the new Thunderbolt roller coaster at Coney Island and anyone who might be thinking of riding it, an article making the rounds on social media last week contained sobering news.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/16/nyregion/00nymeta1-cityroom/00nymeta1-cityroom-articleLarge-v2.png', 'height': 396, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/09/16/nyregion/00nymeta1-cityroom/00nymeta1-cityroom-articleLarge-v2.png', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 396}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/16/nyregion/00nymeta1-cityroom/00nymeta1-cityroom-jumbo-v2.png', 'height': 590, 'width': 895, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/16/nyregion/00nymeta1-cityroom/00nymeta1-cityroom-superJumbo-v2.png', 'height': 590, 'width': 895, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/16/nyregion/00nymeta1-cityroom/00nymeta1-cityroom-thumbStandard-v2.png', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/09/16/nyregion/00nymeta1-cityroom/00nymeta1-cityroom-thumbStandard-v2.png', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/16/nyregion/00nymeta1-cityroom/00nymeta1-cityroom-thumbLarge-v2.png', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Fake News Story Causes Real Headache at Coney Island', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hoaxes and Pranks', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-09-17T14:59:57+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andy Newman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Newman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/09fd05ce-71e2-5505-bfd0-44b4c25fd0ef', 'word_count': 507, 'uri': 'nyt://article/09fd05ce-71e2-5505-bfd0-44b4c25fd0ef'}, {'abstract': 'The neighborhood has a bustling farmer’s market, refurbished playgrounds and new shops and restaurants. But none of this comes cheap.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/09/21/realestate/union-square-a-place-to-converge-and-buy-kale.html', 'snippet': 'The neighborhood has a bustling farmer’s market, refurbished playgrounds and new shops and restaurants. But none of this comes cheap.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When Victoria Baradi Anderson moved into the Union Square neighborhood in 1998, she was attracted to its central location and easy subway access. “I was single and enjoying the night life,” she said. After graduating from college and living with her parents for a year, she bought a studio on East 14th Street for less than $100,000. There was little in the surrounding streets that appealed to her, including the 3.6-acre park that gave the area its name and “was not as nice as it is now,” she said.', 'print_section': 'RE', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Union Square: A Place to Converge, and Buy Kale', 'kicker': 'Living In', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Place to Converge, and Buy Kale', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Real Estate and Housing (Residential)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Jacobson, Aileen', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Union Square (Manhattan, NY)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-09-17T15:00:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'RealEstate', 'section_name': 'Real Estate', 'byline': {'original': 'By Aileen Jacobson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Aileen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Jacobson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/42546300-75ba-5818-b8f2-4db073658267', 'word_count': 1343, 'uri': 'nyt://article/42546300-75ba-5818-b8f2-4db073658267'}, {'abstract': 'By charging different prices for generic drugs, health insurers may be violating the spirit of the Affordable Care Act.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/09/18/upshot/how-insurers-are-finding-ways-to-shift-costs-to-the-sick.html', 'snippet': 'By charging different prices for generic drugs, health insurers may be violating the spirit of the Affordable Care Act.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Health insurance companies are no longer allowed to turn away patients because of their pre-existing conditions or charge them more because of those conditions. But some health policy experts say insurers may be doing so in a more subtle way: by forcing people with a variety of illnesses — including Parkinson’s disease, diabetes and epilepsy — to pay more for their drugs.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/18/upshot/18up-generic/18up-generic-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/09/18/upshot/18up-generic/18up-generic-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/18/upshot/18up-generic/18up-generic-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/18/upshot/18up-generic/18up-generic-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/18/upshot/18up-generic/18up-generic-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/09/18/upshot/18up-generic/18up-generic-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/18/upshot/18up-generic/18up-generic-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'How Insurers Are Finding Ways to Shift Costs to the Sick', 'kicker': 'Public Health', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Health Insurance and Managed Care', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Drugs (Pharmaceuticals)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Generic Brands and Products', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Patient Protection and Affordable Care Act (2010)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-09-17T15:00:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Upshot', 'section_name': 'The Upshot', 'byline': {'original': 'By Charles Ornstein', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Charles', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ornstein', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f4d9f607-42bb-5905-8941-483886e1dd8e', 'word_count': 1293, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f4d9f607-42bb-5905-8941-483886e1dd8e'}, {'abstract': 'A follow-up to the middle-grades novel “Wonder” offers a snippet of story and a pithy saying for each day of the year.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/09/17/books/365-days-of-wonder-by-r-j-palacio.html', 'snippet': 'A follow-up to the middle-grades novel “Wonder” offers a snippet of story and a pithy saying for each day of the year.', 'lead_paragraph': 'After R. J. Palacio’s “Wonder” was published in 2012 and became a middle-grades juggernaut, many hoped the author would continue the story of August Pullman, the Manhattan boy born with serious facial deformities, and his friends and family. “Wonder” ends as Auggie is finishing fifth grade at Beecher Prep, his first year in school after being home-schooled for the surgery-filled first decade of his life. He is given a special award recognizing his courage by the school’s principal. But then what? Palacio’s characters were hard to say goodbye to, and in the current series-heavy world of children’s books, it seemed natural that there would be more to come.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/17/books/17childrens/17childrens-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 898, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/09/17/books/17childrens/17childrens-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 898}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/17/books/17childrens/17childrens-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 684, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'cr