s by 10 percent while they are being trained in cloud, mobile and big data computing.', 'lead_paragraph': 'IBM is cutting the pay of some employees in its services business by 10 percent for six months while they spend up to one day a week in training programs.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/17/business/17bits-ibmlabor/17bits-ibmlabor-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/09/17/business/17bits-ibmlabor/17bits-ibmlabor-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/17/business/17bits-ibmlabor/17bits-ibmlabor-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/17/business/17bits-ibmlabor/17bits-ibmlabor-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/17/business/17bits-ibmlabor/17bits-ibmlabor-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/09/17/business/17bits-ibmlabor/17bits-ibmlabor-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/17/business/17bits-ibmlabor/17bits-ibmlabor-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'IBM Offers Workers Training and Pay Cuts', 'kicker': 'Bits', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Labor and Jobs', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'International Business Machines Corporation', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'United States', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-09-17T15:18:41+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'byline': {'original': 'By Steve Lohr', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Steve', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Lohr', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9864710b-2e88-5bca-b591-f06560963ee9', 'word_count': 653, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9864710b-2e88-5bca-b591-f06560963ee9'}, {'abstract': 'Loewe’s creative director Jonathan Anderson reimagines the archetype of a chair.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/09/17/jonathan-anderson-loewe-steven-meisel-javier-carvajal-armchair/', 'snippet': 'Loewe’s creative director Jonathan Anderson reimagines the archetype of a chair.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When Jonathan Anderson took over as Loewe’s creative director last September, he had a clear mandate: to liven up the 168-year-old Spanish fashion and leather goods label. In just over a year he’s done precisely that, spearheading a seaside ad campaign featuring archival and newly commissioned images by Steven Meisel and unveiling youthful new\xa0takes on Oxford shoes and crocodile bags. But that wasn’t enough for the London-based fashion darling who is now reimagining a wood-and-leather armchair that the architect Javier Carvajal designed in 1959 for one of Loewe’s Madrid stores. “There was something so pure in the wooden archetype of the chair,” says Anderson, who gave it a splashy new color palette. “I wanted to interrupt that aesthetic.”', 'print_section': 'M2', 'print_page': '46', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/28/t-magazine/28design-look-loewe/28design-look-loewe-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/09/28/t-magazine/28design-look-loewe/28design-look-loewe-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/28/t-magazine/28design-look-loewe/28design-look-loewe-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 667, 'width': 1000, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/28/t-magazine/28design-look-loewe/28design-look-loewe-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 667, 'width': 1000, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/28/t-magazine/28design-look-loewe/28design-look-loewe-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/09/28/t-magazine/28design-look-loewe/28design-look-loewe-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/28/t-magazine/28design-look-loewe/28design-look-loewe-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Best Seat in the House', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Best Seat in the House', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Furniture', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Interior Design and Furnishings', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Anderson, Jonathan William', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Meisel, Steven', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-09-17T15:26:33+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'TStyle', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Brooke Bobb', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Brooke', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bobb', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4648b964-6c98-5fa5-b962-8aea35d8fe99', 'word_count': 127, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4648b964-6c98-5fa5-b962-8aea35d8fe99'}, {'abstract': 'A stew of ground beef and tomatoes gives a taste of island home cooking.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/09/21/magazine/the-ultimate-cuban-comfort-food-picadillo.html', 'snippet': 'A stew of ground beef and tomatoes gives a taste of island home cooking.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Here is one of the great dishes of the Cuban diaspora: picadillo, a soft, fragrant stew of ground beef and tomatoes, with raisins added for sweetness and olives for salt. Versions of it exist across the Caribbean and into Latin America. James Villas, the elegant hedonist who for many years was the food and wine editor of Town & Country magazine, championed one from St. Lucia, in the Lesser Antilles. But many Cubans claim the dish as their own, a taste of the pre-revolutionary island at its homey best. “Picadillo has a sentimental resonance,” said Betty Cortina, a Cuban-American food writer who is the editor of The Miami Herald’s lifestyle magazine, Indulge. She even took a recipe for picadillo with her to college at the University of Florida. “Everyone who is of Cuban descent has a recipe for it, and each one of those is the most authentic. It’s a comfort food, probably the most consummate example of one in Cuban cuisine.”', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '54', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/21/magazine/21eat/21eat-articleLarge-v4.jpg', 'height': 570, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/09/21/magazine/21eat/21eat-articleLarge-v4.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 570}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/21/magazine/21eat/21eat-jumbo-v4.jpg', 'height': 973, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/21/magazine/21eat/21eat-superJumbo-v4.jpg', 'height': 1946, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/21/magazine/21eat/21eat-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/09/21/magazine/21eat/21eat-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/21/magazine/21eat/21eat-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Ultimate Cuban Comfort Food: Picadillo', 'kicker': 'Eat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Cuban for Comfort', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cooking and Cookbooks', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sifton, Sam', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Meat', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Beef', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Recipes', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tomatoes', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Cuba', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-09-17T15:27:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sam Sifton', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sifton', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9f92991a-9f95-5118-91ef-d6a11697a4cc', 'word_count': 761, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9f92991a-9f95-5118-91ef-d6a11697a4cc'}, {'abstract': 'The film predicted a future of ad-covered blimps and buildings. What’s actually happened is somewhat different, but, to some, equally scary.', 'web_url': 'https://op-talk.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/09/17/what-blade-runner-got-wrong/', 'snippet': 'The film predicted a future of ad-covered blimps and buildings. What’s actually happened is somewhat different, but, to some, equally scary.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Ubiquitous, intrusive advertising has long been a feature of dystopian fiction — directors and writers seem convinced that one of the many things that will make the future terrible is its sheer density of ads.\xa0But we now live in the future that dystopians of the 1980s\xa0and 1990s\xa0were predicting, and what’s happened isn’t quite what they feared — though, to some, it’s still pretty scary.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/17/opinion/nyto-blade/nyto-blade-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 595, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/09/17/opinion/nyto-blade/nyto-blade-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 595}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/17/opinion/nyto-blade/nyto-blade-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1015, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/17/opinion/nyto-blade/nyto-blade-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1968, 'width': 1986, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/17/opinion/nyto-blade/nyto-blade-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/09/17/opinion/nyto-blade/nyto-blade-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/17/opinion/nyto-blade/nyto-blade-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'What ‘Blade Runner’ Got Wrong', 'kicker': 'Op-Talk', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Online Advertising', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Political Advertising', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Social Media', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Facebook Inc', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-09-17T15:27:47+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Anna North', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Anna', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'North', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'ny