You're the Boss Editors", 'person': [{'firstname': '', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/fbc3b8ce-76c0-5799-a3d0-28dbec2a65e8', 'word_count': 152, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fbc3b8ce-76c0-5799-a3d0-28dbec2a65e8'}, {'abstract': 'Tom Stoppard’s play “Indian Ink” is finally getting its Off Broadway premiere at the Roundabout Theater.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/09/21/theater/indian-ink-at-the-roundabout-theater.html', 'snippet': 'Tom Stoppard’s play “Indian Ink” is finally getting its Off Broadway premiere at the Roundabout Theater.', 'lead_paragraph': '“Indian Ink” was the last play Tom Stoppard wrote that didn’t soon become a New York event. When it opened at the West End in the mid-90s, his “Arcadia” was a London hit and played in a star-studded production at Lincoln Center. But for some reason, Lincoln Center, which went on to produce his “The Invention of Love,” “The Coast of Utopia” and “Rock ’n’ Roll,” passed on “Indian Ink,” an intellectually lively, time-traveling drama that moves between a poet in the 1930s and an academic in the future examining her life. ', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/21/arts/21GUIDE7/21GUIDE7-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 393, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/09/21/arts/21GUIDE7/21GUIDE7-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 393}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/21/arts/21GUIDE7/21GUIDE7-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 670, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, '