+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Erik Piepenburg', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Erik', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Piepenburg', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d1240ba1-fbe9-5ca2-8591-fba25217d4d5', 'word_count': 734, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d1240ba1-fbe9-5ca2-8591-fba25217d4d5'}, {'abstract': 'At Abyssinia in Harlem, the flatbread does the heavy lifting with the Ethiopian dishes.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/09/24/dining/hungry-city-abyssinia-in-harlem.html', 'snippet': 'At Abyssinia in Harlem, the flatbread does the heavy lifting with the Ethiopian dishes.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The description “ground mildly spiced chickpeas” does not prepare you for what arrives, which looks less like a self-sufficient dish than a sauce or a displaced soup, as red as the desert. It’s not that mild, either, thanks to a nice shock of berbere, an Ethiopian spice blend dominated by red chile, with gentler warming notes from cinnamon, cardamom and cloves. ', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Fingers Stand In for Forks', 'kicker': 'Hungry City', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Fingers Stand In for Forks', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Restaurants', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mishan, Ligaya', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Harlem (Manhattan, NY)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Abyssinia (Manhattan, NY, Restaurant)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-09-18T18:39:47+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Dining', 'section_name': 'Food', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ligaya Mishan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ligaya', 'middlename