akil', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Threats and Threatening Messages', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Pakistan', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Karachi (Pakistan)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-09-18T19:49:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Zia ur-Rehman', 'person': [{'firstname': '', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/db0d4030-eed4-573b-857a-6163656ea901', 'word_count': 504, 'uri': 'nyt://article/db0d4030-eed4-573b-857a-6163656ea901'}, {'abstract': 'A biography of Bill Cosby chronicles the outspoken comedian’s rise to stardom, along with personal and professional setbacks.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/09/21/books/review/mark-whitakers-cosby.html', 'snippet': 'A biography of Bill Cosby chronicles the outspoken comedian’s rise to stardom, along with personal and professional setbacks.', 'lead_paragraph': 'There are at least two vexing revelations within the pages of Mark Whita\xadker’s wonderfully thorough biography of America’s most accomplished comedian. The first is how remarkably unfunny Bill Cosby’s countless riffs and bits come across on paper. (“It’s the Lord, Noah!” “Right!” Sure.) The second is the number of failed or short-lived television series — other than the one that forever altered television — that bear the name Cosby. Both are keys to the man’s complicated \xadascendance. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/14/books/14DRUMMING/14DRUMMING-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 768, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'cr