tion_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jeannette Catsoulis', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jeannette', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Catsoulis', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/5e2c571a-8895-57b2-96ad-46934052c116', 'word_count': 296, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5e2c571a-8895-57b2-96ad-46934052c116'}, {'abstract': 'In “Fort Bliss,” Michelle Monaghan finds rough going returning to her family after a tour in Afghanistan.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/09/19/movies/fort-bliss-stars-michelle-monaghan-as-a-soldier-mother.html', 'snippet': 'In “Fort Bliss,” Michelle Monaghan finds rough going returning to her family after a tour in Afghanistan.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A story about finding peace during wartime, “Fort Bliss” turns on the struggles of an Army medic, Maggie Swann (Michelle Monaghan), who’s trying to make it back to normal after a tour in Afghanistan. You first meet her while she’s dodging battlefield bullets. Soon she’s slicked in someone else’s blood, and on her way to a commendation. A few beats later, though, Maggie has moved onto the El Paso base that gives the movie its name and is trying to reconnect with her confused, alienated 5-year-old, Paul (Oakes Fegley), after a lengthy separation. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/19/arts/FORTBLISS/FORTBLISS-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/09/19/arts/FORTBLISS/FORTBLISS-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/19/arts/FORTBLISS/FORTBLISS-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/19/arts/FORTBLISS/FORTBLISS-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/19/arts/FORTBLISS/FORTBLISS-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/09/19/arts/FORTBLISS/FORTBLISS-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/19/arts/FORTBLISS/FORTBLISS-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'At Home in War. In War at Home.', 'kicker': 'Movie Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'At Home in War. In War at Home.', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Myers, Claudia', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Monaghan, Michelle', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Fort Bliss (Movie)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Fegley, Oakes', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-09-18T21:15:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By Manohla Dargis', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Manohla', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Dargis', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/ec8a7bb8-46d0-5b47-8ac9-48d51667076b', 'word_count': 482, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ec8a7bb8-46d0-5b47-8ac9-48d51667076b'}, {'abstract': 'A selected guide to talks, walks, readings, exhibitions, parades, festivals and other events in New York City.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/09/19/arts/spare-times-for-sept-19-25.html', 'snippet': 'A selected guide to talks, walks, readings, exhibitions, parades, festivals and other events in New York City.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Photoville (through Sept. 28) Drop-bys are definitely encouraged at this photographic village, held inside and outside 63 shipping containers at Pier 5 in Brooklyn Bridge Park, Joralemon Street and the East River, Fulton Ferry. Presented by United Photo Industries, this is the third year of this free event, which also features panel discussions, workshops and other activities, some interactive. Among the images on display are untitled works like the portrait above by Nicole Wells, a finalist in the Head On Portrait Prize in Australia, and the one by Stanley Greene, top, one of 10 large prints in “Za’atari,” a collection focusing on a Syrian refugee camp that includes works by other photographers. Doug Menuez’s “Geek Sex. Adobe Systems, Mountain View, California, 1991,” below, from his book “Fearless Genius: The Digital Revolution in Silicon Valley 1985-2000,” captures a mirthful mood. In a highlight event on Sept. 27 at 6:30 p.m., Tyler Stableford will discuss his photos documenting farmers, ranchers, beekeepers and others across the country; a reception will precede the talk at 5 p.m. Photoville hours: Fridays and Thursday, 4 to 10 p.m.; Saturdays, noon to 10 p.m.; and Sundays, noon to 8 p.m. A beer garden is on site, Pier 5 in Brooklyn Bridge Park, Joralemon Street and the East River, throughout the event. More information is available at 718-215-9075 or photovillenyc.org. It’s a view of the world from the shores of the East River. (Anne Mancuso)', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '20', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Spare Times for Sept. 19-25', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Spare Times', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2014-09-18T21:18:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Schedule', '_id': 'nyt://article/4eabd87a-e8e2-5084-a7fc-f372957b2413', 'word_count': 4304, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4eabd87a-e8e2-5084-a7fc-f372957b2413'}, {'abstract': '“Henry & Me” is an animated film about a cancer-stricken boy who’s transported to a dreamlike place where Yankees greats play on.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/09/19/movies/henry-me-with-richard-gere-austin-williams-and-others.html', 'snippet': '“Henry & Me” is an animated film about a cancer-stricken boy who’s transported to a dreamlike place where Yankees greats play on.', 'lead_paragraph': 'HENRY & ME', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/19/arts/19HENRY/HENRY-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 339, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/09/19/arts/19HENRY/HENRY-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 339}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/19/arts/19HENRY/HENRY-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 579, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/19/arts/19HENRY/HENRY-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1158, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/19/arts/19HENRY/HENRY-thumbStand