, 'byline': {'original': 'By Floyd Norris', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Floyd', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Norris', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ebf3ac74-30ff-53ce-a030-ed85bc8ec8e3', 'word_count': 1280, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ebf3ac74-30ff-53ce-a030-ed85bc8ec8e3'}, {'abstract': 'The composer M. Lamar offers a bracing alternative to the dispiriting traffic in blandly competent art clogging the gallery system these days.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/09/19/arts/design/m-lamar-negrogothic-a-manifesto-the-aesthetics-of-m-lamar.html', 'snippet': 'The composer M. Lamar offers a bracing alternative to the dispiriting traffic in blandly competent art clogging the gallery system these days.', 'lead_paragraph': 'With “Negrogothic, a Manifesto, the Aesthetics of M. Lamar,” the composer M. Lamar offers a bracing alternative to the dispiriting traffic in blandly competent art clogging the New York gallery system these days. Surrealistic, campy and ferociously expressionist, Mr. Lamar’s multimedia work is a heady blend of music, performance, film and political allegory that grapples with the legacy of slavery in America.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '27', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'M. Lamar: ‘Negrogothic, a Manifesto, the Aesthetics of M. Lamar’', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'M. Lamar: ‘Negrogothic, a Manifesto, the Aesthetics of M. Lamar’', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lamar, M (1984- )', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-09-18T22:01:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Art & Design', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ken Johnson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ken', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Johnson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/0d5e9647-e11f-5c1b-a329-80c9c21a5630', 'word_count': 290, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0d5e9647-e11f-5c1b-a329-80c9c21a5630'}, {'abstract': 'Answers to questions about inviting neighbors to a party; giving cigarettes to the homeless; and handling a colleague’s work habits.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/09/21/fashion/anger-management.html', 'snippet': 'Answers to questions about inviting neighbors to a party; giving cigarettes to the homeless; and handling a colleague’s work habits.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When our children were growing up (they are in college now), one of our neighbors did not invite our daughter to her children’s birthday parties. My husband insisted we invite all the neighborhood children to our parties. For years, I have harbored anger toward this woman who excluded our daughter. (Who would make a child so miserable?) Now, we are giving a neighborhood party for another couple that is moving away. Do I invite the family I have hated for 13 long years?', 'print_section': 'ST', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/28/fashion/social_inline/social_inline-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 599, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/07/28/fashion/social_inline/social_inline-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 599}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/28/fashion/social_inline/social_inline-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 923, 'width': 925, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/28/fashion/social_inline/social_inline-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 923, 'width': 925, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/28/fashion/social_inline/social_inline-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/28/fashion/social_inline/social_inline-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/28/fashion/social_inline/social_inline-thumbLarge-v3.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Anger Management', 'kicker': 'Social Q’s', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Anger Management', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Customs, Etiquette and Manners', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-09-18T22:02:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Styles', 'section_name': 'Fashion & Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Philip Galanes', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Philip', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Galanes', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/20806146-203d-59a6-bb76-4f5665717d34', 'word_count': 864, 'uri': 'nyt://article/20806146-203d-59a6-bb76-4f5665717d34'}, {'abstract': 'Roberto Minervini’s “Stop the Pounding Heart” is the story of a young Texas girl in a strict Christian home who finds herself attracted to a rodeo rider.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/09/19/movies/stop-the-pounding-heart-reveals-minervinis-texas.html', 'snippet': 'Roberto Minervini’s “Stop the Pounding Heart” is the story of a young Texas girl in a strict Christian home who finds herself attracted to a rodeo rider.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In Roberto Minervini’s “Stop the Pounding Heart,” nonprofessional actors play versions of their real-life selves. This practice, which can blur the distinction between drama and documentary and allow a glimpse at truths otherwise invisible to the camera, flourished in the Italian neorealism of the 1940s and the work of Iranian filmmakers like Abbas Kiarostami and Mohsen Makhmalbaf in the 1990s. Mr. Minervini, an Italian director who has now made three features in rural Texas, extends this tradition with impressive sensitivity and skill.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/19/arts/19STOPYHRPOUNDING/19STOPYHRPOUNDING-articleLarge-v3.jpg', 'height': 317, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/09/19/arts/19STOPYHRPOUNDING/19STOPYHRPOUNDING-articleLarge-v3.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 317}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/19/arts/19STOPYHRPOUNDING/19STOPYHRPOUNDING-jumbo-v3.jpg', 'height': 541, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/19/arts/19STOPYHRPOUNDING/19STOPYHRPOUNDING-superJumbo-v3.jpg', 'height': 1081, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/19/arts/19STOPYHRPOUNDING/19STOPYHRPOUNDING-thumbStandard-v4.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/09/19/arts/19STOPYHRPOUNDING/19STOPYHRPOUNDING-thumbStandard-v4.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/19/arts/19STOPYHRPOUNDING/19STOPYHRPOUNDING-thumbLarge-v4.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Bible Belt Soul Search ', 'kicker': 'Movie Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Bible Belt Soul Search', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Minervini, Roberto', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Trichell, Colby', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Carlson, Sara', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Stop the Pounding Heart (Movie)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Makhmalbaf, Mohsen', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kiarostami, Abbas', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-09-18T22:03:44+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By A.O. Scott', 'person': [{'firstname': 'A.', 'middlename': 'O.', 'lastname': 'Scott', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/bba081c8-6eec-58d9-a35f-90581c4901e4', 'word_count': 367, 'uri': 'nyt://article/bba081c8-6eec-58d9-a35f-90581c4901e4'}, {'abstract': 'The shift in Ms. Mack’s latest works is depressing, even if it’s a comment on the absence of soul and authenticity in the art-fair system.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/09/19/arts/design/anissa-mack-deep-deep-pepsi.html', 'snippet': 'The shift in Ms. Mack’s latest works is depressing, even if it’s a comment on the absence of soul and authenticity in the art-fair system.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Anissa Mack’s abiding interest in Americana has taken many immersive, socially minded forms. In what is probably her best-known project, “The Last Full Weekend Each September,” she produced entries for every single category of a crafting contest at a county fair in Durham, Conn. Her latest works, however, seem made for a different kind of fair: an international art fair, with its own list of approved categories for visual expression. This shift is depressing, even if it’s intended as a comment on the absence of soul and authenticity in the art-fair system (and in some places, you’re not sure that it is.)', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '27', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/19/arts/19MACK-GALLERIES/MACK-GALLERIES-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 914, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/09/19/arts/19MACK-GALLERIES/MACK-GALLERIES-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 914}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/19/arts/19MACK-GALLERIES/MACK-GALLERIES-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 672, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/19/arts/19MACK-GALLERIES/MACK-GALLERIES-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1344, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/19/arts/19MACK-GALLERIES/MACK-GALLERIES-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/09/19/arts/19MACK-GALLERIES/MACK-GALLERIES-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight