or not.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/09/19/opinion/the-importance-of-taping-interrogations.html', 'snippet': 'A full record of the questioning of a suspect can show a judge or jury whether a confession was coerced or not.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Confessions are among the most compelling and critical pieces of evidence in a criminal trial. Even without any physical evidence tying a defendant to a crime, a few simple words can be enough for a conviction. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '28', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Importance of Taping Interrogations', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Importance of Taping Interrogations', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Patz, Etan', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hernandez, Pedro (1961- )', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Interrogations', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Confessions', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Murders and Attempted Murders', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Video Recordings, Downloads and Streaming', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Missing Persons', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'False Arrests, Convictions and Imprisonments', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-09-19T00:48:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Editorial Board', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Editorial Board'}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/a18fa668-91c3-58c0-a477-d43663d64e63', 'word_count': 525, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a18fa668-91c3-58c0-a477-d43663d64e63'}, {'abstract': 'At least 29 people were killed and more than 65 wounded in attacks in and around the predominantly Shiite neighborhood of Kadhimiya in Baghdad.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/09/19/world/middleeast/iraq-deadly-attacks-strike-baghdad.html', 'snippet': 'At least 29 people were killed and more than 65 wounded in attacks in and around the predominantly Shiite neighborhood of Kadhimiya in Baghdad.', 'lead_paragraph': ' At least 29 people were killed and more than 65 wounded on Thursday night in attacks in and around the predominantly Shiite neighborhood of Kadhimiya in Baghdad, raising the day’s death toll to at least 45. The attacks were part of a broad upswing in sectarian violence in the capital since the Sunni extremists calling themselves the Islamic State surged toward the city a few months ago. Bombings and assassinations against both Sunnis and Shiites have become a daily occurrence. Earlier Thursday, at least 16 people were killed and dozens were wounded by four explosions and a mortar attack in and around Baghdad, police officials said. Then in the Kadhimiya area, two car bombs killed at least 24 people. A short time later, a dozen mortar rounds fired into the same area killed at least five more. There was no immediate claim of responsibility for the attacks. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Iraq: Deadly Attacks Strike Baghdad', 'kicker': 'World Briefing', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Iraq: Deadly Attacks Strike Baghdad', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kirkpatrick, David D', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Iraq', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Islamic State in Iraq and Syria (ISIS)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Deaths (Fatalities)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Terrorism', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Baghdad (Iraq)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sunni Muslims', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Shiite Muslims', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-09-19T00:51:44+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Middle East', 'byline': {'original': 'By David D. Kirkpatrick', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/e500a739-f883-5bdd-b8fd-f18057a83001', 'word_count': 147, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e500a739-f883-5bdd-b8fd-f18057a83001'}, {'abstract': 'Officials of the Syrian opposition coalition said they had created a commission of inquiry into the deaths of children caused by adulterated measles vaccine administered this week.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/09/19/world/middleeast/syria-opposition-investigates-vaccine.html', 'snippet': 'Officials of the Syrian opposition coalition said they had created a commission of inquiry into the deaths of children caused by adulterated measles vaccine administered this week.', 'lead_paragraph': ' Officials of the Syrian opposition coalition said Thursday that they had created a commission of inquiry into the deaths of children in insurgent-held Idlib Province caused by adulterated measles vaccine administered this week. The announcement came amid new indications that the disaster had been the result of negligence and not sabotage, and that it was possibly caused by a mixing of vaccines and an anesthetic used to relax muscles during surgery. The opposition coalition, which considers itself the interim government in that part of the country, said on its website that the commission would submit a final report on Sept. 25. The announcement also said 30 children had died. Conflicting accounts on Wednesday said 15 to 50 children had died within minutes of receiving the vaccine. The World Health Organization and Unicef, which have been overseeing an extensive vaccination campaign in Syria, have expressed concern that the vaccine disaster will discourage parents from protecting their children from measles, rubella and polio. The diseases are making a comeback in Syria because of the war and the collapse of public health services. Save the Children, a charity that helped plan the vaccination campaign and financially supported it in Idlib, said in a statement that it was “deeply saddened and shocked” by the deaths and expressed readiness to “ensure there is clarity about how this tragic situation occurred.” ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Syria: Opposition Investigates Vaccine', 'kicker': 'World Briefing', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Syria: Opposition Investigates Vaccine', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Syria', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Vaccination and Immunization', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Children and Childhood', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'German Measles (Rubella)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': "United Nations Children's Fund", 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'World Health Organization', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Save the Children', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-09-19T00:54:54+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Middle East', 'byline': {'original': 'By Rick Gladstone', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Rick', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gladstone', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt: