'Fashion & Style', 'subsection_name': 'Weddings', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/51206091-60b0-5d2b-a7fd-88477ed873cb', 'word_count': 1089, 'uri': 'nyt://article/51206091-60b0-5d2b-a7fd-88477ed873cb'}, {'abstract': 'From the International Herald Tribune archives: Nazis arrest workers in Slovakia in 1939.', 'web_url': 'https://iht-retrospective.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/09/19/1939-slovaks-are-terrorized/', 'snippet': 'From the International Herald Tribune archives: Nazis arrest workers in Slovakia in 1939.', 'lead_paragraph': 'COPENHAGEN — Reports reaching here indicate that new acts of terrorism are being perpetrated in Bohemia and Slovakia. Following a revolt at the Skoda works, the Gestapo made hundreds of arrests. In many cases, families of arrested workers received a few days later the papers of the member of their family arrested and a primed notice warning them that they were not to wear mourning or to speak of the death. It is believed probable that the Gestapo carried out executions at Prague, in the basement of the Petchek Bank. Other executions have been reported from Brno, where a revolt has been suppressed. Rudolf Beran, former Premier, has been arrested. A number of executions have taken place at Zilina, in Slovakia, without the Slovak authorities being advised. To their inquiry the Gestapo replied that it was ‘‘obliged to take all measures of prudence necessary to prevent acts directed against the German Army.’’— New York Herald Tribune, European Edition, Sept. 20, 1939', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '1939: Slovaks Are Terrorized', 'kicker': 'IHT Retrospective', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'World War II (1939-45)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Czechoslovakia', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Germany', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-09-19T15:24:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'byline': {'original': 'By International Herald Tribune', 'person': [{'firstname': 'International', 'middlename': 'Herald', 'lastname': 'Tribune', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/fc65447b-aa5e-53a9-bc91-75b976146203', 'word_count': 161, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fc65447b-aa5e-53a9-bc91-75b976146203'}, {'abstract': 'Fringed, knee-high moccasins added to the southwest spirit of Veronica Etro’s spring 2015 collection.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/09/19/milan-fashion-week-spring-2015-etro-shoes/', 'snippet': 'Fringed, knee-high moccasins added to the southwest spirit of Veronica Etro’s spring 2015 collection.', 'lead_paragraph': 'T plucks the best shoes right off the runway.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/19/t-magazine/19shoe-etro-gilchrist/19shoe-etro-gilchrist-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 800, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/09/19/t-magazine/19shoe-etro-gilchrist/19shoe-etro-gilchrist-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 800}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/19/t-magazine/19shoe-etro-gilchrist/19shoe-etro-gilchrist-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 768, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/19/t-magazine/19shoe-etro-gilchrist/19shoe-etro-gilchrist-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1536, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/19/t-magazine/19shoe-etro-gilchrist/19shoe-etro-gilchrist-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/09/19/t-magazine/19shoe-etro-gilchrist/19shoe-etro-gilchrist-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/19/t-magazine/19shoe-etro-gilchrist/19shoe-etro-gilchrist-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Etro’s Swaying, Bohemian Footwear', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fashion and Apparel', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Shoes and Boots', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Etro, Veronica', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Etro', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Milan (Italy)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-09-19T15:35:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'TStyle', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Malina Joseph Gilchrist', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Malina', 'middlename': 'Joseph', 'lastname': 'Gilchrist', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c2c931ef-34db-5c09-8a0e-734a7a55a5ff', 'word_count': 108, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c2c931ef-34db-5c09-8a0e-734a7a55a5ff'}, {'abstract': 'From the International Herald Tribune archives: Khrushchev voices support for nations in Africa in 1964.', 'web_url': 'https://iht-retrospective.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/09/19/1964-khrushchev-to-help-africans/', 'snippet': 'From the International Herald Tribune archives: Khrushchev voices support for nations in Africa in 1964.', 'lead_paragraph': 'MOSCOW — At a Kremlin reception for the 1,000 delegates to the leftist World Youth Forum here, Mr. Khrushchev declared Soviet support for Angolan fighters against ‘‘Portuguese Fascism,’’ the struggle of Negroes in the Congo against ‘‘conquerors and traitors’’ and the struggle against ‘‘racist oppression’’ in South Africa. If they want arms, ‘‘we are ready to discuss the question in a brotherly fashion and give our help,’’ he said. Mr. Khrushchev told the youth forum delegates: ‘‘I did not look at hydrogen or atomic bombs, but I saw an assortment of weapons used for infantry, artillery, tanks and rockets. I saw them and I said: ‘See what man has built for man. It’s terrible.’’’ In his 45-minute extemporaneous speech, Mr. Khrushchev gave his third clarification of his remarks last week about Soviet weaponry. He was quoted then as saying the Soviet Union had a ‘‘terrible new weapon.’’ Today he said he had been misquoted in the ‘‘bourgeois press.’’ — New York Herald Tribune, European Edition, Sept. 21, 1964', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '1964: Khrushchev to Help Africans', 'kicker': 'IHT Retrospective', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Khrushchev, Nikita S', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'AFRICA', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'USSR (Former Soviet Union)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-09-19T15:38:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'byline': {'original': 'By International Herald Tribune', 'person': [{'firstname': 'International', 'middlename': 'Herald', 'lastname': 'Tribune', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organi