, 'byline': {'original': 'By Rachel Lee Harris', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Rachel', 'middlename': 'Lee', 'lastname': 'Harris', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/006b14e8-9759-53d3-8e33-0b5eb5b1852a', 'word_count': 291, 'uri': 'nyt://article/006b14e8-9759-53d3-8e33-0b5eb5b1852a'}, {'abstract': 'Karen Washington of the Bronx went from novice gardener to urban farming pioneer.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/09/21/realestate/urban-farmings-grande-dame-karen-washington.html', 'snippet': 'Karen Washington of the Bronx went from novice gardener to urban farming pioneer.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Karen Washington, a community activist who has been called “urban farming’s de facto godmother,” found her bliss when she moved to the Bronx nearly 30 years ago and began growing vegetables in her backyard. Gardening was not part of her heritage. ', 'print_section': 'RE', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Believer in Vacant Lots', 'kicker': 'What I Love', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Believer in Vacant Lots', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Gardens and Gardening', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Real Estate and Housing (Residential)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Bronx (NYC)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Beard, James, Foundation', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Awards, Decorations and Honors', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-09-19T15:47:19+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'RealEstate', 'section_name': 'Real Estate', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dan Shaw', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Shaw', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b951f050-8fef-5d4a-8453-333c04c54d5e', 'word_count': 1223, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b951f050-8fef-5d4a-8453-333c04c54d5e'}, {'abstract': 'There’s plenty of life left in the aging protagonists of Margaret Atwood’s “tales.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/09/21/books/review/stone-mattress-by-margaret-atwood.html', 'snippet': 'There’s plenty of life left in the aging protagonists of Margaret Atwood’s “tales.”', 'lead_paragraph': '“Stone Mattress,” Margaret Atwood’s first collection since “Moral Disorder” in 2006, begins with three linked stories about women who have been romantically involved with a middling poet named Gavin Putnam. Constance, the protagonist of the first, has become a fantasy novelist in the decades since their relationship ended (she calls herself C. W. Starr, in the tradition of J. K. Rowling and P. D. James), famous for an invented world called Alphinland that she claims Gavin never understood: “It was a dangerous place, and — granted — preposterous in some ways, but it was not sordid. The denizens of it had standards. They understood gallantry, and courage, and also revenge.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/21/books/review/0921-bks-BELL/0921-bks-BELL-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 684, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/09/21/books/review/0921-bks-BELL/0921-bks-BELL-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 684}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/21/books/review/0921-bks-BELL/0921-bks-BELL-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 898, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/21/books/review/0921-bks-BELL/0921-bks-BELL-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1796, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/21/books/review/0921-bks-BELL/0921-bks-BELL-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/09/21/books/review/0921-bks-BELL/0921-bks-BELL-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/21/books/review/0921-bks-BELL/0921-bks-BELL-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Not Dead Yet', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Not Dead Yet', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Atwood, Margaret', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-09-19T15:47:43+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Matt Bell', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Matt', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bell', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/f259655c-8335-56df-8763-56248272939a', 'word_count': 1142, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f259655c-8335-56df-8763-56248272939a'}, {'abstract': 'An Irish woman leaves behind a sick brother and an abusive home life in this coming-of-age novel.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/09/21/books/review/a-girl-is-a-half-formed-thing-by-eimear-mcbride.html', 'snippet': 'An Irish woman leaves behind a sick brother and an abusive home life in this coming-of-age novel.', 'lead_paragraph': 'God alone might’ve written the \xadBible, but the Irish provided the punctuation. While the Roman Empire \xadcollapsed into the vernacular across the European continent, monks in the cold stone \xadmonasteries of that cold stone island hand-copied \xadLatin and Greek versions of Scripture. Their “manuscripts” revived \xadantiquity’s systems of notation, resurrecting spaces between words and pauses between thoughts, and turned the Flesh of body text into the Word of divine \xadrevelation through the sacrament of quotation. The first thing God says to the first man, Adam, is, Don’t pick the fruit from a certain tree. The second thing God says is that the man must have a partner, and so He puts Adam to sleep and removes a rib He turns into a woman: a half-formed thing, a dependent.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/21/books/review/21cohen/21cohen-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 905, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/09/21/books/review/21cohen/21cohen-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 905}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/21/books/review/21cohen/21cohen-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 679, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/21/books/review/21cohen/21cohen-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1779, 'width': 1179, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/21/books/review/21cohen/21cohen-