2014-09-19T15:52:21+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Books', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By MARIA RUSSO', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Maria', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'RUSSO', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Slideshow', '_id': 'nyt://slideshow/171b6341-7257-5559-82d9-da418cffd8d9', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://slideshow/171b6341-7257-5559-82d9-da418cffd8d9'}, {'abstract': 'The etiquette of borrowing, recommending and reshelving books.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/09/21/books/review/be-polite-with-your-books.html', 'snippet': 'The etiquette of borrowing, recommending and reshelving books.', 'lead_paragraph': 'What is the etiquette anyway regarding the making of a literary reference in casual conversation? Say, for instance, you want to refer to your newborn’s recent gastric exclamation as an “airborne toxic event.” The allusion is self-contained enough — i.e., your audience will be amused even without knowing its provenance — that you don’t need to footnote it with “DeLillo. ‘White Noise.’ ”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '25', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/21/books/review/0921-bks-ALFORD-1/0921-bks-ALFORD-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 383, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/09/21/books/review/0921-bks-ALFORD-1/0921-bks-ALFORD-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 383}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/21/books/review/0921-bks-ALFORD-1/0921-bks-ALFORD-1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 653, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/21/books/review/0921-bks-ALFORD-1/0921-bks-ALFORD-1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 892, 'width': 1399, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/21/books/review/0921-bks-ALFORD-1/0921-bks-ALFORD-1-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/09/21/books/review/0921-bks-ALFORD-1/0921-bks-ALFORD-1-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/21/books/review/0921-bks-ALFORD-1/0921-bks-ALFORD-1-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Be Polite With Your Books', 'kicker': 'Author’s Note', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Be Polite With Your Books', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Alford, Henry', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Writing and Writers', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-09-19T15:52:47+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Henry Alford', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Henry', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Alford', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c9e560c7-b1ba-559c-b8d4-8c37a10e6601', 'word_count': 1062, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c9e560c7-b1ba-559c-b8d4-8c37a10e6601'}, {'abstract': 'A child psychologist is troubled by her own adolescence.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/09/21/books/review/margaret-forsters-unknown-bridesmaid.html', 'snippet': 'A child psychologist is troubled by her own adolescence.', 'lead_paragraph': 'There may be no bigger mystery to a child than her own family — those adults who pretend not to notice even the most glaring issues, who make peculiar requests and then refuse to explain a thing. For Julia, the central character in Margaret Forster’s psychological puzzle-box of a novel, even basic factual information can be mystifying. Were her actions as a girl harmless or grave? She herself doesn’t know, and the uncertainty colors the rest of her life. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '19', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/09/books/review/21wildgen/21wildgen-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 972, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/09/09/books/review/21wildgen/21wildgen-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 972}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/09/books/review/21wildgen/21wildgen-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 633, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/09/books/review/21wildgen/21wildgen-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1568, 'width': 968, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/09/books/review/21wildgen/21wildgen-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/09/09/books/review/21wildgen/21wildgen-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/09/books/review/21wildgen/21wildgen-thumbLarge-v3.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Past Treatment', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Past Treatment', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Forster, Margaret', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-09-19T15:53:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michelle Wildgen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michelle', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Wildgen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/a5c76610-4aa6-5835-aa96-d5473ae6b32d', 'word_count': 648, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a5c76610-4aa6-5835-aa96-d5473ae6b32d'}, {'abstract': 'Disguised as a man, Laird Hunt’s heroine joins the Union Army.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/09/21/books/review/neverhome-by-laird-hunt.html', 'snippet': 'Disguised as a man, Laird Hunt’s heroine joins the Union Army.', 'lead_paragraph': 'How do you solve a problem like Ash Thompson? The heroine of “Neverhome,” Laird Hunt’s enthralling new novel set during the Civil War, is at once sentimental and aloof, a savior and a killer, a folk hero who shuns her own legend, a fierce and wounded woman who finds strength in her troubled past. Above all she is a liar, but one whose every word has the sheen of truth. “I was strong and he was not,” she begins her tale, “so it was me went to war to defend the Republic.” Yet it becomes clear that Ash is hardly motivated by patriotism, and that an equally devastating war awaits her return home.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '19', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/06/books/review/21ABBOTT/21ABBOTT-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 876, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/09/06/books/review/21ABBOTT/21ABBOTT-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 876}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/06/books/review/21ABBOTT/21ABBOTT-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 702, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/06/books/review/21ABBOTT/21ABBOTT-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1028, 'width': 704, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/06/books/review/21ABBOTT/21ABBOTT-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/09/06/books/review/21ABBOTT/21ABBOTT-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/06/books/review/21ABBOTT/21ABBOTT-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'She Wears the Britches', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'She Wears the Britches', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hunt, Laird', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-09-19T15:53:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Karen Abbott', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Karen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Abbott', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/ab2fe597-b79a-53da-80c7-1f19e0a358ab', 'word_count': 717, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ab2fe597-b79a-53da-80c7-1f19e0a358ab'}, {'abstract': 'A short video and photographs documenting indiscriminate bombing in Aleppo, Syria, prompt reflections of how we experience war from a distance, through images or in words.', 'web_url': 'https://op-talk.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/09/19/this-is-what-a-city-under-bombardment-looks-like/', 'snippet': 'A short video and photographs documenting indiscriminate bombing in Aleppo, Syria, prompt reflections of how we experience war from a distance, through images or in words.', 'lead_paragraph': '“Who believes today that war can be abolished? No one, not even pacifists,” writes Susan Sontag in a\xa02002 essay for The New Yorker, “Looking at War.” “We hope only (so far in vain) to stop genocide and bring to justice those who commit gross violations of the laws of war.” We seek, too, to stem the suffering of those caught in war’s cross hairs.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'This Is What a City Under Bombardment Looks Like', 'kicker': 'Op-Talk', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Bombs and Explosives', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Emergency Medical Treatment', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Photography', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Assad, Bashar al-', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sontag, Susan', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Free Syrian Army', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Coalition of Syrian Revolutionary and Opposition Forces', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'ALEPPO (SYRIA)', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Syria', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-09-19T15:56:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Anna Altman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Anna', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Altman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ce4bf72a-f03c-543d-b160-db3ac18bcba7', 'word_count': 711, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ce4bf72a-f03c-543d-b160-db3ac18bcba7'}, {'abstract': 'Swallowing their fears, these adventuresome characters set off down unfamiliar streets and explore unknown worlds.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/09/21/books/review/anthony-brownes-what-if-and-more.html', 'snippet': 'Swallowing their fears, these adventuresome characters set off down unfamiliar streets and explore unknown worlds.', 'lead_paragraph': '“Someone goes on a journey” is one of the guiding precepts of narrative; “Are we there yet?” is one of the guiding precepts of childhood. Picture books must inhabit the middle of this Venn diagram, offering stories as scenic and driving as any novel, at a pace more novel than driving through scenery. Four new package deals have come across my desk; let’s see what we can book.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/21/books/review/0921-bks-HAMDLER-1/0921-bks-HAMDLER-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 468, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/09/21/books/review/0921-bks-HAMDLER-1/0921-bks-HAMDLER-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 468}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/21/books/review/0921-bks-HAMDLER-1/0921-bks-HAMDLER-1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 798, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/21/books/review/0921-bks-HAMDLER-1/0921-bks-HAMDLER-1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1596, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/21/books/review/0921-bks-HAMDLER-1/0921-bks-HAMDLER-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/09/21/books/review/0921-bks-HAMDLER-1/0921-bks-HAMDLER-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/21/books/review/0921-bks-HAMDLER-1/0921-bks-HAMDLER-1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Walks on the Wild Side', 'kicker': "Children's Books", 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Walks on the Wild Side', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Browne, Anthony', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Badescu, Ramona', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Chaud, Benjamin', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Stead, Philip Christian', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Becker, Aaron (1974- )', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-09-19T15:58:37+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Daniel Handler', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Daniel', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Handler', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/95415220-ea17-51a4-aaa8-e096524b0695', 'word_count': 1072, 'uri': 'nyt://article/95415220-ea17-51a4-aaa8-e096524b0695'}, {'abstract': 'Students at a therapeutic boarding school discover a way to return to the past, before their personal traumas.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/09/21/books/review/belzhar-by-meg-wolitzer.html', 'snippet': 'Students at a therapeutic boarding school discover a way to return to the past, before their personal traumas.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Early in Meg Wolitzer’s acclaimed novel “The Interestings,” two boys at summer camp indulge in unlikely late-night mischief when, by the glow of their flashlights, they sneak passages of “All the President’s Men.” Wolitzer’s characters are often wildly bookish. In her first novel, “Sleepwalking,” three “death girls” worship Sylvia Plath, Anne Sexton and another, invented, suicidal poet; in “The Uncoupling” sex lives are undone by a high school production of “Lysistrata”; and in “The Wife,” the heroine marries her college creative writing professor. In “Belzhar,” her second novel for young readers — after the ingenious “The Fingertips of Duncan Dorfman,” a middle-grade book — Wolitzer tells a flashlight-worthy story that illuminates some dark corners of adolescent desire.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '15', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Secret Histories', 'kicker': "Children's Books", 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Secret Histories', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wolitzer, Meg', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-09-19T15:58:47+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Amber Dermont', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Amber', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Dermont', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/f0520efb-0d7e-5bf8-af42-b9e18c5f0484', 'word_count': 678, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f0520efb-0d7e-5bf8-af42-b9e18c5f0484'}, {'abstract': 'In this middle-grade novel, a girl finds a way forward after the loss of her mother.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/09/21/books/review/nest-by-esther-ehrlich.html', 'snippet': 'In this middle-grade novel, a girl finds a way forward after the loss of her mother.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A book called “Nest” with a main character called Chirp might seem almost too cute. But Esther Ehrlich’s debut middle-grade novel is anything but saccharine; in fact, its ambition is to be rigorously realistic about tragedy in a child’s life. That so grave a work would festoon itself with whimsy speaks to the book’s virtues and its potential limitations. This is a book to take seriously, that has a lot to say. But is it for the child readers it’s targeting?', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '15', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Rare Bird', 'kicker': "Children's Books", 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Rare Bird', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ehrlich, Esther', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-09-19T15:59:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Priscilla Gilman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Priscilla', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gilman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/1de32148-c631-5679-8d17-d425c76e19e2', 'word_count': 696, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1de32148-c631-5679-8d17-d425c76e19e2'}, {'abstract': 'A picture book about an African-American regiment in World War I that confronted racists as well as the German Army.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/09/21/books/review/harlem-hellfighters-by-j-patrick-lewis-and-gary-kelley.html', 'snippet': 'A picture book about an African-American regiment in World War I that confronted racists as well as the German Army.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On Feb. 17, 1919, the 369th Infantry Regiment returned to New York from France. Under a bright sun and cloudless sky, before a crowd of 250,000, the most decorated \xadAfrican-American combat unit to serve in World War I marched under the newly erected Victory Arch at 23rd Street and up Fifth Avenue. White observers were awed by the spectacle of some 3,000 black soldiers in French helmets, bayonets gleaming, parading in disciplined lock step formation, while black residents flooded the streets and cheered wildly, welcoming home their loved ones and heroes. Capped by this extraordinary moment, the 369th was etched in history as the Harlem Hellfighters.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Waging Two Wars', 'kicker': "Children's Books", 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Waging Two Wars', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lewis, J Patrick', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kelley, Gary', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-09-19T15:59:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Chad Williams', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Chad', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Williams', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/7dd9d3d2-4c58-58ad-a927-bd212df2be46', 'word_count': 681, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7dd9d3d2-4c58-58ad-a927-bd212df2be46'}, {'abstract': 'On Sept. 25 at 3 p.m. I will be holding another “AMA” (Ask Me Anything) on reddit. I’ll be taking questions on my new book with Sheryl WuDunn, “A Path Appears,” on how to make a difference, as well as anything else you care to ask me about.', 'web_url': 'https://kristof.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/09/19/my-reddit-ama-put-me-on-the-spot/', 'snippet': 'On Sept. 25 at 3 p.m. I will be holding another “AMA” (Ask Me Anything) on reddit. I’ll be taking questions on my new book with Sheryl WuDunn, “A Path Appears,” on how to make a difference, as well as anything else you care to ask me about.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Next week I’ll hold another “AMA” (Ask Me Anything) on reddit. I’ll be taking questions on my new book with Sheryl WuDunn, “A Path Appears,” on how to make a difference, as well as anything else you care to ask me about. (The book comes out Sept. 23, so if you order now and speed read, you can ask me about stories in the book.)', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/19/opinion/19kristof_reddit/19kristof_reddit-articleLarge-v3.jpg', 'height': 403, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/09/19/opinion/19kristof_reddit/19kristof_reddit-articleLarge-v3.jpg', 'xlar