position was peculiar, no question. Hearing Christian Tetzlaff play Bach’s sonatas and partitas for unaccompanied violin, some of the most intellectually stimulating and emotionally fulfilling music ever written, on Thursday evening, I kept thinking of a child’s coloring book. Bach drew those wildly imaginative and fanciful but spare lines of melody and counterpoint, leaving it to the player to add color.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Bach, Played in Living Color', 'kicker': 'Music Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Bach, Played in Living Color', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': '92nd Street Y', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Tetzlaff, Christian', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Classical Music', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-09-19T20:51:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By James R. Oestreich', 'person': [{'firstname': 'James', 'middlename': 'R.', 'lastname': 'Oestreich', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/379846f9-246c-5efd-ad58-d85e523c6cb3', 'word_count': 450, 'uri': 'nyt://article/379846f9-246c-5efd-ad58-d85e523c6cb3'}, {'abstract': 'I had to stop hating my abuser to start loving myself. I had to let go of my past so that I could step into my future.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/09/21/opinion/sunday/charles-blow-up-from-pain.html', 'snippet': 'I had to stop hating my abuser to start loving myself. I had to let go of my past so that I could step into my future.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I WAS away at college doing much of nothing, just pushing bac