ame': 'Ryzik', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4084adab-b104-5785-9e1d-83e58506f133', 'word_count': 670, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4084adab-b104-5785-9e1d-83e58506f133'}, {'abstract': 'While early concepts for this week’s cover included portraits of Hart in Red Rocks, Colo., where he announced his presidential candidacy in 1987, the art direction quickly shifted to include images of other politicians brought down by scandals.', 'web_url': 'https://6thfloor.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/09/19/under-cover-gary-hart-first-of-many/', 'snippet': 'While early concepts for this week’s cover included portraits of Hart in Red Rocks, Colo., where he announced his presidential candidacy in 1987, the art direction quickly shifted to include images of other politicians brought down by scandals.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the Sept. 21 cover story, Matt Bai writes about Gary Hart, a onetime shoe-in for the Democratic presidential nomination whose political career came to an end when The Miami Herald reported that he was having an affair. While early cover concepts included portraits of Hart in Red Rocks, Colo., where he announced his presidential bid in 1987, the art direction quickly shifted to include images of other politicians brought down by scandals — because, as Gail Bichler, the magazine’s design director, points out, “Bai’s story really deals with the media crossing a line for the first time and making a politician’s personal indiscretions into news.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/21/magazine/21cover_type/21cover_type-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 730, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/09/21/magazine/21cover_type/21cover_type-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheig