ent_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/95148674-94b3-53ad-800b-fcd7452e5534', 'word_count': 367, 'uri': 'nyt://article/95148674-94b3-53ad-800b-fcd7452e5534'}, {'abstract': 'Scenes from the Milan Fashion Week photo diary of Alfredo Piola.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/09/21/milan-fashion-week-marni-springsummer-2015/', 'snippet': 'Scenes from the Milan Fashion Week photo diary of Alfredo Piola.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Scenes from the Milan Fashion Week photo diary of Alfredo Piola.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Milan Fashion Week: Marni Spring/Summer 2015', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fashion and Apparel', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Milan Fashion Week', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Marni Group', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-09-21T13:40:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'TStyle', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alfredo Piola', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alfredo', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Piola', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9372cf37-e6e3-5188-a90f-ac10a045dbaa', 'word_count': 34, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9372cf37-e6e3-5188-a90f-ac10a045dbaa'}, {'abstract': 'From the International Herald Tribune archives: France accused Germany of partition plans in 1939.', 'web_url': 'https://iht-retrospective.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/09/21/1939-reich-maps-show-france-partitioned-says-daladier/', 'snippet': 'From the International Herald Tribune archives: France accused Germany of partition plans in 1939.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In a radio speech, Premier Edouard Daladier stated maps showing the dismemberment of France had already been printed in Germany, thus proving that Hitler’s assertion that he wanted nothing from France was as false as his past statements regarding Austria, Czecho-Slovakia and Poland. He refuted Hitler’s attempt to disclaim war responsibility, said it was futile for Germany to try to dissociate France and England, and declared French soldiers would fight to the end to crush the system of threats and alarms. A Radio Agency report from Brussels said some dikes of the Albert Canal had been opened to cause floods between Antwerp and Liège, thus making it impossible for an enemy army to pass the north and northeastern frontiers with offensive weapons. Prime Minister Neville Chamberlain said England’s war budget would be presented to the House of Commons Wednesday. German radio stations started anti-French broadcasts. After a prolonged silence, Propaganda Minister Josef Goebbels summoned newspaper men to his office to tell them Germany did not intend to invade Holland or Belgium and to deny that there had been riots in Slovakia. — New York Herald Tribune, European Edition, Sept. 22, 1939', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '1939: Reich Maps Show France Partitioned, Says Daladier', 'kicker': 'IHT Retrospective', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Defense and Military Forces', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'World War II (1939-45)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hitler, Adolf', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'France', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-09-21T13:56:43+0000', 'docu