mothy (1963- )', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-09-21T20:15:24+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Anthony Tommasini', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Anthony', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Tommasini', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/1b732751-4ed8-53b4-8382-deda2dfca1a9', 'word_count': 561, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1b732751-4ed8-53b4-8382-deda2dfca1a9'}, {'abstract': 'In “I-Cure,” the choreographer Ivo Dimchev pushes taboos to make the audience ponder the nature of healing and other issues.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/09/22/arts/dance/i-cure-provokes-at-the-queer-new-york-arts-festival.html', 'snippet': 'In “I-Cure,” the choreographer Ivo Dimchev pushes taboos to make the audience ponder the nature of healing and other issues.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When a choreographer like Ivo Dimchev proposes a performance work intended to promote healing, you can’t help feeling suspicion. It’s the good kind. This Bulgarian artist, based in Brussels, has a persuasive subversive streak. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/22/arts/DIMCHEV/DIMCHEV-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 592, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/09/22/arts/DIMCHEV/DIMCHEV-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 592}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/22/arts/DIMCHEV/DIMCHEV-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1010, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', '