rticle/78e94024-a9b4-59c5-8ec9-49d3f17b8f2c', 'word_count': 176, 'uri': 'nyt://article/78e94024-a9b4-59c5-8ec9-49d3f17b8f2c'}, {'abstract': 'As the reclusive genius Richard D. James releases his first album in 13 years, the graphic designer with whom he has frequently collaborated discusses their work together.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/09/22/t-magazine/aphex-twin-syro-record-designers-republic-ian-anderson.html', 'snippet': 'As the reclusive genius Richard D. James releases his first album in 13 years, the graphic designer with whom he has frequently collaborated discusses their work together.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Ever since a distinctive three-armed logo appeared in the early ’90s on the sleeves of the albums of Aphex Twin — the alias of the prolific electronic music producer Richard D. James — it has remained a vital emblem for dance music fans around the world. So when a lime-green blimp bearing that insignia appeared over London (and mysterious stencils of it appeared on sidewalks around Manhattan and Brooklyn) back in mid-August, electronic music fans rejoiced, knowing that after 13 years, a new Aphex Twin album was coming. Since his disappearance from public view, James’s influence has only grown: His example led Radiohead to embrace electronic music throughout the aughts, he’s been sampled by Kanye West and the electronic dance music poster boy Skrillex has called the Aphex Twin track “Flim” his favorite of all time.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/22/t-magazine/22aphex-hartman-slide-YC4I/22aphex-hartman-slide-YC4I-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 450, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/09/22/t-magazine/22aphex-hartman-slide-YC4I/22aphex-hartman-slide-YC4I-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 450}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': Non