ns', 'value': 'Federal Reserve Bank of Dallas', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Economy', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Quantitative Easing', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Banking and Financial Institutions', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-09-22T16:00:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Binyamin Appelbaum', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Binyamin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Appelbaum', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d802f7dc-4833-546a-8bad-0d82e8012cd5', 'word_count': 938, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d802f7dc-4833-546a-8bad-0d82e8012cd5'}, {'abstract': 'How to decide where best to board your train, in a puzzle from Gary Hewitt.', 'web_url': 'https://wordplay.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/09/22/train-2/', 'snippet': 'How to decide where best to board your train, in a puzzle from Gary Hewitt.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Before we get to this week’s puzzle — several days ago I asked Donald Knuth, the legendary computer scientist currently at Stanford University, how he would have solved our recent Bugs on a Square problem, which is a classic among software engineers. He responded by saying he would have worked the problem out with algebra (and not a simulation, which surprised me). And then he followed with a fitting analogy for the kind of puttering that defines Numberplay.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Gary Hewitt’s Train Puzzle', 'kicker': 'Wordplay', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Creativity', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value