'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-09-22T18:26:34+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Home', 'section_name': 'Home & Garden', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alexandra Lange', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alexandra', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Lange', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Question', '_id': 'nyt://article/f9b57212-ef69-5e0e-a92f-f5ea02fb20ec', 'word_count': 968, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f9b57212-ef69-5e0e-a92f-f5ea02fb20ec'}, {'abstract': 'Tennessee law forbids combining a mother and father’s surnames into a single name for baby — or giving an infant a mother’s last name without both parents’ “mutual assent.”', 'web_url': 'https://parenting.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/09/22/weekly-quandary-whose-last-name/', 'snippet': 'Tennessee law forbids combining a mother and father’s surnames into a single name for baby — or giving an infant a mother’s last name without both parents’ “mutual assent.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the Weekly Quandary, we pull from the comments, follow up on the weekly open thread or email a question that we know (or at least suspect) plagues more than one parent. You — the readers — provide the advice: How have you made this work better in your family? This week’s quandary came via email, but it was an email with a specific source: an article about a couple in Tennessee, Kim Sarubbi and Carl Abramson, who combined their surnames to give their first two children a single, blended last name instead of a hyphenated one. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/22/us/motherlode-birth-certificate/motherlode-birth-certificate-jumbo.png', 'height': 366, 'width': 540, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/22/us/motherlo