ject', 'value': 'Salad Dressings', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Salads', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-09-22T20:02:12+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Dining', 'section_name': 'Food', 'byline': {'original': 'By Julia Moskin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Julia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Moskin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/91c5acac-8a64-5add-849f-f5ae9d166592', 'word_count': 554, 'uri': 'nyt://article/91c5acac-8a64-5add-849f-f5ae9d166592'}, {'abstract': 'Three books — one optimistic, one cataclysmic and one polemic — discuss what climate change might bring.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/09/23/science/three-divergent-visions-of-our-future-under-climate-change.html', 'snippet': 'Three books — one optimistic, one cataclysmic and one polemic — discuss what climate change might bring.', 'lead_paragraph': 'First, some cheerful news from the Future of Western Civilization Department: Cliff swallows in the Great Plains are evolving to have shorter wings. This helps them to swerve more easily away from speeding cars on the interstate. “Over time,” a behavioral ecologist at the University of Tulsa says, “we’ve begun to notice that we were seeing fewer dead birds on the roads.”', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/23/science/23SCIB1/23SCIB1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 764, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/09/23/science/23SCIB1/23SCIB1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 764}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/23/science/23SCIB1/23SCIB1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024,