29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Theater', 'byline': {'original': 'By BEN BRANTLEY', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ben', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'BRANTLEY', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/294ab42f-e0f5-5142-a609-c51235b7a517', 'word_count': 962, 'uri': 'nyt://article/294ab42f-e0f5-5142-a609-c51235b7a517'}, {'abstract': 'Television highlights.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/09/23/arts/television/whats-on-tv-tuesday.html', 'snippet': 'Television highlights.', 'lead_paragraph': '9 P.M. (13, 49) AMERICAN MASTERS: THE BOOMER LIST Timothy Greenfield-Sanders, above with Amy Tan, traverses the baby boomer generation with the help of 19 celebrities — one born each year, from 1946 to 1964 — as the last members turn 50. Subjects including Samuel L. Jackson (born in 1948), Billy Joel (1949), Ms. Tan (1952), Eve Ensler (1953), Kim Cattrall (1956), Rosie O’Donnell (1962) and John Leguizamo (1964) discuss their achievements, struggles and identities, as well as the movements that have shaped their lives in the environment, civil rights, politics, technology and the arts. In “Joan Rivers: A Piece of Work” (2010), at 10:30 on Channel 13, Ricki Stern and Annie Sundberg “tagged along with their subject, racking up miles and yowzah moments in her relentless pursuit of fame, money, attention, love, kicks, masochistic thrills or whatever it is that makes Ms. Rivers, née Joan Alexandra Molinsky, run,” Manohla Dargis wrote in The New York Times. Following Ms. Rivers the year she turned 75 (she died on Sept. 4 at 81), they begin by moving in close to her cosmetically altered face — without makeup. “To their credit,” Ms. Dargis said, “the filmmakers don’t try to make her look good, and while they omit some of her uglier routines, they don’t (perhaps can’t) ask you to love her.”', 'print_section': 'C', 