inal': 'By Vanessa Friedman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Vanessa', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Friedman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/22bf7b61-5a20-5d76-8e04-5a186c91eb49', 'word_count': 629, 'uri': 'nyt://article/22bf7b61-5a20-5d76-8e04-5a186c91eb49'}, {'abstract': 'Wall Street investors are bankrolling A.I.G.’s former chief executive in his suit over the government’s bailout. | New inversion rules may lack bite. | Citizens Financial Group is making its public market debut. | In defense of hedge funds.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2014/09/24/morning-agenda-wall-st-helps-out-a-i-g-s-ex-chief/', 'snippet': 'Wall Street investors are bankrolling A.I.G.’s former chief executive in his suit over the government’s bailout. | New inversion rules may lack bite. | Citizens Financial Group is making its public market debut. | In defense of hedge funds.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WALL ST. BANKROLLS A.I.G.’S EX-CHIEF | Maurice R. Greenberg, the former chief executive of the American International Group, is getting by with a little help from his friends. To help cover the cost of a lawsuit that accuses the government of shortchanging A.I.G. in its 2008 bailout, Mr. Greenberg has raised several million dollars from three Wall Street companions, Ben Protess and Aaron M. Kessler write in DealBook. The investors, who are entitled to a cut of any damages Mr. Greenberg collects from the government, are said to have contributed about 15 percent of the tens of millions of dollars in legal costs. The lawsuit is set to go to trial next week in Washington.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Morning Agenda: Wall St. Helps Out A.I.G.’s Ex-Chief', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2014-09-24T11:30:12+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sydney Ember', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sydney', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ember', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5ebd569f-b52d-5518-b82d-8fa4ead1c2e6', 'word_count': 2096, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5ebd569f-b52d-5518-b82d-8fa4ead1c2e6'}, {'abstract': 'Highlights from the International Herald Tribune archives: German troops are moving in the Belgian towns of Antwerp and Liege in 1914.', 'web_url': 'https://iht-retrospective.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/09/24/1914-germans-bring-up-guns/', 'snippet': 'Highlights from the International Herald Tribune archives: German troops are moving in the Belgian towns of Antwerp and Liege in 1914.', 'lead_paragraph': 'ANTWERP — There is information from a reliable source to the effect that, in spite of the very heavy rain, the Germans are bringing up toward Antwerp siege guns with a range of at least seventeen miles. It is also stated on good authority that there has been during the past few weeks a movement of German troops at Liege. Opinions are divided as to whether these are going to Russia or coming to reinforce the Germans in Brussels. Antwerp is provisioned to stand a twelve months’ siege. Cattle has been arriving from America at the rate of 3,000 head a week for the past six weeks. Fearing that the Germans will cut off supplies from Ostend, the Belgians had several thousand head of cattle and many thousand pigs placed in pens in the fields surrounding every town.\xa0— The New York Herald, European Edition, Sept. 25, 1914\xa0', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/25/blogs/25iht-oldsept25-antwerp/25iht-oldsept25-antwerp-jumbo.png', 'height': 478, 'width': 266, 'subType': 'jumbo', 