ot', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nyt', 'middlename': 'Down', 'lastname': 'Bot', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/bcbd3f37-b99d-53ac-af95-e22e9848ab85', 'word_count': 388, 'uri': 'nyt://article/bcbd3f37-b99d-53ac-af95-e22e9848ab85'}, {'abstract': 'What’s next after a contentious reaction to a TV article and its focus on race.', 'web_url': 'https://publiceditor.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/09/24/shonda-rhimes-alessandra-stanley-margaret-sullivan-column/', 'snippet': 'What’s next after a contentious reaction to a TV article and its focus on race.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In more than two years as public editor at The Times, I’ve encountered very few subjects that have aroused as much passion and reaction as an Arts & Leisure article about the TV producer Shonda Rhimes and the stereotype of the “Angry Black Woman” the story leaned on. I wrote about it on Monday, and quickly hundreds of readers responded in deeply felt comments; my email has been overflowing with more commentary; and many people I know, inside and outside The Times, have been eager to talk about it. And other writers (Emily Nussbaum at The New Yorker, Linda Holmes at NPR, and many others) have taken it up.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Diversity, Strong Editing and Moving Forward From the Shonda Rhimes Furor', 'kicker': 'The Public Editor&#039;s Journal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'News and News Media', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Public Editor (Times Column)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Race and Ethnicity', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Baquet, Dean', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rhimes, Shonda', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Stanley, Alessandra', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-09-24T18:53:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Margaret Sullivan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Margaret', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sullivan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c82c9a03-7ed0-54ad-80fe-7a71b2d1054c', 'word_count': 1267, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c82c9a03-7ed0-54ad-80fe-7a71b2d1054c'}, {'abstract': 'Alan Howard, founder of the Brevan Howard hedge fund, donated $34.9 million to finance the center. The last financial crisis was devastating, he said. “We must not allow that to happen again.”', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2014/09/24/new-british-center-seeks-to-offer-ideas-on-preventing-financial-crises/', 'snippet': 'Alan Howard, founder of the Brevan Howard hedge fund, donated $34.9 million to finance the center. The last financial crisis was devastating, he said. “We must not allow that to happen again.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'LONDON — The list of remedies to prevent a future financial calamity is long, and depending how closely you look, complicated. Banks must hold more capital, the system must have less risk and more rules now govern more institutions.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'New British Center Seeks to Offer Ideas on Preventing Financial Crises', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Banking and Financial Institutions', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hedge Funds', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Philanthropy', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Regulation and Deregulation of Industry', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', '