'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Books', 'byline': {'original': 'By John Williams', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Williams', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/06c5c427-0943-5eb5-86df-767b8649c98b', 'word_count': 966, 'uri': 'nyt://article/06c5c427-0943-5eb5-86df-767b8649c98b'}, {'abstract': 'The pianist Inon Barnatan, starting a concert-packed season, gave a thoughtful recital at SubCulture on Tuesday.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/09/25/arts/music/inon-barnatan-at-subcultures-pianofest.html', 'snippet': 'The pianist Inon Barnatan, starting a concert-packed season, gave a thoughtful recital at SubCulture on Tuesday.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Inon Barnatan is ubiquitous this season. An Israeli-born New Yorker, Mr. Barnatan is the New York Philharmonic’s first “artist in association.” So he’s at the piano for a chamber recital with Philharmonic instrumentalists in December, and makes his full Philharmonic debut in March. He’s a guest artist with the Chamber Music Society of Lincoln Center, with a concert in November. He’s resident pianist for the Jerusalem Quartet’s Brahms survey at the 92nd Street Y in October. And, lest downtown feel left out, he was the star attraction at SubCulture’s second PianoFest on Tuesday night.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/25/arts/25INON/25INON-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/09/25/arts/25INON/25INON-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/25/arts/25INON/25INON-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/25/arts/25INON/25INON-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/25/arts/25INON/25INON-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/09/25/arts/25INON/25INON-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/25/arts/25INON/25INON-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Multitude of Voices for a Busy Young Pianist, From Bach to Liszt to Jazz', 'kicker': 'Music Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Multitude of Voices for a Busy Young Pianist, From Bach to Liszt to Jazz', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Barnatan, Inon', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Allen, David', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'SubCulture (Manhattan, NY, Music Venue)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-09-24T21:15:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Allen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Allen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/22a389aa-5abd-5f23-b7c9-d9fb50ff32f0', 'word_count': 417, 'uri': 'nyt://article/22a389aa-5abd-5f23-b7c9-d9fb50ff32f0'}, {'abstract': 'The Philharmonic began its subscription series programs on Tuesday with the new Clarinet Concerto by Unsuk Chin and Mahler’s First Symphony.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/09/25/arts/music/new-york-philharmonic-presents-an-unsuk-chin-premiere.html', 'snippet': 'The Philharmonic began its subscription series programs on Tuesday with the new Clarinet Concerto by Unsuk Chin and Mahler’s First Symphony.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The conductor Alan Gilbert, exuding excitement, looked almost relieved to be on the stage of Avery Fisher Hall with the musicians of the New York Philharmonic on Tuesday night. The occasion was the first of the orchestra’s subscription series programs this season, offering the American premiere of Unsuk Chin’s haunting Clarinet Concerto and a formidable account of Mahler’s First Symphony.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/25/arts/philharmonic/philharmonic-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 380, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/09/25/arts/philharmonic/philharmonic-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 380}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/25/arts/philharmonic/philharmonic-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 648, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/25/arts/philharmonic/philharmonic-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1297, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/25/arts/philharmonic/philharmonic-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/09/25/arts/philharmonic/philharmonic-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/25/arts/philharmonic/philharmonic-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Music Born of Dreams, a Concerto of Many Moods', 'kicker': 'Music Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Music Born of Dreams, a Concerto of Many Moods', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Tommasini, Anthony', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Philharmonic', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Chin, Unsuk', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kriikku, Kari', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Classical Music', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-09-24T21:15:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Anthony Tommasini', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Anthony', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Tommasini', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/e6508f5b-a5d4-59e9-9ffc-f597cd791636', 'word_count': 678, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e6508f5b-a5d4-59e9-9ffc-f597cd791636'}, {'abstract': '“Made in Brooklyn Revisited,” Randy Graff’s show at 54 Below, looks back at bouncy 1960s hits and at Broadway ballads about life and dreams.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/09/25/arts/music/randy-graff-in-made-in-brooklyn-revisited-at-54-below.html', 'snippet': '“Made in Brooklyn Revisited,” Randy Graff’s show at 54 Below, looks back at bouncy 1960s hits and at Broadway ballads about life and dreams.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Randy Graff fondly remembers that halcyon time “when Brooklyn was Brooklyn.” Ms. Graff, 59, who won a Tony for her role in the 1989 Broadway musical “City of Angels,” has put together a show, “Made in Brooklyn Revisited” at 54 Below that celebrates the borough and the creative talent nurtured there before the invasion of hipsters, double-wide strollers and artisanal this and that. Ms. Graff’s grandparents came to Brooklyn from “the old country” (her words). ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Recalling Old Dreams in the Old Neighborhood', 'kicker': 'Music Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Recalling Old Dreams in the Old Neighborhood', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Graff, Randy', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': '54 Below (Manhattan, NY, Restaurant)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-09-24T21:17:57+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Stephen Holden', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Stephen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Holden', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/6d2be873-3238-516c-82dc-bac9e78180a8', 'word_count': 311, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6d2be873-3238-516c-82dc-bac9e78180a8'}, {'abstract': 'Paolo Nutini performed songs from “Caustic Love,” his current album with nods to R&B, on Tuesday at the Apollo.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/09/25/arts/music/paolo-nutini-promotes-caustic-love-at-the-apollo-theater.html', 'snippet': 'Paolo Nutini performed songs from “Caustic Love,” his current album with nods to R&B, on Tuesday at the Apollo.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Moments after he stepped onstage at the Apollo Theater on Tuesday night, Paolo Nutini was optimally unkempt. His hair had been pre-touseled, his body was tensed, and his face was contorted in concentration as he gripped his microphone and worked from croon to rasp in “Scream (Funk My Life Up).” Soon enough, one side of his shirt would come untucked from his jeans. Mr. Nutini, a Scottish songwriter, is a first-rate student of vintage American soul, and the Apollo stage was a historically apt place to prove it.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/25/arts/SUB-PAOLO/SUB-PAOLO-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/09/25/arts/SUB-PAOLO/SUB-PAOLO-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/25/arts/SUB-PAOLO/SUB-PAOLO-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/25/arts/SUB-PAOLO/SUB-PAOLO-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/25/arts/SUB-PAOLO/SUB-PAOLO-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/09/25/arts/SUB-PAOLO/SUB-PAOLO-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidt