tles, Khalid Shaikh Mohammed, the alleged mastermind of the terrorist attacks of Sept. 11, 2001, will finally be put on trial before a military commission at Guantánamo Bay, Cuba, though a trial date hasn’t yet been set. If he is convicted, as expected, he will almost certainly face the death penalty. And, assuming one believes in the death penalty, it would be hard to think of a stronger candidate for its use.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '31', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Don’t Execute Those We Tortured', 'kicker': 'Op-Ed Contributor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Don’t Execute Those We Tortured', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mohammed, Khalid Shaikh', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hafetz, Jonathan', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Capital Punishment', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Torture', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'September 11 (2001)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Detainees', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Human Rights and Human Rights Violations', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sentences (Criminal)', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Military Tribunals', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Central Intelligence Agency', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'United States', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Guantanamo Bay Naval Base (Cuba)', 'rank': 12, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 13, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bush, George W', 'rank': 14, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Padilla, Jose (1970- )', 'rank': 15, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Terrorism', 'rank': 16, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-09-24T22:03:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jonathan Hafetz', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jonathan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hafetz', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/8196aa17-205b-5377-a565-72d2c7cbad2f', 'word_count': 811, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8196aa17-205b-5377-a565-72d2c7cbad2f'}, {'abstract': 'Designs for a dollhouse by a Victorian tween have a new life in literature.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/09/25/garden/a-suffragists-start.html', 'snippet': 'Designs for a dollhouse by a Victorian tween have a new life in literature.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Birdsall Otis Edey (1872-1940) was not quite in her teens when she made a paper doll’s house from cutup magazines and catalogs. She grew up to be a suffragist and a president of the Girl Scouts of the U.S.A., wrote plays and poetry, and built a theater that seated 150 on her property in Bellport, N.Y. But it was the doll house collages that impressed Eric Boman when he saw an exhibition about Ms. Edey’s life at the Bellport-Brookhaven Historical Society. Mr. Boman, a photographer and writer, turned the artwork into a book, “The Paper Doll’s House of Miss Sarah Elizabeth Birdsall Otis, Aged Twelve,” which will be published next month (Thames & Hudson, $30). If Mrs. Edey had followed the path suggested by her dream home, she might have become an interior designer whose style was elegant, playful and scaled for drama. But why did she choose collage as her mode of expression? “Girls would draw and paint a doll’s house,” Mr. Boman said, “but this girl, I think, she didn’t think her handiwork was up to snuff.” Information: thamesandhudsonusa.com.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit':