SIS Militants', 'kicker': 'United Nations General Assembly', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2014-09-25T00:10:44+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'World', 'byline': {'original': 'By Somini Sengupta', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Somini', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sengupta', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/08a04759-4e2e-54fb-8234-f54bc10f61c3', 'word_count': 738, 'uri': 'nyt://article/08a04759-4e2e-54fb-8234-f54bc10f61c3'}, {'abstract': 'Tuscany’s regional government recently approved a new rural preservation plan, saying that too many vines had been planted and warned of the risk of soil erosion.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/09/25/world/europe/in-tuscany-wine-makers-protest-soil-preservation-plan.html', 'snippet': 'Tuscany’s regional government recently approved a new rural preservation plan, saying that too many vines had been planted and warned of the risk of soil erosion.', 'lead_paragraph': 'MONTALCINO, Italy — Its gentle sloping hills, olive groves, cypresses and sunflower fields have made Tuscany a muse to writers and artists for centuries. But the region is equally renowned for its verdant vineyards and their wines, some of the most prized in Italy and the world.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/25/world/TUSCANY-1/TUSCANY-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/09/25/world/TUSCANY-1/TUSCANY-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'image