y Nicholas Kristof', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nicholas', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kristof', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/3b180c1d-1523-5102-ac12-ca7792065b94', 'word_count': 781, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3b180c1d-1523-5102-ac12-ca7792065b94'}, {'abstract': 'In a system where a primary surgeon gets paid $6,200 and an assistant gets $117,000 for work on the same operation, patients need more information in how they are going to be charged.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/09/25/opinion/warnings-on-big-medical-bills.html', 'snippet': 'In a system where a primary surgeon gets paid $6,200 and an assistant gets $117,000 for work on the same operation, patients need more information in how they are going to be charged.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It’s not an uncommon situation for patients to be billed by doctors for costly services they did not request or, in some cases, were not even aware they had received. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '30', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Warnings on Big Medical Bills', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Warnings on Big Medical Bills', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Health Insurance and Managed Care', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York State', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Doctors', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Medicine and Health', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'United States', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Law and Legislation', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-09-25T01:11:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_nam