zation': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1b937472-040e-5026-b913-14303f994404', 'word_count': 740, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1b937472-040e-5026-b913-14303f994404'}, {'abstract': 'If the country is to survive in one piece, its new leaders must act fast to correct the failures of the last decade.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/09/26/opinion/afghanistans-failed-transformation.html', 'snippet': 'If the country is to survive in one piece, its new leaders must act fast to correct the failures of the last decade.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On Sunday, after months of bitter wrangling, the two leading candidates in Afghanistan’s presidential election agreed to form a national unity government. Ashraf Ghani, a Pashtun technocrat, is to be president, and Abdullah Abdullah, a former foreign minister of mixed Tajik and Pashtun descent, is to be chief executive, a newly created post akin to prime minister. The power-sharing agreement came after an audit of the ballots cast in a June runoff, in an election widely believed to have been partially rigged. It has no basis in Afghanistan’s election law. And given the rancor that has come before, it may not hold very long.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '31', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/26/opinion/26rashid/26rashid-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 714, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/09/26/opinion/26rashid/26rashid-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 714}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/26/opinion/26rashid/26rashid-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 861, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/26/opinion/26rashid/26rashid-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1721, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/26/opinion/26rashid/26rashid-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/09/26/opinion/26rashid/26rashid-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/26/opinion/26rashid/26rashid-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Afghanistan’s Failed Transformation', 'kicker': 'Op-Ed Contributor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': "Afghanistan's Failed Transformation", 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'AFGHANISTAN', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rashid, Ahmed', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Afghanistan War (2001- )', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ghani, Ashraf', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'ABDULLAH, ABDULLAH', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Karzai, Hamid', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Politics and Government', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-09-25T11:35:37+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ahmed Rashid', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ahmed', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rashid', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/70c36a47-a7ea-5fba-b006-6ba5ed8b68fd', 'word_count': 1100, 'uri': 'nyt://article/70c36a47-a7ea-5fba-b006-6ba5ed8b68fd'}, {'abstract': 'Guangdong, China’s most populous province, has seen about 7,500 cases of dengue this year, Xinhua reported. Most years, Guangdong sees fewer than 1,000 cases of dengue, a mosquito-borne disease.', 'web_url': 'https://sinosphere.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/09/25/guangdong-sees-surge-in-dengue-fever-cases/', 'snippet': 'Guangdong, China’s most populous province, has seen about 7,500 cases of dengue this year, Xinhua reported. Most years, Guangdong sees fewer than 1,000 cases of dengue, a mosquito-borne disease.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The southern Chinese province of Guangdong has seen a surge in dengue fever cases this year, raising fears that factors such as climate change and urbanization have accelerated the spread of the mosquito-borne disease in the region.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/25/world/25sino-dengue01/25sino-dengue01-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 424, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/09/25/world/25sino-dengue01/25sino-dengue01-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 424}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/25/world/25sino-dengue01/25sino-dengue01-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 724, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/25/world/25sino-dengue01/25sino-dengue01-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1448, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/25/world/25sino-dengue01/25sino-dengue01-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType