and-the-bill-of-rights-we-deserve/', 'snippet': 'In this Text to Text, we pair the United States Bill of Rights with Sam Apple’s satirical “The Bill of Rights We Deserve.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Bill of Rights protects some of our most basic freedoms, such as the right to practice a religion, to assemble peaceably and to speak freely. Sometimes, however, our society seems to prioritize other freedoms not enshrined in the Constitution, such as the right to buy what we want, eat what we want and even binge-watch what we want. These are individual liberties that Sam Apple safeguards in his satirical “The Bill of Rights We Deserve,” which we pair with the original Bill of Rights in this Text to Text.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/12/11/us/11Bill1_cnd-LN/11Bill1_cnd-LN-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 403, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/12/11/us/11Bill1_cnd-LN/11Bill1_cnd-LN-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 403}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/12/11/us/11Bill1_cnd-LN/11Bill1_cnd-LN-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 687, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/12/11/us/11Bill1_cnd-LN/11Bill1_cnd-LN-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1375, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/12/11/us/11Bill1_cnd-LN/11Bill1_cnd-LN-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/12/11/us/11Bill1_cnd-LN/11Bill1_cnd-LN-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/12/11/us/11Bill1_cnd-LN/11Bill1_cnd-LN-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Text to Text | The Bill of Rights and ‘The Bill of Rights We Deserve’', 'kicker': 'The Learning Network', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2014-09-25T14:00:47+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael Gonchar', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gonchar', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7ff0f578-6d91-59a2-9b11-3553bbb6f02e', 'word_count': 1761, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7ff0f578-6d91-59a2-9b11-3553bbb6f02e'}, {'abstract': 'Congress will revive legislation to rein in “political intelligence gathering.”', 'web_url': 'https://takingnote.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/09/25/eric-cantor-wall-street-defender-gone-but-not-forgotten/', 'snippet': 'Congress will revive legislation to rein in “political intelligence gathering.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'There was no great ceremony when Eric Cantor departed Congress for a plush job on Wall Street this month. No need for tearful goodbyes for a politician who failed electorally but morphed into an instant multi-millionaire when a New York investment bank snapped him up as vice chairman.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Eric Cantor, Wall Street Defender, Gone But Not Forgotten', 'kicker': 'Taking Note', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Banking and Financial Institutions', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Lobbying and Lobbyists', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cantor, Eric', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-09-25T14:21:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Francis X. Clines', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Francis', 'middlename': 'X.', 'lastname': 'Clines', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/165f4fd0-a4c8-59ce-a759-e6cb3a289521', 'word_count': 380, 'uri': 'nyt://article/165f4fd0-a4c8-59ce-a759-e6cb3a289521'}, {'abstract': 'Why it’s not 1955 anymore.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/09/25/unseemly-notes/', 'snippet': 'Why it’s not 1955 anymore.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Something I didn’t know: David Brooks’s column that, among other things, lamented the failure of the wealthy to follow a “code of seemliness” was actually part of a broader complaint to that effect among conservative intellectuals. For example, Charles Murray’s “Coming Apart” is mainly about what has gone wrong with working-class values, but he devotes a fair bit of space to the “unseemly” ostentation of the elite; strange to say, he focuses on Hollywood producers rather than private equity managers, but still.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Unseemly Notes', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2014-09-25T14:24:47+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3010d47d-969b-5340-b58b-8cb9dc158325', 'word_count': 430, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3010d47d-969b-5340-b58b-8cb9dc158325'}, {'abstract': 'Charles Evans says what must be said.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/09/25/asymmetric-monetary-risks/', 'snippet': 'Charles Evans says what must be said.', 'lead_paragraph': 'How much slack is there in the U.S. labor market? Good question. The measured unemployment rate is fairly low, but labor force participation also seems low, and I have doubts about studies purporting to say that it’s overwhelmingly long-term demographics. Wage gains are still slow. My guess is that there’s considerably more slack than the unemployment number might lead you to suspect, but the truth is that I don’t know.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Asymmetric Monetary Risks', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2014-09-25T14:36:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/123f8b01-f72a-5355-80a0-637fa72861d9', 'word_count': 249, 'uri': 'nyt://article/123f8b01-f72a-5355-80a0-637fa72861d9'}, {'abstract': 'Paris', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/slideshow/2014/09/25/fashion/runway-womens/ann-demeulemeester-spring-2015-rtw.html', 'snippet': 'Paris', 'lead_paragraph': 'Paris', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/25/fashion/runway-womens/demeulemster-fashion-week-slide-734F/demeulemster-fashion-week-slide-734F-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 900, 'width': 600, 'subType