mer 2015 collection, the hairstylist Paul Hanlon created a ponytail meant to give the impression of a girl who’s just finished a run in the park.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/09/25/paris-fashion-week-spring-summer-2015-backstage-beauty-paco-rabanne-hair/', 'snippet': 'For the label’s sporty spring/summer 2015 collection, the hairstylist Paul Hanlon created a ponytail meant to give the impression of a girl who’s just finished a run in the park.', 'lead_paragraph': '“The clothes have a cool, sporty vibe, so I wanted the models’ hair to mirror that,” said the hair stylist Paul Hanlon backstage at today’s Paco Rabanne spring/summer 2015 show. The resulting creation: a low-slung, textural spin on the timeless gym-class ponytail, meant to give the impression of a girl who’s just finished her daily run in the park. “She’s strong, she’s not afraid to get outdoors and really immerse herself in the elements,” he explained, gesturing to a few small, glossy strands strewn across the forehead. Before securing lengths with a small black elastic tie, Hanlon added a mist of Toni & Guy’s Sea Salt Texturising Spray to the ends and worked Toni & Guy’s Groupie spray wax into the roots to “make the hair looked lived in and a little sweaty.” The final touch? “A quick blast of L’Oreal’s classic Elnett, and that’s it,” he said. “She’s good to go.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/25/t-magazine/25backstage-paco-rigg/25backstage-paco-rigg-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/09/25/t-magazine/25backstage-paco-rigg/25backstage-paco-rigg-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/25/t-magazine/25backstage-paco-rigg/25backstage-paco-ri