ed to run for president.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In a passive aggressive way, former President Bill Clinton recently criticized new rules from the Obama Treasury Department to curb blatant corporate tax dodging. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'In Defense of Corporate Tax Dodging', 'kicker': 'Taking Note', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2014-09-25T19:01:57+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Teresa Tritch', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Teresa', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Tritch', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8f0038e3-689c-5ebd-a9d9-c6d30d76fb62', 'word_count': 694, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8f0038e3-689c-5ebd-a9d9-c6d30d76fb62'}, {'abstract': 'Food news for Climate Week.', 'web_url': 'https://bittman.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/09/25/what-were-reading-now-20/', 'snippet': 'Food news for Climate Week.', 'lead_paragraph': ' From Manhattan to Melbourne to war-torn Aleppo, at least half a million people (and more than a few polar bears) participated in 2,700 simultaneous climate events around the world on Sunday to demand action on climate change. In New York, organizers estimate nearly 400,000 people turned out for the People’s Climate March, on the eve of the U.N. Climate Summit. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What We’re Reading Now', 'kicker': 'Mark Bittman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Global Warming', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sustainable Living', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-09-25T19:04:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mark Bittman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mark', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bittman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ff9b996f-b2c8-55e1-9c50-334b08a159ff', 'word_count': 438, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ff9b996f-b2c8-55e1-9c50-334b08a159ff'}, {'abstract': 'The Public Theater will offer free tickets through a lottery to the first performance of each main stage show in the coming season.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/09/25/public-theater-to-offer-free-tickets-by-lottery/', 'snippet': 'The Public Theater will offer free tickets through a lottery to the first performance of each main stage show in the coming season.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Public Theater announced on Thursday that it would distribute free tickets, by lottery, to the first performance of every main stage show during its 2014-2015 season. The free tickets, a program being started with the help of TodayTix, a theater ticket app, will be handed out both through a mobile lottery, which will begin a week before each preview performance, and through lotteries to be held in the lobby of the Public, at Astor Place and Lafayette Street.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Public Theater to Offer Free Tickets by Lottery', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Public Theater to Offer Free Tickets by Lottery', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Eustis, Oskar', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Public Theater', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-09-25T19:09:51+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Randy