{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New Hampshire', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-09-25T20:06:34+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Travel', 'section_name': 'Travel', 'byline': {'original': 'By Gustave Axelson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Gustave', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Axelson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/60d18c8d-f1da-56c2-8a45-541d42310137', 'word_count': 1811, 'uri': 'nyt://article/60d18c8d-f1da-56c2-8a45-541d42310137'}, {'abstract': 'In an inventive homage, Web-generated images of words from Borges’s writings will be projected on the Manhattan Bridge in Brooklyn this weekend.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/09/26/nyregion/every-word-borges-wrote-writ-large-in-brooklyn.html', 'snippet': 'In an inventive homage, Web-generated images of words from Borges’s writings will be projected on the Manhattan Bridge in Brooklyn this weekend.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In his fiction, the Argentine writer Jorge Luis Borges conceived of such poetic paradoxes as an infinite library, a single point that contains the universe, and a map of an empire at the exact scale of that empire. The artists Daniel Temkin and Rony Maltz have fashioned a clever homage to him in “Borges: The Complete Works,” part of the Dumbo Arts Festival in Brooklyn this weekend. Their piece is a twist on the word search: that staple of elementary school, in which actual words are embedded in a grid of seemingly random characters. Their 135,000-character puzzle (actually two, one for the original Spanish and another in English translation) contains every word Borges ever wrote. It can be reached via the web, where participants can find and “circle” words, using a keyboard. An image of the word search will be projected onto the Manhattan Bridge, and updated every 30 seconds to reflect newly circled words. Participation is free and open to all.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '32', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Every Word Borges Wrote, Writ Large in Brooklyn', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Every Word Borges Wrote, Writ Large in Brooklyn', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Borges, Jorge Luis', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Dumbo (Brooklyn, NY)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-09-25T20:06:34+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By A. C. Lee', 'person': [{'firstname': 'A.', 'middlename': 'C.', 'lastname': 'Lee', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/cb30f0d2-cbd4-545d-816c-c92c6ae37898', 'word_count': 423, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cb30f0d2-cbd4-545d-816c-c92c6ae37898'}, {'abstract': 'Creative minds in fashion, art and design are looking up this season, gathering inspiration from the ether.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/video/t-magazine/100000003138291/in-the-air-clouds.html', 'snippet': 'Creative minds in fashion, art and design are looking up this season, gathering inspiration from the ether.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Creative minds in fashion, art and design are looking up this season, gathering inspiration from the ether.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/25/multimedia/tmag-ita-clouds/tmag-ita-clouds-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 338, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/09/25/multimedia/tmag-ita-clouds/tmag-ita-clouds-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 338}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 