ners in Wales during their strike in 1984. In this video, the director, Matthew Warchus, discusses a scene in which the miners hold a dance in their community hall. One of their new supporters, Jonathan (Dominic West), takes to the dance floor to show the locals some moves.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Matthew Warchus Narrates a Scene From ‘Pride’', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Warchus, Matthew', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'West, Dominic', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-09-25T20:20:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mekado Murphy', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mekado', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Murphy', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3b9be845-d28a-5dad-818b-0c69f4f753f7', 'word_count': 71, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3b9be845-d28a-5dad-818b-0c69f4f753f7'}, {'abstract': 'Much of Mr. Armajani’s new work is oddly stark, raw and hermetic.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/09/26/arts/design/siah-armajani-the-tomb-series.html', 'snippet': 'Much of Mr. Armajani’s new work is oddly stark, raw and hermetic.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Siah Armajani was born in Iran in 1939 and came to the United States in 1960 to attend Macalester College in Minnesota. He settled in Minnesota and has lived there ever since, becoming an American artist of a kind we rarely find anymore: one steeped in America’s pro-democracy literary and philosophical traditions. Much of his work has taken the form of sculptural monuments, some realized on an architectural scale, celebrating historical figures he admires, from Whitman and Thoreau, to John Dewey and Frank O’Hara.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '27', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/26/arts/26ARMAJANI-GALLERY1/ARMAJANI-GALLERY1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 239, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/09/26/arts/26ARMAJANI-GALLERY1/ARMAJANI-GALLERY1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 239}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/26/arts/26ARMAJANI-GALLERY1/ARMAJANI-GALLERY1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 407, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/26/arts/26ARMAJANI-GALLERY1/ARMAJANI-GALLERY1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 815, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/26/arts/26ARMAJANI-GALLERY1/ARMAJANI-GALLERY1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/09/26/arts/26ARMAJANI-GALLERY1/ARMAJANI-GALLERY1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/26/arts/26ARMAJANI-GALLERY1/ARMAJANI-GALLERY1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Siah Armajani: ‘The Tomb Series’', 'kicker': 'Art in Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Siah Armajani: ‘The Tomb Series’', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cotter, Holland', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'ARMAJANI, SIAH', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-09-25T20:24:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Art & Design', 'byline': {'original': 'By Holland Cotter', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Holland', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cotter', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/1970b4e8-5324-5a30-9714-07dc3738c29e', 'word_count': 311, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1970b4e8-5324-5a30-9714-07dc3738c29e'}, {'abstract': 'With the eyes of a city and a sport upon him, Derek Jeter smacked the winning hit in the final at-bat of his final game at Yankee Stadium.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/09/26/sports/baseball/derek-jeters-finale-yankee-stadium.html', 'snippet': 'With the eyes of a city and a sport upon him, Derek Jeter smacked the winning hit in the final at-bat of his final game at Yankee Stadium.', 'lead_paragraph': 'He almost started crying as he drove himself to Yankee Stadium in the afternoon. He had to turn away from his teammates before the game when they presented him with gifts, so overcome was he by the emotion. In the first inning, he said, he barely knew what was happening, and later, in the top of the ninth, his eyes welled with tears to the point that he worried that he might break down in front of the crowd of 48,613.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Night’s Hero: Who Else?', 'kicker': 'Yankees 6, Orioles 5', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Hero: Who Else?', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Waldstein, David', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Baseball', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Jeter, Derek', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Yankees', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Baltimore Orioles', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-09-25T20:24:56+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Baseball', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Waldstein', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Waldstein', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6c43b2ee-755b-593e-ae9c-482136716e49', 'word_count': 1289, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6c43b2ee-755b-593e-ae9c-482136716e49'}, {'abstract': '“Tower,” a massive steel sculpture that cuts a 110-foot swath across Hauser & Wirth, is a cautionary symbol of the changes occurring in Chelsea.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/09/26/arts/design/monika-sosnowska-tower.html', 'snippet': '“Tower,” a massive steel sculpture that cuts a 110-foot swath across Hauser & Wirth, is a cautionary symbol of the changes occurring in Chelsea.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Monika Sosnowska’s “Tower,” a massive steel sculpture that cuts a 110-foot swath across the main gallery of Hauser & Wirth, is a cautionary symbol of the changes occurring in Chelsea. Officially, it’s modeled on the framework Mies van der Rohe used for his famed Lake Shore Drive Apartments, with an eye to the impact of the International Style on Soviet-era architecture in Poland, where Ms. Sosnowska was born. But it’s difficult to look at it without thinking about the luxury condos going up right outside the gallery, along 10th Avenue, or about the way that works of such dehumanizing scale have come to seem banal in this art district.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '27', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/26/arts/26SOSNOWSKA-GALLERY2/26SOSNOWSKA-GALLERY2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 439, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/09/26/arts/26SOSNOWSKA-GALLERY2/26SOSNOWSKA-GALLERY2-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 439}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/26/arts/26SOSNOWSKA-GALLERY2/26SOSNOWSKA-GALLERY2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 749, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/26/arts/26SOSNOWSKA-GALLERY2/26SOSNOWSKA-GALLERY2-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1499, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/26/arts/26SOSNOWSKA-GALLERY2/26SOSNOWSKA-GALLERY2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/09/26/arts/26SOSNOWSKA-GALLERY2/26SOSNOWSKA-GALLERY2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/26/arts/26SOSNOWSKA-GALLERY2/26SOSNOWSKA-GALLERY2-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Monika Sosnowska: ‘Tower’', 'kicker': 'Art in Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Monika Sosnowska: ‘Tower’', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rosenberg, Karen', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-09-25T20:26:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Art & Design', 'byline': {'original': 'By Karen Rosenberg', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Karen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rosenberg', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/4a9c86dc-67fa-5e5c-9dda-1516375b3adb', 'word_count': 250, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4a9c86dc-67fa-5e5c-9dda-1516375b3adb'}, {'abstract': 'How we got stuck with a too-low inflation target.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/09/25/the-terrible-two/', 'snippet': 'How we got stuck with a too-low inflation target.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Jared Bernstein asks why the Fed (and just about all of its counterparts abroad) targets 2 percent inflation. I did some digging on this very issue in my paper for last spring’s ECB conference, and found that 2 was a convenient round number that seemed to provide a reasonable resolution to the conflict between the price stability crowd and economists worried about the two zeroes.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Terrible Two', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2014-09-25T20:26:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6732af62-e160-5295-b4a4-8c2d59878b5c', 'word_count': 349, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6732af62-e160-5295-b4a4-8c2d59878b5c'}, {'abstract': 'Mr. Colen has dialed back the artistic swagger endemic to his 2010 debut with the Gagosian Gallery.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/09/26/arts/design/dan-colen-miracle-paintings.html', 'snippet': 'Mr. Colen has dialed back the artistic swagger endemic to his 2010 debut with the Gagosian Gallery.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Dan Colen has dialed back the artistic swagger endemic to his 2010 debut with the Gagosian Gallery. He’s sized down the gallery’s big box space by about two-thirds — which should become a trend. Gone is the heavily ironic stance. Ditto the huge paintings festooned with swaths of chewing gum in lieu of brush strokes; trompe l’oeil canvases seemingly drizzled with confetti that conjure 1970s Lyrical Abstraction; and ostentatious ready-mades, including an inverted halfpipe and customized motorcycles tipped over like dominoes.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '27', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/26/arts/26SUBCOLEN-GALLERY3/26SUBCOLEN-GALLERY3-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 732, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/09/26/arts/26SUBCOLEN-GALLERY3/26SUBCOLEN-GALLERY3-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 732}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/26/arts/26SUBCOLEN-GALLERY3/26SUBCOLEN-GALLERY3-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 840, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/26/arts/26SUBCOLEN-GALLERY3/26SUBCOLEN-GALLERY3-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1680, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/26/arts/26SUBCOLEN-GALLERY3/26SUBCOLEN-GALLERY3-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/09/26/arts/26SUBCOLEN-GALLERY3/26SUBCOLEN-GALLERY3-thu