against texting and driving. In the film, Shaw, now in his 20s, recounts the rainy morning in September 2006 that he crossed the line of a Utah highway, knocking into a car containing two scientists, James Furfaro and Keith O’Dell, who were heading to work nearby. Both men were killed. Shaw says he was \xadtexting a girlfriend at the time, adding in unmistakable anguish that he can’t even \xadremember what he was texting about. He is next seen taking part in something almost inconceivable: He enters the scene where one of the dead men’s daughters is being interviewed, and receives from that woman a warm, earnest, tearful, cathartic hug.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/11/books/review/28KOLKER/28KOLKER-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 523, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/09/11/books/review/28KOLKER/28KOLKER-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 523}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/11/books/review/28KOLKER/28KOLKER-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 893, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/11/books/review/28KOLKER/28KOLKER-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1557, 'width': 1785, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/11/books/review/28KOLKER/28KOLKER-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/09/11/books/review/28KOLKER/28KOLKER-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/11/books/review/28KOLKER/28KOLKER-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Attention Must Be Paid', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Attention Must Be Paid', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Richtel, Matt', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Driver Distraction and Fatigue', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Text Messaging', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-09-25T21:46:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Robert Kolker', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Robert', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kolker', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/36c7ad89-7d74-5942-a19d-ebb419a22c69', 'word_count': 1254, 'uri': 'nyt://article/36c7ad89-7d74-5942-a19d-ebb419a22c69'}, {'abstract': '“The Two Faces of January” is based on a 1964 Patricia Highsmith novel about two affluent American tourists vacationing in Europe.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/09/26/movies/the-two-faces-of-january-from-a-highsmith-novel.html', 'snippet': '“The Two Faces of January” is based on a 1964 Patricia Highsmith novel about two affluent American tourists vacationing in Europe.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It’s hard to read the first exchange of looks that sets “The Two Faces of January” down its satisfying, shivering path. The vacationing American tourist, Chester MacFarland (Viggo Mortensen), is walking around the Acropolis with his wife, Colette (Kirsten Dunst), when he notices the other man observing him. It’s a glorious day, and the Americans are, true to the early 1960s time frame and their station, formally attired. She’s wearing a dress and hat, and carrying gloves and a purse. He’s wearing a tie, a three-piece suit, a straw hat and a pair of very dark glasses. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/26/arts/25TWOFACESJANUARY1/TWOFACESJANUARY1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 404, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/09/26/arts/25TWOFACESJANUARY1/TWOFACESJANUARY1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 404}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/26/arts/25TWOFACESJANUARY1/TWOFACESJANUARY1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 689, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/26/arts/25TWOFACESJANUARY1/TWOFACESJANUARY1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1378, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/26/arts/25TWOFACESJANUARY1/TWOFACESJANUARY1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/09/26/arts/25TWOFACESJANUARY1/TWOFACESJANUARY1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/26/arts/25TWOFACESJANUARY1/TWOFACESJANUARY1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headlin