'}], 'pub_date': '2014-09-26T12:23:34+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'subsection_name': 'DVD', 'byline': {'original': 'By J. Hoberman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'J.', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hoberman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/41d79c0d-4382-5bdd-88cd-6ee8f7d9788b', 'word_count': 1295, 'uri': 'nyt://article/41d79c0d-4382-5bdd-88cd-6ee8f7d9788b'}, {'abstract': 'Bollywood films are gaining a foothold in many international markets, but Britain and the United States remain elusive.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/09/28/movies/shah-rukh-khan-and-bollywoods-global-fortunes-advance.html', 'snippet': 'Bollywood films are gaining a foothold in many international markets, but Britain and the United States remain elusive.', 'lead_paragraph': 'MUMBAI — Shah Rukh Khan has many titles.\xa0The 48-year-old Bollywood superstar is known as King Khan, King of Bollywood and Bollywood Badshah (or emperor). This summer Mr. Khan became a knight. In a glittering ceremony in Mumbai, the French foreign affairs minister, Laurent Fabius, conferred France’s highest civilian honor — the Knight of the Legion of Honor — on Mr. Khan. In the official news release, Mr. Fabius said, “The French people hail Shah Rukh Khan’s talent and generosity,” which transcend “cultural and historical differences.” ', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '15', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/28/arts/28BOLLYWOOD1/28BOLLYWOOD1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/09/28/arts/28BOLLYWOOD1/28BOLLYWOOD1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'capt