qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1e53c0d5-8e43-55e3-b864-f8026ff3763c', 'word_count': 101, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1e53c0d5-8e43-55e3-b864-f8026ff3763c'}, {'abstract': 'An attack in Karachi reveals a web of lives connected by violence in this novel-in-stories.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/09/28/books/review/the-scatter-here-is-too-great-by-bilal-tanweer.html', 'snippet': 'An attack in Karachi reveals a web of lives connected by violence in this novel-in-stories.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Among people who routinely deal with violence, pain, cruelty and injustice — \xaddomestic violence counselors, aid \xadworkers, paramedics, public defenders, immigrant advocates — there’s a phenomenon known as secondary trauma. It’s also sometimes called vicarious trauma, or empathic strain. In her book, “Trauma Stewardship,” Laura van Dernoot \xadLipsky, a former social worker, defines it as being unable to live with the knowledge that, despite our best efforts, most of the world’s suffering goes “unnoticed and unattended.” “Still,” she writes, “people who are working to help those who suffer . . . must somehow reconcile their own joy — the authentic wonder and delight in life — with the irrefutable fact of suffering in the world.” ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/16/books/review/28row/28row-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/09/16/books/review/28row/28row-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/16/books/review/28row/28row-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/16/books/review/28row/28row-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1333, 'width': 2000, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/16/books/review/28row/28row-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/09/16/books/review/28row/28row-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/16/books/review/28row/28row-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Ticking Time Bomb', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Ticking Time Bomb', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Tanweer, Bilal', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-09-26T14:41:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jess Row', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jess', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Row', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/124917b2-a2d0-50a9-948c-02e32b1b2c83', 'word_count': 926, 'uri': 'nyt://article/124917b2-a2d0-50a9-948c-02e32b1b2c83'}, {'abstract': 'Two families find themselves on opposite sides of Sri Lanka’s civil war in this debut novel.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/09/28/books/review/island-of-a-thousand-mirrors-by-nayomi-munaweera.html', 'snippet': 'Two families find themselves on opposite sides of Sri Lanka’s civil war in this debut novel.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In one of the many startling scenes in “Island of a Thousand Mirrors,” Nayomi Munaweera’s first novel, a Sri Lankan girl riding the train to school is suddenly surrounded by a machete-wielding mob, who demand proof she isn’t Tamil. In her panic, she recites the Buddhist sutras “preaching unattachment, impermanence, the inevitability of death,” an unholy trinity that could apply to all civil wars. This chilling exchange reminded me of a conversation I once had on a London bus with a Somali refugee, who swerved from banal chitchat into dark reminiscence. He recalled a moment in Mogadishu when he was forced to recite his genealogy, the string of grandfathers’ names that place all Somalis within their clans, and he borrowed a school friend’s lineage, as his own would have marked him for death. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/16/books/review/28mohamed/28mohamed-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/09/16/books/review/28mohamed/28mohamed-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/16/books/review/28mohamed/28mohamed-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/16/books/review/28mohamed/28mohamed-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/16/books/review/28mohamed/28mohamed-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/09/16/books/review/28mohamed/28mohamed-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/16/books/review/28mohamed/28mohamed-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Say You’re One of Them', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Say You’re One of Them', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mohamed, Nadifa', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Munaweera, Nayomi', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-09-26T14:41:37+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nadifa Mohamed', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nadifa', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Mohamed', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/c08f7c43-b291-59a7-a86a-c34c1188b646', 'word_count': 607, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c08f7c43-b291-59a7-a86a-c34c1188b646'}, {'abstract': 'A son of Italian immigrants on his relationship with the ancestral homeland.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/09/28/books/review/my-two-italies-by-joseph-luzzi.html', 'snippet': 'A son of Italian immigrants on his relationship with the ancestral homeland.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Joseph Luzzi takes the title of his brief, bittersweet new book, “My Two Italies,” from Shelley, who, in a letter of 1818, distinguished between the Italy of “the green earth and transparent sea, and the mighty ruins” and the human reality he encountered there: “The one is the most sublime . . . the other is the most degraded, disgusting and odious.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '13', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/28/books/review/0928-bks-SELIGMAN/0928-bks-SELIGMAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 576, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/09/28/books/review/0928-bks-SELIGMAN/0928-bks-SELIGMAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 576}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/28/books/review/0928-bks-SELIGMAN/0928-bks-SELIGMAN-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 983, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/28/books/review/0928-bks-SELIGMAN/0928-bks-SELIGMAN-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1416, 'width': 1475, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/28/books/review/0928-bks-SELIGMAN/0928-bks-SELIGMAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/09/28/books/review/0928-bks-SELIGMAN/0928-bks-SELIGMAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/28/books/review/0928-bks-SELIGMAN/0928-bks-SELIGMAN-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Road to Calabria', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Road to Calabria', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Luzzi, Joseph', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-09-26T14:42:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Craig Seligman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Craig', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Seligman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/092ac079-f9d7-530d-b4e9-ee0a2993e791', 'word_count': 888, 'uri': 'nyt://article/092ac079-f9d7-530d-b4e9-ee0a2993e791'}, {'abstract': 'Caitlin Doughty, whose “Smoke Gets in Your Eyes” is No. 14 on the hardcover nonfiction list, is a young undertaker who has made it her mission to demystify death and promote a D.I.Y. approach to funerals.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/10/05/books/review/inside-the-list.html', 'snippet': 'Caitlin Doughty, whose “Smoke Gets in Your Eyes” is No. 14 on the hardcover nonfiction list, is a young undertaker who has made it her mission to demystify death and promote a D.I.Y. approach to funerals.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Dead: Nonfiction books usually rely on fairly pedestrian titles and subtitles to tell readers what’s inside — see for instance “The Roosevelts: An Intimate History,” by Geoffrey C. Ward and Ken Burns, No. 6 in its second week on the hardcover nonfiction list. But every now and then a book arrives that reminds you naming can be an art in itself; the best subtitles work almost like line breaks in poetry, forcing you to re-evaluate what came before. So let’s pause a moment to appreciate Caitlin Doughty’s “Smoke Gets in Your Eyes,” new at No. 14, which has the sit-up-and-take-notice subtitle “And Other Lessons From the Crematory.” Doughty is a young undertaker from Los Angeles who has made it her mission to demystify death, encouraging customers and the public at large to embrace a D.I.Y. approach to funerals. “They are grossed out,” she told The Atlantic in a recent online interview, “but that’s because they’ve been told their whole lives that death and bodies are best kept to the ‘professionals’ and that they are dangerous. Most corpses are safer than living bodies. Corpses don’t decompose immediately. They’re not a time bomb of deadness waiting to attack. They’re your mom or your husband, or the shell of them, at least. . . . Your average corpse is completely safe.” I’m not sure how soothing I find this pep talk — it’s not putrefaction I fear, it’s death — but I do admire Doughty’s cheerful sang-froid. I bet she’s excellent at her job.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/05/books/review/05list-doughty/05list-doughty-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 450, 'width': 451, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/05/books/review/05list-doughty/05list-doughty-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 450, 'width': 451, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/05/books/review/05list-doughty/05list-doughty-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/10/05/books/review/05list-doughty/05list-doughty-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/05/books/review/05list-doughty/05list-doughty-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Inside the List', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Inside the List', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-09-26T14:42:56+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Gregory Cowles', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Gregory', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cowles', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b3922721-c59c-56a1-8ca2-e0313e95e705', 'word_count': 490, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b3922721-c59c-56a1-8ca2-e0313e95e705'}, {'abstract': 'Readers respond to a recent review of Henry Kissinger’s “World Order” and more.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/09/28/books/review/letters-world-order.html', 'snippet': 'Readers respond to a recent review of Henry Kissinger’s “World Order” and more.', 'lead_paragraph': '‘World Order’', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Letters: ‘World Order’', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Letters', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-09-26T14:43:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a3217550-f097-5b8d-ad82-d974a045231c', 'word_count': 866, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a3217550-f097-5b8d-ad82-d974a045231c'}, {'abstract': 'In “Werner Herzog: A Guide for the Perplexed,” the filmmaker talks about his notoriously volatile relationship with the actor Klaus Kinski, among many other subjects.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/09/28/books/review/disquiet-on-the-set.html', 'snippet': 'In “Werner Herzog: A Guide for the Perplexed,” the filmmaker talks about his notoriously volatile relationship with the actor Klaus Kinski, among many other subjects.', 'lead_paragraph': '“Werner Herzog: A Guide for the Perplexed,” the newly revised and expanded collection of interviews with the adventurous filmmaker, might leave readers even more perplexed but will entertain them along the way.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/18/books/review/28openbook/28openbook-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 286, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/09/18/books/review/28openbook/28openbook-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 286}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/18/books/review/28openbook/28openbook-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 488, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/18/books/review/28openbook/28openbook-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 623, 'width': 1308, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/18/books/review/28openbook/28openbook-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/09/18/books/review/28openbook/28openbook-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/18/books/review/28openbook/28openbook-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Disquiet on the Set', 'kicker': 'Open Book', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Disquiet on the Set', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Herzog, Werner', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-09-26T14:43:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By John Williams', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Williams', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0223969d-458f-5c42-9ed3-0630769080b0', 'word_count': 416, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0223969d-458f-5c42-9ed3-0630769080b0'}, {'abstract': 'David Cronenberg’s debut novel centers on two journalists and their fascination with scandal and social media.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/09/28/books/review/consumed-by-david-cronenberg.html', 'snippet': 'David Cronenberg’s debut novel centers on two journalists and their fascination with scandal and social media.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I seem to be in the middle of a minor “Cronenberg moment.” I recently pinned my thumb in a car door, so tightly I had to click the door open again to get it out. Days later the pain had faded, but the nail had turned black. After a few weeks’ growth, a feeble dividing edge of torn nail has crept from my cuticle, as the dead nail begins its crawl out, to be replaced by — what? A new nail, I hope. (Stay tuned.)', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/12/books/review/28lethem/28lethem-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 728, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/09/12/books/review/28lethem/28lethem-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 728}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/12/books/review/28lethem/28lethem-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 845, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/12/books/review/28lethem/28lethem-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1059, 'width': 873, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/12/books/review/28lethem/28lethem-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/09/12/books/review/28lethem/28lethem-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/12/books/review/28lethem/28lethem-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'All Atwitter', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'All Atwitter', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cronenberg, David', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-09-26T14:48:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jonathan Lethem', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jonathan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Lethem', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/9a6e4a17-61c1-5c98-a128-2e413bd94b42', 'word_count': 1233, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9a6e4a17-61c1-5c98-a128-2e413bd94b42'}, {'abstract': 'The decision by the California Public Employees’ Retirement System to terminate its $4.5 billion hedge fund portfolio to makes it the biggest public pension to turn its back on hedge funds.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/09/27/business/in-calperss-departure-a-watershed-moment-for-hedge-funds.html', 'snippet': 'The decision by the California Public Employees’ Retirement System to terminate its $4.5 billion hedge fund portfolio to makes it the biggest public pension to turn its back on hedge funds.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Assets at hedge funds, once a niche investment vehicle for ultra-wealthy investors, hit a record $2.8 trillion this year. There are now more than 10,000 hedge funds and counting. Their steep fees have minted scores of billionaire managers and spawned not a few criminals in what has turned into a gold rush of the 21st century.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/27/business/27stewart02/27stewart02-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 900, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/09/27/business/27stewart02/27stewart02-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 900}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/27/business/27stewart02/27stewart02-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 684, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/27/business/27stewart02/27stewart02-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1367, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/27/business/27stewart02/27stewart02-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/09/27/business/27stewart02/27stewart02-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/27/business/27stewart02/27stewart02-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Hedge Funds Lose Calpers, and More', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Hedge Funds Lose Calpers, and More', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'California Public Employees Retirement System', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hedge Funds', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Pensions and Retirement Plans', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Prices (Fares, Fees and Rates)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-09-26T14:49:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By James B. Stewart', 'person': [{'firstname': 'James', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e07f0c12-91b9-5d88-8071-f09f8316dad5', 'word_count': 1140, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e07f0c12-91b9-5d88-8071-f09f8316dad5'}, {'abstract': 'A first novel set in World War II Germany.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/09/28/books/review/the-undertaking-by-audrey-magee.html', 'snippet': 'A first novel set in World War II Germany.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Audrey Magee’s debut novel is a love story. Or it would be, if deep emotions were allowed to enter into it. Peter Faber is a soldier desperate to escape the fighting. Katharina Spinell is a typist desperate for some financial security. Brought together by an agency, they marry in separate simultaneous ceremonies. He gets honeymoon leave; she gets the promise of a widow’s pension if he dies. When they meet, they find that they’re attracted to each other — an attraction so intense that it survives years of wartime separation. They also happen to be Nazis. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '23', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/16/books/review/28thomas/28thomas-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 900, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/09/16/books/review/28thomas/28thomas-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 900}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/16/books/review/28thomas/28thomas-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 683, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/16/books/review/28thomas/28thomas-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1366, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/16/books/review/28thomas/28thomas-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, '