'_id': 'nyt://article/20141af9-3e99-513c-808e-16e84373a679', 'word_count': 439, 'uri': 'nyt://article/20141af9-3e99-513c-808e-16e84373a679'}, {'abstract': 'A phone call said to have been made by Mohammad Ali Baryalei, a former Sydney bouncer who is now a leader in the Islamic State, set off a counterterrorism crackdown in Australia.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/09/27/world/asia/isis-australia-mohammad-ali-baryalei.html', 'snippet': 'A phone call said to have been made by Mohammad Ali Baryalei, a former Sydney bouncer who is now a leader in the Islamic State, set off a counterterrorism crackdown in Australia.', 'lead_paragraph': 'SYDNEY, Australia — In Kings Cross, this city’s bawdy, alcohol-infused red-light district, Mohammad Ali Baryalei once patrolled the sidewalk outside the Love Machine club, his basso voice luring customers in, his muscle keeping the unwanted out. For a time, the police said, his was a world of prostitutes, drugs, gangs and gambling.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'ISIS Lieutenant Emerges From Australian Red-Light District', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Australia Fears Reach of ISIS Into Its Cities', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Australia', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Perlez, Jane', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Islamic State in Iraq and Syria (ISIS)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Baryalei, Mohammad Ali', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Terrorism', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Murders and Attempted Murders', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Muslims and Islam', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-09-26T17:00:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jane Perlez', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jane', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Perlez', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/30fca222-47c3-5053-b2b6-1774d41decd2', 'word_count': 1489, 'uri': 'nyt://article/30fca222-47c3-5053-b2b6-1774d41decd2'}, {'abstract': 'Highlights from the International Herald Tribune archives: France bans Communism in 1939.', 'web_url': 'https://iht-retrospective.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/09/26/1939-french-ban-communism/', 'snippet': 'Highlights from the International Herald Tribune archives: France bans Communism in 1939.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Daladier Cabinet yesterday decreed the dissolution of the French Communist party, the most powerful foreign section of the Comintern since Hitler suppressed its German branch six years ago. Communism now ceases to exist as an organized force in any major country of Europe with the exception of course, of Soviet Russia. The action of the French government in proscribing the Communist party comes as no surprise. It had been foreseen for weeks, ever since Soviet Russia signed the non-aggression pact with Nazi Germany and followed it up by making an agreement with that country for the partition of Poland. Even Leftist papers like ‘‘L’OEuvre’’ have been advocating the Abolition of the French Communist party since the outbreak of the war. Leon Blum, the Socialist party leader, in ‘‘Le Populate’’ made an urgent appeal to the French Communists to save their party, by formally renouncing their allegiance to Moscow. — New York Herald Tribune, European Edition, Sept. 27, 1939 ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/27/blogs/27iht-oldsept27-blum/27iht-oldsept27-blum-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 762, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/09/27/blogs/27iht-oldsept27-blum/27iht-oldsept27-blum-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 762}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/27/blogs/27iht-oldsept27-blum/27iht-oldsept27-blum-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 807, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/27/blogs/27iht-oldsept27-blum/27iht-oldsept27-blum-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1206, 'width': 950, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/27/blogs/27iht-oldsept27-blum/27iht-oldsept27-blum-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/09/27/blogs/27iht-oldsept27-blum/27iht-oldsept27-blum-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/27/blogs/27iht-oldsept27-blum/27iht-oldsept27-blum-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '1939: French Ban Communism', 'kicker': 'IHT Retrospective', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Communism (Theory and Philosophy)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Politics and Government', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'France', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-09-26T17:02:24+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'byline': {'original': 'By International