 'subsection_name': 'Middle East', 'byline': {'original': 'By Robert Mackey', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Robert', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Mackey', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7dd66460-9be9-5b83-b020-1819cbd436a1', 'word_count': 410, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7dd66460-9be9-5b83-b020-1819cbd436a1'}, {'abstract': 'Susan Ruddie’s bridal atelier in Brooklyn, the Wedding Dresser, is also her home — and a destination for brides who need alterations to off-the-rack or vintage gowns.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/09/28/nyregion/at-the-wedding-dresser-altering-before-the-altar.html', 'snippet': 'Susan Ruddie’s bridal atelier in Brooklyn, the Wedding Dresser, is also her home — and a destination for brides who need alterations to off-the-rack or vintage gowns.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Off the main drag of Flatbush Avenue and its jerk chicken counters and Caribbean grocers stocking fresh coconuts and live crabs is a six-story apartment building at 45 Linden Boulevard. With crown molding and an intricate tiled floor, the lobby evokes prewar opulence, now a little worse for wear. There are 57 units in the building, but 4E is surely the busiest. The space doubles as Susan Ruddie’s home and her bridal atelier, the Wedding Dresser. “We used to have a living room,” Ms. Ruddie said. “Now all the furniture is in storage.” ', 'print_section': 'MB', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'At the Wedding Dresser, Altering Before the Altar', 'kicker': 'Neighborhood Joint', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'To Alter Before the Altar', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Wedding Dresser, The (Brooklyn, NY)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Ditmas Park (Brooklyn, NY)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Weddings and Engagements', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ruddie, Susan', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dresses', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fashion and Apparel', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-09-26T23:39:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jessica Leigh Hester', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jessica', 'middlename': 'Leigh', 'lastname': 'Hester', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a00a0503-c05a-579f-ad21-9da1f02d4455', 'word_count': 621, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a00a0503-c05a-579f-ad21-9da1f02d4455'}, {'abstract': 'A 1910 magazine article described Edgar Rudolph Randolph Parker, a rabid self-promoter, as “the most conceited charlatan that dental quackery has ever known.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/09/28/nyregion/was-painless-parker-a-vaudeville-joke-or-a-real-brooklyn-dentist.html', 'snippet': 'A 1910 magazine article described Edgar Rudolph Randolph Parker, a rabid self-promoter, as “the most conceited charlatan that dental quackery has ever known.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'Q. I had always thought that the dentist named Painless Parker was a vaudeville joke, but my wife insists there was a real one in Brooklyn. Was there?', 'print_section': 'MB', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/28/nyregion/28FYI/28FYI-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 451, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/09/28/nyregion/28FYI/28FYI-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 451}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/201