prison.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The PEN American Center will hold a vigil for the Uighur writer and academic Ilham Tohti on Monday evening in response to his being sentenced to life in prison. Mr. Tohti, left, an economics professor and vocal critic of the Chinese government on human rights and social issues, was sentenced last week by China on charges of separatism. The court stated that Mr. Tohti “bewitched” his students and “exploited foreign forces to create pressure to make Xinjiang an international matter.” Since his arrest in January, Mr. Tohti has received global attention from free-speech activists around the globe. In May he was awarded the 2014 PEN/Barbara Goldsmith Freedom to Write Award. Of the 38 writers who were in prison at the time they received the award, 35 have been subsequently released. The candlelight vigil on Monday is to feature the novelist Barbara Kingsolver, who will read a statement Mr. Tohti wrote in 2013, specifically in the event of his arrest. The vigil is scheduled for 8 p.m. outside the New School in New York.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/29/arts/ARTS4/ARTS4-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 795, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/09/29/arts/ARTS4/ARTS4-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 795}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/29/arts/ARTS4/ARTS4-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 773, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/29/arts/ARTS4/ARTS4-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1197, 'width': 903, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/29/arts/ARTS4/ARTS4-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/09/29/arts/ARTS4/ARTS4-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/29/arts/ARTS4/ARTS4-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Vigil Is Planned for Uighur Writer', 'kicker': 'Arts, Briefly', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Vigil Is Planned for Uighur Writer', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Tohti, Ilham', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'PEN American Center', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Uighurs (Chinese Ethnic Group)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'China', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Writing and Writers', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-09-28T22:20:31+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Books', 'byline': {'original': 'Compiled by Andrew R. Chow', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': 'R.', 'lastname': 'Chow', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/652d0526-43b1-5320-8ee8-980c8bd2ab83', 'word_count': 173, 'uri': 'nyt://article/652d0526-43b1-5320-8ee8-980c8bd2ab83'}, {'abstract': 'Wynton Marsalis will bring his Jazz at Lincoln Center Orchestra to St. Louis to open that city’s new jazz center on Thursday.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/09/29/arts/music/wynton-marsalis-to-open-st-louis-jazz-center.html', 'snippet': 'Wynton Marsalis will bring his Jazz at Lincoln Center Orchestra to St. Louis to open that city’s new jazz center on Thursday.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Wynton Marsalis will bring his Jazz at Lincoln Center Orchestra to St. Louis to open that city’s new jazz center on Thursday. The center, newly remodeled and renamed the Harold & Dorothy Steward Center for Jazz, will be a hub and education space for a city that birthed jazz greats like Miles Davis and Clark Terry. The center, which previously housed Jazz at the Bistro, underwent a $10 million renovation this year in hopes of creating a counterpart to New York’s Jazz at Lincoln Center and San Francisco’s SFJazz. In addition to the main concert space, the center will contain rehearsal and practice rooms for instruction sessions. “We have to make sure it’s not just a question of preserving our jazz heritage,” Gene Dobbs Bradford, executive director of Jazz St. Louis, told The Associated Press. “We have to educate and train the next generation of great St. Louis musicians.” Other coming performers include a St. Louis native, David Sanborn; Joshua Redman; and Joey DeFrancesco. The space is named after the parents of Dave Steward, who is the chief executive of World Wide Technology and was the project’s lead donor.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Wynton Marsalis to Open St. Louis Jazz Center', 'kicker': 'Arts, Briefly', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Wynton Marsalis to Open St. Louis Jazz Center', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'St Louis (Mo)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Jazz', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Marsalis, Wynton', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Jazz at Lincoln Center Orchestra', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-09-28T22:22:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'Compiled by Andrew R. Chow', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': 'R.', 'lastname': 'Chow', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/92101a82-05ee-5661-983c-083e84c7b9dc', 'word_count': 189, 'uri': 'nyt://article/92101a82-05ee-5661-983c-083e84c7b9dc'}, {'abstract': 'American fans of “Downton Abbey” will be able to see one of its stars, Elizabeth McGovern, both on TV in English formal wear and live holding a guitar this winter.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/09/29/arts/music/downton-abbey-star-to-tour-with-band.html', 'snippet': 'American fans of “Downton Abbey” will be able to see one of its stars, Elizabeth McGovern, both on TV in English formal wear and live holding a guitar this winter.', 'lead_paragraph': 'American fans of the hit PBS show “Downton Abbey” will be able to see one of its stars, Elizabeth McGovern, both on TV in English formal wear and live holding a guitar this winter. Ms. McGovern, acclaimed for playing Countess Cora Crawley on “Downton,” will embark on her first United States tour with her folk-rock band, Sadie and the Hotheads, The Associated Press reported. Ms. McGovern has been on “Downton” since the show’s inception in 2010 and has received Emmy and Golden Globe nominations for her work. Sadie and the Hotheads formed three years earlier and has since featured performances by another “Downton” star, Michelle Dockery. The group will release its third album, “Still Waiting,” on Thursday, and the band’s tour is scheduled to begin on Dec. 4 in Washington. The Season 5 premiere of “Downton Abbey” in the United States will arrive one month later.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times',