attle in Ghazni Province was the latest sign that the insurgency was becoming increasingly ambitious, posing the central challenge facing the country’s new president.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/09/29/world/asia/taliban-presses-for-advantage-as-politicians-work-on-maneuvers-in-kabul.html', 'snippet': 'A brutal battle in Ghazni Province was the latest sign that the insurgency was becoming increasingly ambitious, posing the central challenge facing the country’s new president.', 'lead_paragraph': 'KABUL, Afghanistan — Afghan security forces managed to defeat a weeklong Taliban assault on a strategic district in central Afghanistan, provincial officials said over the weekend, though the victory was tenuous and the toll was high. At least 64 people were killed, including some civilians who were beheaded by the insurgents, and a number of homes were torched.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Taliban Press for Advantage as Politicians Work on Maneuvers in Kabul', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Taliban Press for an Advantage as Politicians Work on Maneuvers in Kabul', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Afghanistan War (2001- )', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Taliban', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Ghazni Province (Afghanistan)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'AFGHANISTAN', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Terrorism', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ghani, Ashraf', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'ABDULLAH, ABDULLAH', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-09-29T00:03:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Declan Walsh and Fazal Muzhary', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Declan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Walsh', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Fazal', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Muzhary', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5644986d-546c-5ae9-9d30-d9c335a386ee', 'word_count': 762, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5644986d-546c-5ae9-9d30-d9c335a386ee'}, {'abstract': 'Several years ago, beekeepers were vexed: Honeybees, those versatile workhorses of pollination, were vanishing by the millions.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/09/29/us/the-head-scratching-case-of-the-vanishing-bees.html', 'snippet': 'Several years ago, beekeepers were vexed: Honeybees, those versatile workhorses of pollination, were vanishing by the millions.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In 1872, a merchant ship called the Mary Celeste set sail from New York, and four weeks later was found by sailors aboard another vessel to be moving erratically in the Atlantic Ocean 400 miles east of the Azores. Curious, those sailors boarded the Mary Celeste, only to find nary a soul. The cargo was intact, as were supplies of food and water. But there was no sign of the seven-man crew, the captain, or his wife and daughter, who had gone along for the journey. To this day, what turned that brigantine into a ghost ship remains a maritime mystery.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Head-Scratching Case of the Vanishing Bees', 'kicker': 'Retro Report', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Bees', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Agriculture and Farming', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'United States', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hackenberg, David', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'vanEngelsdorp, Dennis', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-09-29T00:04:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Clyde Haberman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Clyde', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Haberman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/48ce588b-031b-536a-b363-0519be294394', 'word_count': 950, 'uri': 'nyt://article/48ce588b-031b-536a-b363-0519be294394'}, {'abstract': 'An underwater view of the perilous state of our marine ecosystems.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/09/29/opinion/swimming-through-garbage.html', 'snippet': 'An underwater view of the perilous state of our marine ecosystems.', 'lead_paragraph': 'CAPE TOWN — YOU get a good feel for the health of the oceans when you stick your head in them for four weeks. This summer, I swam long distances in the Seven Seas: the Mediterranean, Adriatic, Aegean, Black, Red, Arabian and North Seas. The longest swim was 37 miles and took me two days. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '27', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/29/opinion/29ocean1/29ocean2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/09/29/opinion/29ocean1/29ocean2-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/29/opinion/29ocean1/29ocean2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/29/opinion/29ocean1/29oc