ad grown tired of his leadership and were weighing a change. In particular, there were concerns that his eccentric management style ‒ he wore sunglasses while giving a presentation at a conference and used his monthly investment letters to share intimate details ‒ was becoming a distraction.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Morning Agenda: Bond King Quits', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2014-09-29T12:28:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sydney Ember', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sydney', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ember', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1854bb36-57d1-5dbb-a573-f79a19dcc341', 'word_count': 2266, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1854bb36-57d1-5dbb-a573-f79a19dcc341'}, {'abstract': 'Islamic State represents the counterhuman. The human has no alternative but to fight back.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/09/30/opinion/roger-cohen-for-isis-slaughter-is-an-end-in-itself.html', 'snippet': 'Islamic State represents the counterhuman. The human has no alternative but to fight back.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LONDON — In a famous passage from “Survival in Auschwitz,” Primo Levi relates an incident upon arrival in the Nazi death camp that captures the intersection of the human with the inhuman. He and other Italian prisoners have been held in a shed as they await their fate. Levi looks around in search of some means to quench his thirst:', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '23', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/11/01/opinion/cohen-circular/cohen-circular-articleLarge-v2.png', 'height': 30