has certainly been one of the most unlikely. He spent most of his childhood in Europe and Asia, sheltered from pop culture as a member of the religious cult Children of God. After escaping, he became the personal assistant to the eccentric Texas oil billionaire Stanley Marsh III. That unconventional background didn’t stifle Owens’s preternatural songwriting gifts, which were first revealed in his group Girls, which released two passionately received albums – 2009’s “Album” and 2011’s “Father, Son, Holy Ghost” – before splintering.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/29/t-magazine/29owens-diehl/29owens-diehl-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 840, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/09/29/t-magazine/29owens-diehl/29owens-diehl-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 840}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/29/t-magazine/29owens-diehl/29owens-diehl-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 732, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/29/t-magazine/29owens-diehl/29owens-diehl-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1463, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/29/t-magazine/29owens-diehl/29owens-diehl-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/09/29/t-magazine/29owens-diehl/29owens-diehl-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/29/