'Ben Carson thinks it’s possible that anarchy will derail the 2016 election.', 'web_url': 'https://takingnote.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/09/29/no-comment-necessary-anarchy-in-the-u-s-a/', 'snippet': 'Ben Carson thinks it’s possible that anarchy will derail the 2016 election.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Dr. Ben Carson, the retired neurosurgeon famous for saying the Affordable Care Act was “the worst thing that happened in this nation since slavery,” is thinking about running for president in 2016. But he’s not actually sure that an election will take place.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'No Comment Necessary: Will the 2016 Election Take Place?', 'kicker': 'Taking Note', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2016', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Carson, Benjamin S', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wallace, Chris', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Fox News Channel', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-09-29T17:38:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew Rosenthal', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rosenthal', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/45dee3d9-5c09-54cf-aca8-6e36786c769e', 'word_count': 275, 'uri': 'nyt://article/45dee3d9-5c09-54cf-aca8-6e36786c769e'}, {'abstract': 'A technique which early humans used to make knives by flaking off bits of stone was long thought to have originated in Africa and spread from there, but a new study suggests it arose independently in Eurasia.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/09/30/science/toolmaking-may-have-risen-independently.html', 'snippet': 'A technique which early humans used to make knives by flaking off bits of stone was long thought to have originated in Africa and spread from there, but a new study suggests it arose independently in Eurasia.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The so-called Levallois technique — which early humans used to make knives and other instruments by flaking off bits of stone — was long thought to have originated in Africa 500,000 to 600,000 years ago, then taken to Eurasia as part of an exodus from Africa. ', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/30/science/30tools/30tools-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 529, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/30/science/30tools/30tools-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1085, 'width': 560, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/30/science/30tools/30tools-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/09/30/science/30tools/30tools-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/30/science/30tools/30tools-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Toolmaking May Have Risen Independently', 'kicker': 'Observatory', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Archaeology: Toolmaking May Have Risen Independently', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ARCHAEOLOGY', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Quenqua, Douglas', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Science (Journal)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tools', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Adler, Daniel S', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-09-29T17:38:59+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Science', 'section_name': 'Science', 'byline': {'original': 'By Douglas Quenqua', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Douglas', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Quenqua', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b2fa9b0b-84d4-52a4-87d1-5c4095eb45ee', 'word_count': 256, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b2fa9b0b-84d4-52a4-87d1-5c4095eb45ee'}, {'abstract': 'The North Carolina Senate candidate continues to trail Kay Hagan, but his favorability rating in a recent poll is reason for him to be optimistic.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/09/30/upshot/poll-contains-hidden-nugget-of-good-news-for-thom-tillis.html', 'snippet': 'The North Carolina Senate candidate continues to trail Kay Hagan, but his favorability rating in a recent poll is reason for him to be optimistic.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It’s not usually wise to focus too much on individual polls, but Sunday’s CNN/ORC poll in North Carolina is worth a little extra attention.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/30/upshot/30UP-Tillis/30UP-Tillis-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/09/30/upshot/30UP-Tillis/30UP-Tillis-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/30/upshot/30UP-Tillis/30UP-Tillis-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width'