e', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Auto Racing', 'byline': {'original': 'By Viv Bernstein', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Viv', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bernstein', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9f0b7d4a-852c-56f3-b5b2-d41d8c9d01fb', 'word_count': 761, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9f0b7d4a-852c-56f3-b5b2-d41d8c9d01fb'}, {'abstract': 'The Justice Department under Attorney General Eric Holder Jr. failed to prosecute cases related to the financial crisis.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/09/30/opinion/joe-nocera-the-hole-in-holders-legacy.html', 'snippet': 'The Justice Department under Attorney General Eric Holder Jr. failed to prosecute cases related to the financial crisis.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A few weeks ago, Attorney General Eric Holder Jr. gave a speech at the New York University School of Law on the subject of white-collar prosecutions. In it, he offered a full-throated defense of his department’s efforts in the aftermath of the 2008 financial crisis. With his resignation announcement coming eight days later, one can’t help but view his speech as a kind of valedictory.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '23', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/11/01/opinion/nocera-circular/nocera-circular-jumbo-v2.png', 'height': 228, 'width': 228, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/11/01/opinion/nocera-circular/nocera-circular-superJumbo-v2.png', 'height': 228, 'width': 228, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/11/01/opinion/nocera-circular/nocera-circular-thumbStandard-v2.png', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/11/01/opinion/nocera-circular/nocera-circular-thumbStandard-v2.png', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/11/01/opinion/nocera-circular/nocera-circular-thumbLarge-v4.png', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Hole in Holder’s Legacy', 'kicker': 'Op-Ed Columnist', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Hole in Holder’s Legacy', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Holder, Eric H Jr', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Nocera, Joseph', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Justice Department', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Subprime Mortgage Crisis', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Banking and Financial Institutions', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Securities and Commodities Violations', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'United States', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-09-30T00:18:19+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Joe Nocera', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Joe', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Nocera', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/f6eaabaa-0d65-56d0-9df3-f8a4ca3c46c3', 'word_count': 816, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f6eaabaa-0d65-56d0-9df3-f8a4ca3c46c3'}, {'abstract': 'In a state where members of the Supreme Court are elected, one justice uses Twitter, and often, to connect to his constituents.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/09/30/opinion/justice-don-willett-of-the-texas-supreme-court-lights-up-twitter.html', 'snippet': 'In a state where members of the Supreme Court are elected, one justice uses Twitter, and often, to connect to his constituents.', 'lead_paragraph': 'To judge by his Twitter feed, Don Willett seems like an all-around solid guy: native Texan, proud conservative, three charming kids, loves Blue Bell ice cream and Baylor football, skilled at selfies, and not above the occasional lowbrow joke. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Some Judicial Opinions Require Only 140 Characters', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Some Judicial Opinions Require Only 140 Characters', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Willett, Don R', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Social Media', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Texas', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Supreme Courts (State)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Twitter', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Courts and the Judiciary', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-09-30T00:22:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jesse Wegman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jesse', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Wegman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/949affc7-d1cf-5a55-aac1-dd8601363b11', 'word_count': 750, 'uri': 'nyt://article/949affc7-d1cf-5a55-aac1-dd8601363b11'}, {'abstract': 'Prosecutors on Monday demanded a life sentence for the former Bosnian leader Radovan Karadzic as his trial draws to a close.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/09/30/world/europe/the-hague-bosnians-trial-nears-end.html', 'snippet': 'Prosecutors on Monday demanded a life sentence for the former Bosnian leader Radovan Karadzic as his trial draws to a close.', 'lead_paragraph': ' Prosecutors on Monday demanded a life sentence for the former Bosnian leader Radovan Karadzic as his trial draws to a close, accusing him of being the “driving force” to rid large parts of the ethnically mixed Bosnia of its non-Serb inhabitants during the 1990s. Mr. Karadzic, 69, denies responsibility for the killings. The Bosnian conflict, from 1992 to 1995, tore apart communities where different ethnic groups and Christians and Muslims had long lived together. It left more than 100,000 people dead and uncounted others maimed or traumatized by rape. Four years of court hearings dealt with the 11 charges, including genocide, brought against Mr. Karadzic. He is accused of being responsible for the siege of Sarajevo, the execution of more than 7,000 captive men and boys in Srebrenica, and the brutal “ethnic cleansing” of towns and villages in 1992. Mr. Karadzic has led his own defense and will speak on Thursday and Friday. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Hague: Bosnian’s Trial Nears End', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Hague: Bosnian’s Trial Nears End', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'War Crimes, Genocide and Crimes Against Humanity', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Simons, Marlise', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Karadzic, Radovan', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Bosnia and Herzegovina', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'International Criminal Court', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Serbia', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Hague (Netherlands)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-09-30T00:22:12+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Europe', 'byline': {'original': 'By Marlise Simons', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Marlise', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Simons', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/b07f261f-da11-5fff-8b59-42043f312767', 'word_count': 153, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b07f261f-da11-5fff-8b59-42043f312767'}, {'abstract': 'If the commission, which is expected to vote on the rule on Tuesday, strikes it down, cable and satellite providers could show games even if they are not sold out.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/09/30/business/media/fcc-appears-poised-to-loosen-nfl-blackout-rule-despite-league-protests.html', 'snippet': 'If the commission, which is expected to vote on the rule on Tuesday, strikes it down, cable and satellite providers could show games even if they are not sold out.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WASHINGTON — For decades, football fans in various markets wondered whether, come Sunday, they would be able to watch their lamentable but beloved team on television. If tickets to that week’s game were not sold out, the National Football League could keep the game off television in the local market as a way to protect gate receipts.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': ' F.C.C. Appears Poised to Loosen Sports Blackout Rule, Despite Protests by the N.F.L.', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'F.C.C. Appears Poised to Loosen Sports Blackout Rule, Despite Protests by the N.F.L.', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wyatt, Edward', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Federal Communications Commission', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Football League', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Football', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Regulation and Deregulation of Industry', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-09-30T00:24:44+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'By Edward Wyatt', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Edward', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Wyatt', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b1d5ddf4-214a-50d9-b272-578013bc1344', 'word_count': 764, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b1d5ddf4-214a-50d9-b272-578013bc1344'}, {'abstract': 'Authorities in Guerrero are searching for 57 students from a teachers college who have been missing since Friday when gunmen fired on a demonstration they were attending.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/09/30/world/americas/mexico-students-missing-after-protest-gunfire-in-guerrero.html', 'snippet': 'Authorities in Guerrero are searching for 57 students from a teachers college who have been missing since Friday when gunmen fired on a demonstration they were attending.', 'lead_paragraph': ' The authorities in Guerrero, one of Mexico’s most violent states, are searching for 57 students from a teachers college who have been missing since Friday when gunmen fired on a demonstration they were attending, killing three students and three others nearby. The gunfire also wounded 25 people. The Guerrero state prosecutor, Iñaky Blanco Cabrera, said that witnesses identified the gunmen as local police officers in Iguala, where the protest occurred, and that more than 20 had been detained. Mr. Blanco described the episode as an instance of “undoubtedly excessive force” by the officers. The federal interior secretary, Miguel Ángel Osorio Chong, called the conduct of the police “incredible” and said federal authorities would join the investigation. Witnesses said police officers had put the missing students on a bus and escorted them from the scene. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Mexico: Students Missing After Protest', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Mexico: Students Missing After Protest', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Police Brutality, Misconduct and Shootings', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Archibold, Randal C', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Missing Persons', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Mexico', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Demonstrations, Protests and Riots', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Guerrero (Mexico)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-09-30T00:25:44+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Americas', 'byline': {'original': 'By Randal C. Archibold', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Randal', 'middlename': 'C.', 'lastname': 'Archibold', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/46671cba-6c35-58d8-8cd9-8af5b7a2f9b9', 'word_count': 134, 'uri': 'nyt://article/46671cba-6c35-58d8-8cd9-8af5b7a2f9b9'}, {'abstract': 'Anne Barnard is in Turkey covering United States airstrikes in Syria, but finding that mostly they are notable only in their absence.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/times-insider/2014/09/29/reporting-from-the-edge-of-war/', 'snippet': 'Anne Barnard is in Turkey covering United States airstrikes in Syria, but finding that mostly they are notable only in their absence.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Anne Barnard is a correspondent based in Lebanon and covering the Middle East. Recently, she has been in Turkey, along with Karam Shoumali, a Times stringer, and Bryan Denton, a freelance photographer working for The Times, to cover the ripple effect of a war they can scarcely see. She reflects on life along the border between Turkey and Syria and the challenges of reporting there.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/29/blogs/border4/border4-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/09/29/blogs/border4/border4-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/29/blogs/border4/border4-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/29/blogs/border4/border4-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/29/blogs/border4/border4-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/09/29/blogs/border4/border4-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/29/blogs/border4/border4-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thum