, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Benjamin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hoffman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9feab14d-8bbe-5ec5-aba5-4a44f769716e', 'word_count': 539, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9feab14d-8bbe-5ec5-aba5-4a44f769716e'}, {'abstract': 'The team behind the popular Lower East Side restaurant are publishing a collection of 100 seasonal dishes meant to provide both information and inspiration. Here, they share a recipe with T.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/09/30/t-magazine/fat-radish-cookbook-recipe-brussels-sprouts.html', 'snippet': 'The team behind the popular Lower East Side restaurant are publishing a collection of 100 seasonal dishes meant to provide both information and inspiration. Here, they share a recipe with T.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When the Fat Radish opened four years ago on the lower Lower East Side, it was a harbinger of change for that rapidly transforming neighborhood and an indication of New York’s changing food preferences. The brainchild of Ben Towill and Phil Winser, childhood friends in England and the duo behind the catering concern Silkstone, it featured a menu with a British tilt, in keeping with the duo’s origins and the gastropub fad of the moment. But it worshipped at the altar of the vegetable, not the hearty cut of meat, and prized seasonal cooking above all else. Immediately, fashionistas and food cognescenti began trekking south of Delancey Street to indulge in the Fat Radish’s healthy comfort food, and in short order the duo opened two more spots: the East Pole, a slightly more upscale establishment on the Upper East Side, and the Leadbelly, an oyster bar across the street from the Fat Radish.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/29/t-magazine/29ashlock-fat-radish/29ashlock-fat-radish-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/09/29/t-magazine/29ashlock-fat-radish/29ashlock-fat-radish-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/29/t-magazine/29ashlock-fat-radish/29ashlock-fat-radish-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/29/t-magazine/29ashlock-fat-radish/29ashlock-fat-radish-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/29/t-magazine/29ashlock-fat-radish/29ashlock-fat-radish-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/09/29/t-magazine/29ashlock-fat-radish/29ashlock-fat-radish-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/29/t-magazine/29ashlock-fat-radish/29ashlock-fat-radish-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'From the Fat Radish, a Cure for the Common Cookbook', 'kicker': 'Food Matters', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2014-09-30T04:00:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'TLiving', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jesse Ashlock', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jesse', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ashlock', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4cfae179-ecbd-5de8-b293-4ff64da956b8', 'word_count': 588, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4cfae179-ecbd-5de8-b293-4ff64da956b8'}, {'abstract': 'T asked some of the field’s top talents to weigh in on the legend’s noteworthy works.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/09/30/t-magazine/dieter-rams-10-iconic-designs.html', 'snippet': 'T asked some of the field’s top talents to weigh in on the legend’s noteworthy works.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As explored in “By the Numbers: Dieter Rams,” in T’s fall design issue, the designer’s prolific four-decade career at Braun and his work for the British company Vitsoe have earned him a place as a living icon. His 10 Principles of Good Design have earned exalted status in the design world. With those in mind, T asked 10 of the most exciting designers working today to talk about 10 of the most innovative products Rams had a hand in, and the influence his work has — or hasn’t — had on their careers.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/29/t-magazine/29dieter-web-oloizia-slide-X5F4/29dieter-web-oloizia-slide-X5F4-articleLarge-v3.jpg', 'height': 534, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/09/29/t-magazine/29dieter-web-oloizia-slide-X5F4/29dieter-web-oloizia-slide-X5F4-articleLarge-v3.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 534}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/29/t-magazine/29dieter-web-oloizia-slide-X5F4/29dieter-web-oloizia-slide-X5F4-jumbo-v3.jpg', 'height': 668, 'width': 750, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'imag