': 'By Anna Holmes and Rivka Galchen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Anna', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Holmes', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Rivka', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Galchen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a9cec4eb-cd1b-538c-ba68-01a148cdf2a9', 'word_count': 1649, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a9cec4eb-cd1b-538c-ba68-01a148cdf2a9'}, {'abstract': 'The crowds on the streets maintain fastidious attention to hygiene and good manners as they rally for democracy.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/10/01/world/asia/in-hong-kong-clean-and-polite-but-a-protest-nonetheless.html', 'snippet': 'The crowds on the streets maintain fastidious attention to hygiene and good manners as they rally for democracy.', 'lead_paragraph': 'HONG KONG — They sleep by the thousands on what are normally the busiest boulevards of this crammed, nonstop city. They live on crackers, bananas and bottled water. They clean up their trash, even taking the time to pick out plastic and paper for recycling. Their shield of choice, and the symbol of their cause, is the umbrella: protection against the sun, rain — and pepper spray used by the riot police.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/01/world/01WATCHING-HongKong2/01WATCHING-HongKong2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/10/01/world/01WATCHING-HongKong2/01WATCHING-HongKong2-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/10/01/world/01WATCHING-HongKong2/01WATCHING-HongKong2