nge of healing herbs and products, which are now available online.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Moon Juice founder Amanda Chantal Bacon has developed a cult following in Los Angeles and beyond for raw juices and organic vegan foods that promise good health. And Bacon (oh, the irony) may be her own best advertisement. Standing in her Silver Lake shop, which opened last year at a spot between the nearly three-year-old original location in Venice and the month-old kiosk at the Ace Hotel Downtown, the mother of one is all shiny chestnut hair and glowing good looks. And that’s due at least in part to the products on the shelves across from the juice bar: flower remedies, house shea butter and rose body balms, ayurvedic toothpastes and a few jumbo-size crystals for good measure. The items are part of Moon Juice’s new Apothecary offshoot, a platform that functions as “a curation of my medicine cabinet, drawer of bathroom goodies and kitchen cabinet,” Bacon says. “It is the best way I can answer the thousands of questions we get about what toothpaste to use and why, or more effective options for skin care that are edible.”', 'print_section': 'ST', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/30/t-magazine/30meers-hartman2/30meers-hartman2-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 734, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/09/30/t-magazine/30meers-hartman2/30meers-hartman2-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 734}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/30/t-magazine/30meers-hartman2/30meers-hartman2-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 837, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/30/t-magaz