, 'pub_date': '2014-09-30T23:31:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Letters', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Letter', '_id': 'nyt://article/d130021b-ad6a-5372-9346-789babf2fd82', 'word_count': 199, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d130021b-ad6a-5372-9346-789babf2fd82'}, {'abstract': 'Jewish settlers moved early Tuesday into 26 apartments in seven buildings in a politically delicate, predominantly Palestinian neighborhood of East Jerusalem.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/10/01/world/middleeast/east-jerusalem-area-sees-influx-of-jewish-settlers.html', 'snippet': 'Jewish settlers moved early Tuesday into 26 apartments in seven buildings in a politically delicate, predominantly Palestinian neighborhood of East Jerusalem.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Jewish settlers moved early\xa0Tuesday\xa0into 26 apartments in seven buildings in a politically delicate, predominantly Palestinian neighborhood of East Jerusalem. Activists opposed to the move described it as the largest influx of settlers into the area in decades. The neighborhood, Silwan — just south of the Old City in territory Israel captured in the 1967 war then annexed — is home to about 50,000 Palestinians. It is also the site of archaeological ruins believed to be the City of David, a Jewish historical landmark. The Palestinians claim East Jerusalem as the capital of a future state, and most of the world considers it to be illegally occupied. A private investment company bought the properties over several years with the help of Elad, a settler organization dedicated to preventing any political division of Jerusalem. The Jewish population in the area is now expected to increase to 700 from 500, according to an Elad representative. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'East Jerusalem Area Sees Influx of Jewish Settlers', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Apartments Draw Jewish Settlers to East Jerusalem', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Israeli Settlements', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Israel', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Jerusalem (Israel)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Palestinians', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Historic Buildings and Sites', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Jews and Judaism', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'East Jerusalem', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-09-30T23:34:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Middle East', 'byline': {'original': 'By Isabel Kershner', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Isabel', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kershner', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/1c8c7380-2bd9-579a-a7ff-33c38afc3283', 'word_count': 152, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1c8c7380-2bd9-579a-a7ff-33c38afc3283'}, {'abstract': 'A growing number of wild horses in the Western United States pose a threat to ranchers and raise questions about how to control them.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/slideshow/2014/09/30/us/HORSES-SS.html', 'snippet': 'A growing number of wild horses in the Western United States pose a threat to ranchers and raise questions about how to control them.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A growing number of wild horses in the Western United States pose a threat to ranchers and raise questions about how to control them.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/30/us/HORSES-SS-slide-V4H5/HORSES-SS-slide-V4H5-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 